var e=[{id:`mj-m9pgd8GhmGk`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:0,title:`Data isolation for Modular Monoliths - DB Schemas, EF Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 16:03`,minutes:16,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`Invalid object name 'Workouts' — the night our module walls leaked`,paragraphs:["Friday, 6 PM. We shipped the 'boring' refactor: one big fitness-app database split into a `users` schema and a `training` schema. EF Core queries were green, the smoke tests passed, and everyone went home feeling like architects.",`Monday, 3:07 AM. The weekly engagement report — a Dapper job nobody had touched in eight months — dies with 'Invalid object name Workouts'. The job predated the schemas, and raw SQL doesn't read your DbContext. The on-call engineer greps the repo and finds six more bare table names sitting in raw queries like unexploded ordnance.`,"While patching, she finds two identical `outbox_messages` tables — one per schema, duplicated on purpose — and a single `__EFMigrationsHistory` in the default schema that both modules write into. The realization lands hard: a schema declaration is not isolation. Isolation is every access path — EF, raw SQL, outbox, and the migration ledger."],problem:`How do you make module data boundaries real — schemas, migrations, outbox tables, raw SQL — so isolation survives contact with the code that predates it?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`SQL Server`,`Dapper`,`Schema-per-Module`,`Outbox Pattern`,`EF Migrations`],note:`One relational database, one DbContext and schema per module, a duplicated outbox table per schema, and schema-aware raw SQL — the pieces that draw hard walls around module data.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why one big schema fails`,body:[`Let me think out loud about the ladder of isolation. Rung zero: no isolation at all. One database, one schema, every table shoulder to shoulder. It's the fastest thing to build — and the day someone in the Training module joins straight onto the Users tables, you don't have a modular monolith anymore. You have a distributed monolith inside a single database file, with all of the coupling and none of the network excuses.`,`So why not jump straight to database-per-module? Strongest walls, sure — but you lose same-database transactions and joins, you're now operating N databases, and for most teams that's buying isolation they don't yet need. Schema-per-module is the sweet spot: a hard, enforceable boundary, one engine, one connection string, one backup story. You can always promote a schema to a database later; you can't easily un-split a database.`],quote:`A schema is the cheapest contract your database will ever sign.`,table:{headers:[`Isolation level`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single shared schema`,`Simplest queries, one migration history`,`Any module can touch any table; refactors ripple everywhere`,`Prototypes, solo devs`],[`Schema per module`,`Hard boundary, per-module migrations, clear ownership`,`Raw SQL needs prefixes; history-table quirks`,`Default for modular monoliths`],[`Database per module`,`Strongest isolation, independent scaling`,`No cross-DB joins or transactions, N databases to operate`,`Modules that must scale or be extracted`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Data isolation for Modular Monoliths - DB Schemas, EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m9pgd8GhmGk`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: schemas in EF Core`,body:["The implementation starts with one line per context: set the default schema — `users` on the UsersDbContext, `training` on the TrainingDbContext. From that moment, every table, index, and foreign key that context emits inherits the schema. That single line is the wall. EF Core then does the boring-but-critical work for you: every generated query is schema-qualified automatically, because the model knows where it lives.","Migrations get interesting the moment you have multiple DbContexts. You can't just run `dotnet ef migrations add` and hope — you have to scope every command: `--context UsersDbContext` and `--output-dir Database/Migrations`, then repeat for the TrainingDbContext from its own infrastructure project. Open the generated migration and you can see the boundary in plain SQL: `users.users`, `users.followers`, `training.workouts`, `training.exercises`, each with its indexes, each stamped with its schema."],quote:`If the boundary isn't visible in the migration file, it isn't a boundary — it's a comment.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Data isolation for Modular Monoliths - DB Schemas, EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m9pgd8GhmGk`,at:`4:15`}},{heading:`03 · The outbox table you must duplicate`,body:["Here's the part that made me pause the first time I saw it: two `outbox_messages` tables, identical shape, one in each schema. Duplicated on purpose. My instinct said 'DRY violation' — but a shared outbox table is a shared schema, and a shared schema is exactly the coupling we just paid to remove. If Training writes events into a table that Users polls, Training's schema is now inside Users' trust boundary.",`Per-module outbox means per-module processor, per-module throughput, per-module backlog. One module's event storm can't starve every other module's delivery. The cost is honest: N processors to run and monitor instead of one. And the 'why not one table with a module column?' question answers itself — one runaway publisher then owns the table everyone reads, and you've rebuilt the contention you were escaping.`],quote:`Isolation you don't apply to your integration events is isolation in name only.`,table:{headers:[`Outbox layout`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`One shared table`,`Single processor, simplest ops`,`Cross-module coupling, contention, one backlog stalls all modules`,`Tiny apps, one team`],[`Outbox per module schema`,`True isolation, per-module throughput, clean ownership`,`N processors to run and monitor`,`Modular monoliths, multiple teams`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Data isolation for Modular Monoliths - DB Schemas, EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m9pgd8GhmGk`,at:`5:09`}},{heading:`04 · Dapper doesn't read your DbContext`,body:["EF Core qualifies schemas for free — the context knows its home. Dapper does not. Raw SQL is your responsibility, prefix by prefix: `users.followers` joined to `users.users`, `training.workouts` joined to `training.exercises`. The moment you introduce schemas, every hand-written query in the codebase is a potential 'Invalid object name' waiting for its 3 AM debut.",`And that failure mode is the nasty part: it compiles, it deploys, it passes the happy-path smoke test, and it dies at runtime on the first request that touches the renamed table. So the discipline is mechanical: schema-qualify 100% of raw SQL, and add a CI grep for bare table names inside Dapper strings. If you only ever go through EF Core or a repository over it, you're immune — which is exactly why the default should be EF Core, and Dapper should be a deliberate exception, not a habit.`],quote:`Raw SQL doesn't inherit your architecture — it inherits whatever you typed at 3 AM.`,table:{headers:[`Data access path`,`Schema handling`,`Failure mode`,`Fit`],rows:[[`EF Core / repositories`,`Automatic, from the DbContext model`,`None — schema flows from the model`,`Default choice`],[`Dapper raw SQL`,`Manual schema prefixes required`,`Runtime 'Invalid object name' (error 208)`,`Hot paths, complex reports`],[`Stored procedures`,`Bound at creation time`,`Drift from the EF model over time`,`Legacy or DBA-owned logic`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Data isolation for Modular Monoliths - DB Schemas, EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m9pgd8GhmGk`,at:`7:30`}},{heading:`05 · The migration history table that wouldn't move`,body:["Last wrinkle, and it's a sneaky one. Script the migrations for the TrainingDbContext and look closely: the `__EFMigrationsHistory` table is created in the default schema, not in `training` — the schema only shows up afterwards, on the module's own tables. Do that for every context and you've got N modules writing their migration ledgers into one shared table in the default schema. That's a shared schema by another name.","The fix is one line per context: point the history table at the module's schema with `MigrationsHistoryTable`. Now each module owns its schema, its tables, its outbox, and its migration ledger. And here's the payoff nobody mentions: the day you need to extract Training into a real service, it's a copy-paste of a self-contained schema — not an archaeology dig through someone else's tables."],quote:`If your migration ledger lives in someone else's schema, your module isn't isolated — it's renting.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Data isolation for Modular Monoliths - DB Schemas, EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m9pgd8GhmGk`,at:`11:54`}}],diagrams:[{id:`schema-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Schema-per-module topology inside one database`,caption:`Two modules, two schemas, duplicated outbox tables — and the one shared history table that gives the game away.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph APP["Modular Monolith - single process"]
        subgraph UM["Users Module"]
            UC["UsersDbContext<br/>default schema: users"]
            UDap["Dapper raw SQL<br/>users.users, users.followers"]
        end
        subgraph TM["Training Module"]
            TC["TrainingDbContext<br/>default schema: training"]
            TDap["Dapper raw SQL<br/>training.workouts, training.exercises"]
        end
    end
    subgraph DB["One SQL Server database"]
        subgraph S1["schema: users"]
            U1[("users")]
            U2[("followers")]
            UO[("outbox_messages")]
        end
        subgraph S2["schema: training"]
            T1[("workouts")]
            T2[("exercises")]
            TO[("outbox_messages")]
        end
        H[("__EFMigrationsHistory<br/>default schema - shared!")]
    end
    UC -->|"EF queries + migrations"| S1
    TC -->|"EF queries + migrations"| S2
    UDap -->|"schema-qualified SQL"| S1
    TDap -->|"schema-qualified SQL"| S2`},{id:`outbox-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Per-module outbox: Training publishes, Users consumes`,caption:`The event row commits in the same transaction as the workout, and each module drains only its own schema's outbox.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as Training endpoint
    participant H as CreateWorkout handler
    participant C as TrainingDbContext (training schema)
    participant O as training.outbox_messages
    participant P as Training outbox processor
    participant U as Users module

    API->>H: POST /workouts
    H->>C: INSERT training.workouts
    H->>O: INSERT event row (same transaction)
    H-->>API: 201 Created
    P->>O: poll for pending events
    O-->>P: WorkoutCreated
    P->>U: dispatch integration event
    Note over U,C: Users module never queries the training schema`}],takeaways:[`Pick the schema-per-module rung by table count: under ~10 tables a shared schema is fine; past ~20 tables or 2+ teams, schemas pay for themselves in one avoided refactor.`,"One DbContext, one schema, one migration folder per module — always pass `--context` and `--output-dir`; with N contexts, an unscoped migration command is a 100% chance of a tangled history.","Duplicate `outbox_messages` into every module schema — one shared outbox is a single contention point where one module's backlog can stall all N modules.","Schema-qualify 100% of raw SQL (`training.workouts`, `users.followers`) and grep CI for bare table names — a missing prefix compiles clean and fails at runtime with error 208.","Give each context its own ledger via `MigrationsHistoryTable('__EFMigrationsHistory', '<schema>')` — otherwise N modules write one shared history table in the default schema and nobody owns it."],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Data isolation for Modular Monoliths - DB Schemas, EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m9pgd8GhmGk`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-rdY5ElleWKY`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:1,title:`How To Use The Specification Design Pattern With EF Core 6`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 19:14`,minutes:19,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`Fifteen GetByIds and One 3 AM Pager`,paragraphs:[`Launch night for the biggest event the platform had ever hosted. At 14:30 right after a canary deploy the invite page starts timing out, and the on-call engineer finds the culprit fast: GetByIdWithAttendeesAndInvitations — a single EF Core query with Includes on two collections. One gathering, two thousand attendees, three thousand invitations. The JOIN fans out multiplicatively, the database CPU pegs at 100%, the connection pool drains, and every other endpoint goes down with it.`,`The postmortem wasn't really about that one query. It was about the repository around it: GetByName, GetById, GetByIdWithCreator (one Include), GetByIdWithAttendeesAndInvitations (three Includes — Creator, Attendees, Invitations). Each method was a hand-typed copy of the last, each born because some feature needed 'the gathering plus one more thing.' The cartesian explosion was just the newest resident of a query graveyard.`,`The fix that stuck: one specification object per query shape — criteria, includes, ordering, and a split-query flag — plus one thirty-line ApplySpecification evaluator that turns a spec into an IQueryable. The repository collapsed to GetOne and GetList, the 3 AM query became a named, unit-tested class, and the pager stayed quiet.`],problem:`How do you express 'which gathering, with which related data, ordered how' without fifteen near-identical repository methods — and without EF Core silently multiplying rows when a query Includes two collections at once?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 6`,`Specification Pattern`,`Repository Pattern`,`LINQ Expressions`,`IQueryable`,`AsSplitQuery`],note:`EF Core 6 supplies the query pipeline; LINQ expression trees carry criteria and includes; a small specification evaluator composes them into one IQueryable per named query shape, with AsSplitQuery as the escape hatch from JOIN fan-out.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why repositories become query graveyards`,body:[`Here's the GatheringRepository as we found it. GetByName queries the Gatherings table by name. Then there's a plain GetById. Then GetByIdWithCreator with one Include. Then GetByIdWithAttendeesAndInvitations with three Includes — Creator, Attendees, Invitations. Sound familiar? Every feature team that needed 'the gathering plus one more thing' added another method, because the previous one loaded either too little or way too much.`,`The obvious fix — 'just expose IQueryable from the repository' — is how you lose the repository entirely. Every handler starts composing its own query, EF-specific calls scatter across the codebase, and there's no named, testable artifact that says 'this is what reading a gathering with its invitations means.' We want the opposite: the query shape declared once, in one place, with a name.`],quote:`A repository with fifteen GetByIds isn't an abstraction — it's a query graveyard with a class name.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Fat repository methods`,`Each method is simple to read`,`Combinatorial explosion of GetById variants`,`2–3 query shapes max`],[`Leaking IQueryable`,`Zero wrapper code`,`Query logic scatters; EF leaks everywhere`,`Prototypes only`],[`Specification pattern`,`Named, reusable, testable query shapes`,`Small evaluator to maintain`,`Domain-heavy apps, many query shapes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use The Specification Design Pattern With EF Core 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rdY5ElleWKY`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · The spec is a query in a box`,body:[`So what actually goes inside a specification? Four things — and the predicate is only one of them. Criteria is an Expression<Func<Gathering, bool>>: that's your WHERE. IncludeExpressions is a list of expression trees — Creator, Attendees, Invitations — that's WHAT gets loaded. OrderBy and OrderByDescending define the ordering shape. And a flag, IsSplitQuery, decides how EF Core executes the whole thing.`,`This is why 'why not just pass a predicate?' misses the point. A bare expression can't carry includes or ordering — the query shape is bigger than the WHERE clause. When the spec is the whole box, the call site reads like intent: new GatheringByIdWithCreator(id). You can unit-test the criteria against an in-memory list, reuse the spec across three handlers, and nobody ever re-types an Include chain.`],quote:`A predicate says what to find; a specification says what to find, what to bring along, and in what order.`,table:{headers:[`Spec ingredient`,`Becomes in EF Core`,`Answers`],rows:[[`Criteria`,`Where(g => ...)`,`Which rows?`],[`IncludeExpressions`,`Include / ThenInclude chains`,`What related data loads?`],[`OrderBy / Descending`,`OrderBy / OrderByDescending`,`In what order?`],[`IsSplitQuery`,`AsSplitQuery()`,`One JOIN or N queries?`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use The Specification Design Pattern With EF Core 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rdY5ElleWKY`,at:`11:45`}},{heading:`03 · Walking the evaluator, line by line`,body:[`ApplySpecification is the whole magic trick, and it's about thirty lines. First: is Criteria null? If not, apply the Where — in our case that's the g.Id == id expression. Next: aggregate the IncludeExpressions in order, so Creator, Attendees, and Invitations chain onto the query exactly the way the three hand-written Includes did.`,`Then ordering: if OrderBy isn't null apply it, if OrderByDescending isn't null apply that — both null here, so we step over them. Finally, check IsSplitQuery — it's true for this spec, so we call AsSplitQuery. Out comes an IQueryable<Gathering> that is the exact same query shape the old method built by hand. The repository hands it to FirstOrDefaultAsync, and that call is what actually hits the database. The evaluator never changes again — every new query shape is just a new spec object.`],quote:`Write the evaluator once, then never think about plumbing again — every future query is just data.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use The Specification Design Pattern With EF Core 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rdY5ElleWKY`,at:`17:36`}},{heading:`04 · Split queries and the cartesian explosion`,body:[`Why does IsSplitQuery deserve a flag at all? Because of the 3 AM query. One gathering, two thousand attendees, three thousand invitations, and a single query with Includes on both collections. The database has to JOIN everything: rows fan out multiplicatively, the same gathering and creator get duplicated across thousands of rows, and EF has to deduplicate them during materialization. That's the cartesian explosion — a 'get one row' request that ships megabytes.`,`AsSplitQuery changes the deal: one SELECT for the gathering, one for attendees, one for invitations — N round trips instead of 1, but no row multiplication and no duplicated payload. On a fast internal network with small collections, the single query wins. The moment you have two or more collection Includes, or big collections, split wins. That's exactly why it's a per-spec flag and not a global setting: the right answer depends on the query shape.`],quote:`Two collection Includes don't add rows to your query — they multiply them.`,table:{headers:[`Execution`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single query (JOIN)`,`1 round trip, one result set`,`Row fan-out with 2+ collection Includes`,`Small or scalar-only Includes`],[`AsSplitQuery`,`No row multiplication, lean payloads`,`N round trips instead of 1`,`2+ collection Includes, large collections`]]}},{heading:`05 · Where specs earn their keep — and where they don't`,body:[`Where this pattern pays rent: query shapes that are named and reused across handlers, criteria you can unit-test in memory without spinning up a database, and a domain layer that expresses 'give me the gathering with its invitations' without referencing EF. The repository shrinks to GetOne(spec) and GetList(spec) — two methods that never grow again.`,`Where it doesn't: one-off ad-hoc queries (just use the DbContext), and fully dynamic user-built filters — a spec class per possible filter combination recreates the graveyard you just escaped. And resist the urge to keep building: the moment your specification library grows a fluent API for projections, caching, and execution strategies, you're writing a second ORM on top of the first. Stop at criteria, includes, ordering, paging, and the split-query flag.`],quote:`Build the smallest specification engine you'll actually use — anything more and you're writing a second ORM.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`Reach for a spec?`,`Why`],rows:[[`Named query reused by 2+ handlers`,`Yes`,`One definition, tested once`],[`One-off admin query`,`No`,`DbContext directly is cheaper`],[`User-built dynamic filters`,`Careful`,`Compose predicates, not spec classes`],[`Spec needs projections + caching`,`No`,`You're rebuilding an ORM`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`spec-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`From specification object to SQL: the ApplySpecification pipeline`,caption:`One evaluator composes criteria, includes, ordering, and the split-query flag into a single IQueryable — the repository never hand-writes a query again.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    QH["Query Handler"] -->|"GetOne(new GatheringByIdWithCreator(id))"| REPO["GatheringRepository"]
    REPO -->|"ApplySpecification(spec)"| EVAL["ApplySpecification Evaluator"]
    subgraph SPEC["GatheringByIdWithCreator : ASpecification of Gathering"]
        C["Criteria: g.Id == id"]
        I["Includes: Creator"]
        O["OrderBy: null"]
        SQ["IsSplitQuery = true"]
    end
    SPEC --> EVAL
    EVAL -->|"Where + Include + AsSplitQuery"| IQ["IQueryable of Gathering"]
    IQ -->|"FirstOrDefaultAsync()"| DB[("EF Core 6 / Database")]
    DB -->|"Gathering with Creator loaded"| QH`},{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path: a query handler reading a gathering with its creator`,caption:`The handler declares intent with a named spec; the evaluator and EF Core translate it into the exact query shape the old hand-written method used to build.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant H as Query Handler
    participant R as GatheringRepository
    participant E as ApplySpecification
    participant S as GatheringByIdWithCreator Spec
    participant DB as Database
    H->>R: GetOne(spec)
    R->>E: ApplySpecification(spec)
    E->>S: read Criteria, Includes, OrderBy, IsSplitQuery
    S-->>E: g.Id == id, Include(Creator), split = true
    E-->>R: IQueryable with Where + Include + AsSplitQuery
    R->>DB: FirstOrDefaultAsync()
    DB-->>R: Gathering row + joined Creator
    R-->>H: Gathering (Creator loaded)`}],takeaways:[`Cap every repository at two methods — GetOne(spec) and GetList(spec). If you've written your fifth hand-rolled GetById variant, each new feature is a copy-paste bug waiting for 3 AM.`,`Collection Includes multiply, they don't add: 1 gathering + 2,000 attendees + 3,000 invitations fans a single JOIN out to millions of rows — flip AsSplitQuery on any spec with 2+ collection Includes.`,`A spec that only wraps a Where predicate is a wasted abstraction — bundle criteria + includes + ordering + the split-query flag, or just call the DbContext directly.`,`Keep the ApplySpecification evaluator under ~50 lines in one file — it's the only code that should know how a spec becomes an IQueryable, so an ORM swap touches one file, not 40 call sites.`,`Benchmark both execution modes before shipping: split queries trade 1 round trip for N — at 20 ms per round trip, a 4-query split read adds ~60 ms of latency, which is still cheaper than shipping a multi-megabyte JOIN payload.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use The Specification Design Pattern With EF Core 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rdY5ElleWKY`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-q_CtqBbQyP4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:2,title:`This Interview Question Changed How I Think About System Design`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:05`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Interview Question That Broke My Default Answer`,paragraphs:[`Six or seven years ago, third-round interview for a mid-level .NET role. Glass conference room, one whiteboard, one senior engineer with a marker he clearly had no intention of handing over. The question: 'A user clicks a button in the UI to generate a report — Excel or PDF. It takes about five minutes. Design it.' I drew the obvious thing in ninety seconds: Blazor posts to a reports endpoint on an ASP.NET Core API, the API pulls data through EF Core, a report generator renders the document, and the endpoint returns the file. Spinner for five minutes, file at the end. I was quietly very pleased with myself.`,`Then the follow-ups started, and every one of them hit the same wall. What happens when the corporate proxy kills the connection at sixty seconds? What happens when two hundred users click at once and every request is pinning a connection? What happens if the user refreshes — do we generate the whole report twice? I patched each hole with a shrug, and he just waited. That silence was the hint. The actual duration, he added, is completely arbitrary — it could be hours, or days. My entire design was balanced on one connection staying open, and nothing about it survived contact with that sentence.`,`With a couple more nudges, the shape finally flipped in my head: stop trying to return the report — return a promise. Accept the job, hand back a reference, do the work somewhere that doesn't care whether the browser is still listening, and let the client ask about it later. It took me the length of one interview to unlearn the default answer I'd been shipping for years, and I've never drawn a synchronous long-running request on a whiteboard since.`],problem:`The user has to wait for the process to finish — and that single sentence is the trap. The moment the work outlives the HTTP connection, request/response stops being the right contract, and the design has to become a workflow.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`Blazor`,`EF Core`,`Job Table`,`Background Worker`,`Azure Blob Storage`,`SignalR`,`202 Accepted`],note:`The moving parts of an asynchronous report pipeline: the API accepts and returns in milliseconds, a durable job store carries state across restarts, a background worker does the heavy lifting, blob storage holds the artifact, and polling or SignalR closes the loop.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the design that dies at 60 seconds`,body:[`So, my first instinct on that whiteboard — and honestly the instinct most of us have — was the obvious one. User clicks the button, the Blazor app fires a POST to a reports endpoint on the ASP.NET Core API, the API queries the database through EF Core, hands the rows to a report generator, and streams the finished Excel or PDF back in the response. One deployable, zero new infrastructure, easy to test. And here's the thing — for a report that takes two seconds, that is the correct design. I want to be fair to it.`,`But this one takes five minutes, and five minutes is a different universe. The browser or a proxy gives up long before you're done. Your load balancer's idle timeout — usually 60 to 100 seconds — drops the connection while you're still rendering row 40,000 of 200,000. Every in-flight request pins a connection and holds work that can't be safely retried, because a retry means generating the whole thing again. And the user? A spinner with no progress, no cancel, and no idea whether it's even alive.`,`And the actual duration is arbitrary — the interviewer made that point explicitly. It could be hours. It could be days. The moment I heard that, the whole 'hold the connection open' idea was dead on arrival. The question quietly stopped being about reports and started being about what HTTP actually is: a blocking, request/response protocol. You don't fight that. You design around it.`],quote:`A five-minute HTTP request isn't a request anymore — it's a liability with a timeout attached.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Synchronous POST`,`One service, zero new infra, trivial to test`,`Timeouts at 60-100s, pinned connections, unsafe retries, no progress`,`Only for sub-10s work`],[`202 + job polling`,`Survives disconnects, retry-safe, stateless clients`,`Extra GET traffic, seconds of notify lag`,`Default for minutes-long work`],[`202 + SignalR push`,`Instant UX, live progress streaming`,`Connection state, scale-out backplane, reconnect logic`,`High-value, frequently watched jobs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — This Interview Question Changed How I Think About System Design`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q_CtqBbQyP4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: shrink the synchronous part`,body:[`So here's the flip. If HTTP is blocking, shrink the blocking part until it's honest. The POST to /reports no longer generates anything — it validates the request, writes a job row to the database with status Queued, and immediately returns 202 Accepted with a Location header pointing at /reports/{id}. That's it. The synchronous part of your system is now measured in milliseconds, which is exactly what HTTP is good at.`,`Now, why persist the job in the database instead of dropping it into an in-memory queue? Because deployments happen. App pools recycle. Workers crash mid-render. If the job only lives in RAM, every one of those events silently eats someone's report. A job table via EF Core gives you durability, history, and — for free — the status endpoint, because GET /reports/{id} just reads that row. The database is the queue until the day you outgrow it, and most systems never do.`,`One more thing I'd add today that I didn't back then: idempotency. If the client retries the POST — and clients do retry — you don't want two five-minute jobs for the same report. A dedupe or correlation key on the job row turns that retry into 'here's the job you already started.' Cheap insurance, huge payoff.`],quote:`The moment the work outlives the connection, you owe the client a job — not a response.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — This Interview Question Changed How I Think About System Design`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q_CtqBbQyP4`,at:`1:12`}},{heading:`03 · The worker side: dequeue, generate, upload`,body:[`On the other side of that job row sits a background worker — a hosted service in .NET, or its own process if you want independent scaling. Its loop is boring in the best way: find the next Queued job, claim it by flipping status to Running with a claimed-by stamp and a heartbeat, fetch the parameters and the data from the database, and kick off the exact same report-generation flow we had in the naive design. Nothing about generating the report changed — only where it lives.`,`When the document is ready, upload it to object storage — S3 or Azure Blob Storage — and write the artifact URI back onto the job, marking it Completed. Why object storage and not the database or the server's file system? The file system dies with the pod and breaks the moment you run two instances. Database BLOBs bloat your backups and force every download through your API. Object storage is cheap, survives every deploy, and lets you hand the client a pre-signed URL so the download never touches your servers at all.`,`And be honest about failure: the worker will die mid-job someday. The heartbeat and the claim stamp are what let another worker pick up the orphaned job — which is exactly why the generation step has to be idempotent. At-least-once isn't pessimism; it's the default physics of distributed systems.`],quote:`The queue is the contract; the worker is just a tenant in it.`,table:{headers:[`Artifact store`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Object storage (S3 / Blob)`,`Cheap, pre-signed URLs, survives deploys, scales`,`Extra service, lifecycle and cleanup to manage`,`Default for generated reports`],[`Database BLOB`,`One system, transactional with the job row`,`Bloats DB and backups, downloads flow through the API`,`Small files, low volume`],[`Server file system`,`Zero setup, fast local I/O`,`Lost on redeploy, breaks with more than one instance`,`Single-box prototypes only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — This Interview Question Changed How I Think About System Design`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q_CtqBbQyP4`,at:`4:29`}},{heading:`04 · Closing the loop: polling versus push`,body:[`Last mile: how does the user find out? Two honest options. Polling — the client hits GET /reports/{id} every couple of seconds until the status flips to Completed. It's stateless, it works through every proxy and CDN, and anyone can implement it. The cost is traffic and a few seconds of notification lag. Push — SignalR over WebSockets — where the worker or a completion handler notifies the browser the instant the artifact lands. Instant, gorgeous, and now you own connection state, scale-out backplanes, and reconnect logic.`,`My rule of thumb: poll by default, earn the push. A five-minute report polled every two seconds is about 150 requests per user per job — nothing. The same job polled every 200 milliseconds because someone wanted a 'live' feel is roughly 1,500 requests, 99% of which return 'still running' and change nothing. And whichever you pick, expose progress — rows processed, percentage done. A five-minute spinner feels broken; a progress bar at 60% feels like a feature.`,`For genuinely long jobs — hours, days — add a third channel: email or a webhook when it's done, because nobody keeps a tab open for six hours. The status URI keeps working either way; you're just layering convenience on top of the same source of truth.`],quote:`Polling is a tax you pay for simplicity; push is an investment you pay in complexity.`,table:{headers:[`Notification path`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Polling every 2-5s`,`Stateless, proxy-proof, trivial to build`,`Traffic overhead, seconds of lag`,`Default choice`],[`SignalR push`,`Instant delivery, streams progress`,`Owning connection state and scale-out`,`Rich UX, many live viewers`],[`Email / webhook`,`No open tab needed, works for hours-long jobs`,`Out-of-band delivery and templating`,`Very long-running work`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — This Interview Question Changed How I Think About System Design`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q_CtqBbQyP4`,at:`6:18`}},{heading:`05 · What I'd say in that interview today`,body:[`So what would I say in that interview today? I'd draw the ladder out loud. Level zero: synchronous request — only if the work is genuinely fast. Level one: 202 Accepted, durable job row, status endpoint, background worker, artifact in object storage, polling. That's the floor for anything measured in minutes, and it's maybe a day of work. Level two is where the senior-sounding words live: idempotency keys, worker heartbeats and orphan recovery, cancellation, retention policies so blob storage doesn't become a landfill, and the outbox pattern so 'job created' and 'request accepted' succeed or fail together.`,`But the real lesson of that question wasn't the queue. It was that the interviewer kept asking 'okay, now what breaks?' — and every answer pointed at the same assumption I'd never examined: that the connection would just... stay open. System design, it turns out, is mostly noticing which of your assumptions has a timeout on it. I've reused that one flip — accept fast, work in the background, let the client ask later — for exports, imports, video transcoding, bulk emails, you name it. One question, six years of interest.`],quote:`Interview questions are never about the answer — they're about which trade-off you reach for first.`}],diagrams:[{id:`before-blocking`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Before: one request, one very long connection`,caption:`The naive design I drew on the whiteboard — every failure mode hangs off that single held-open HTTP connection.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    U[User] --> B[Blazor UI]
    B -- "POST /reports" --> A[ASP.NET Core API]
    A -- "EF Core queries" --> DB[(SQL Database)]
    A -- "render" --> R[Report Generator]
    R -- "Excel / PDF bytes" --> A
    A -- "response after 5 min" --> B
    LB[Load balancer idle timeout] -. "drops connection at 60-100s" .-> A`},{id:`after-async`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`After: accept fast, work in the background`,caption:`The async job workflow — the API's synchronous duty shrinks to milliseconds, and the status URI becomes the client's source of truth.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant U as User (Browser)
    participant A as ASP.NET Core API
    participant J as Job Store (EF Core)
    participant W as Background Worker
    participant DB as SQL Database
    participant S as Blob Storage

    U->>A: POST /reports
    A->>J: Insert job (status = Queued)
    A-->>U: 202 Accepted, Location: /reports/{id}
    W->>J: Claim next Queued job
    W->>DB: Fetch report data
    Note over W: Generate Excel / PDF (about 5 min)
    W->>S: Upload artifact
    W->>J: Mark Completed + artifact URI
    loop every 2-5s (or SignalR push instead)
        U->>A: GET /reports/{id}
        A-->>U: status = Running, progress 40%
    end
    U->>A: GET /reports/{id}
    A-->>U: status = Completed, download URI
    U->>S: Download via pre-signed URL`}],takeaways:[`Anything that can take longer than about 30 seconds should never run inside an HTTP request — return 202 Accepted with a Location header to a status endpoint instead.`,`Persist the job to durable storage before you respond — an in-memory queue loses every in-flight report on the first app pool recycle or deployment.`,`Poll job status every 2-5 seconds with jitter, or push with SignalR — polling a 5-minute job every 200 ms fires roughly 1,500 requests that do nothing 99% of the time.`,`Write generated artifacts to object storage and hand back a pre-signed URL — never stream a multi-megabyte file through your API twice.`,`Design for at-least-once execution: give every job an idempotency key, because any background worker will eventually process the same job twice.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — This Interview Question Changed How I Think About System Design`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q_CtqBbQyP4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`interview-framework`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-ka_WCmNdybE`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:3,title:`Design a File Upload Service Like a Senior Engineer`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 8:02`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`Azure`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The Night 300 Product Videos Took Our API Down With Them`,paragraphs:[`Launch week. A partner integrator starts bulk-syncing 300 product videos — 200 MB to 2 GB apiece — through our shiny new file upload endpoint. The design was the one every tutorial shows: POST /files/upload, ASP.NET Core binds the body, we write the bytes into a varbinary(max) column right next to the metadata, return 201 Created. It worked beautifully in the demo. With a 40 KB PDF.`,`By 1:40 AM the first pod got OOMKilled — IFormFile was buffering whole videos in memory, and ten concurrent uploads meant gigabytes of heap per instance. We scaled from 2 pods to 8, which just meant 8 pods dying in sequence. Worse, the database disk hit 95%: those LOB pages had quietly turned a 20 GB database into a 600 GB one, the nightly backup started failing, and support chat was a wall of 'upload failed' screenshots.`,`The 3 AM fix wasn't more pods or a bigger disk. It was admitting the API should never have been in the byte-moving business: presigned URLs straight to blob storage, metadata in Postgres, and a queue feeding background workers for the virus-scan-and-thumbnail pipeline. Uploads went from timing out at 90 seconds to finishing client-side in under a minute — and the API stopped caring how big a file was.`],problem:`Large files flowing through the API into a relational database turn every upload into a memory bomb, every table into a page-size explosion, and every scale-out attempt into an expensive exercise in shoveling bytes.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`Azure Blob Storage`,`Postgres`,`Presigned URLs`,`Message Queue`,`Background Workers`],note:`The API brokers trust — auth, metadata, and short-lived presigned URLs — while blob storage holds the bytes and a queue fans each upload out to background workers for async processing.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: ship the dumb version on purpose`,body:[`Let's start where every real system starts: a client, an API, and a database. The client hits POST /files/upload, the API validates, writes the bytes and metadata into a relational table, hands back an ID. Download is the mirror image — SELECT the blob, stream it out. Done. And honestly? That's the right place to start, because it proves out your auth, your schema, and your client contract with zero extra infrastructure.`,`But senior engineering means scoring that design against the requirements, not just getting it to compile. Upload and download: pass. Metadata: pass. Security: pass — it's just an API. Large files: big question mark, because every byte is transiting your API process. Async processing: technically possible with a BackgroundService, but it's living on the same instance that's already choking on uploads. Write down which requirement fails and when — that's the design work.`],quote:`Ship the dumb version first — just write down which requirement it's going to fail, and when.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Design a File Upload Service Like a Senior Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ka_WCmNdybE`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where large files hurt`,body:[`Here's the thing about pushing a 2 GB file through your API: something has to hold it. Model binding with IFormFile buffers it in memory; even streaming to disk, you've got Kestrel request size limits, 90-second load balancer timeouts, and a worker thread pinned for the whole ride. Ten concurrent uploads and your pods are trading RAM for bytes — the one resource your API actually needs to serve everything else.`,`And the obvious fix — just add more API instances — doesn't fix it, because you're scaling compute to do a courier's job. Every instance pays the full memory and network tax to touch bytes it doesn't care about. The requirement doesn't fail because your servers are small; it fails because the bytes are in the wrong place.`],quote:`If your API's main job is moving bytes, you've built a very expensive courier service.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Stream through API`,`Simple, one trust boundary, easy validation`,`Buffers RAM, API scales with bytes, timeout-prone`,`Files under ~5 MB`],[`Direct-to-storage (presigned)`,`API never touches bytes, near-infinite scale`,`More moving parts, client must support PUT`,`Files over ~5 MB`],[`Chunked direct upload`,`Resumable, survives flaky networks`,`Complex client-side assembly logic`,`Huge or mobile uploads`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Design a File Upload Service Like a Senior Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ka_WCmNdybE`,at:`0:28`}},{heading:`03 · Why the database hates your blobs`,body:[`Next crime scene: the database. Most relational databases will happily store binary data — varbinary(max), bytea, whatever — but they store it in pages, and pages have a hard upper limit, often just 8 KB. A 2 GB file doesn't fit in a page, so the engine shatters it across thousands of overflow pages — TOAST in Postgres, LOB pages in SQL Server. That's the page size explosion, and it makes every table scan, every index rebuild, every backup slower and fatter.`,`Your 20 GB database becomes 600 GB, your restore time goes from minutes to hours, and your DBA finds out the hard way at 2 AM. Meanwhile, the blob never needed transactional guarantees with its metadata row — it needed durability and cheap throughput, which is literally the product that blob storage sells.`],quote:`A database can carry your blobs the way I can carry a fridge — technically possible, structurally a bad idea.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Blobs in RDBMS`,`One system, single transaction with metadata`,`Page explosion, huge backups, slow scans`,`Tiny icons only`],[`Filesystem share`,`Cheap, dead simple`,`No durability, no scale-out, ops pain`,`Single box, throwaway`],[`Blob storage`,`Durable, cheap, presigned URLs, event hooks`,`Eventual consistency, new failure mode`,`Default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Design a File Upload Service Like a Senior Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ka_WCmNdybE`,at:`1:03`}},{heading:`04 · Flipping the flow: presigned URLs and the lazy API`,body:[`So flip it. The API stops being a pipe and becomes a bouncer. The client asks to upload; the API authenticates, validates size and content type, inserts a metadata row with status Pending, and hands back a presigned PUT URL — a short-lived, single-blob, single-permission ticket signed by the storage account. The client streams bytes directly to blob storage; the API never sees them.`,`Then the magic: blob storage fires a BlobCreated event, which lands on a queue, which wakes up a worker to do the real work — virus scan, thumbnail, metadata extraction. Download is the same trick in reverse: authorize, mint a presigned GET, or hand out a CDN URL. Your API now handles ten uploads a second or ten thousand with the same footprint, because bytes never touch it.`],quote:`Stop uploading through your API — hand the client a signed ticket and get out of the way.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Design a File Upload Service Like a Senior Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ka_WCmNdybE`,at:`1:34`}},{heading:`05 · Async processing without strangling the API`,body:[`A BackgroundService inside the API feels free until you realize it shares fate with the thing it's serving — deploy to update the thumbnailer and you kill in-flight uploads; a CPU-heavy virus scan starves request threads on the same box. Once uploads land in blob storage, the queue is the natural seam: one message per blob, a separate worker pool that scales independently, retries with backoff, and a dead-letter queue for the genuinely cursed files.`,`One rule above all: workers must be idempotent, because queues promise at-least-once delivery and you will run that virus scan twice. And give the client a window into the state machine — Pending, Uploaded, Processing, Ready, Failed — via a status endpoint or webhook, because 'we'll email you when it's done' is not a UX.`],quote:`The upload isn't done when the bytes land — it's done when the status says Ready.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-process BackgroundService`,`Zero infra, easy debugging`,`Shares fate with API, restarts lose work`,`Prototypes`],[`Queue + worker pool`,`Independent scale, retries, backpressure`,`More infra, idempotency required`,`Production`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Design a File Upload Service Like a Senior Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ka_WCmNdybE`,at:`2:17`}}],diagrams:[{id:`upload-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Evolved File Upload Architecture`,caption:`The API brokers trust and metadata; bytes flow client-to-blob directly, and storage events drive async processing.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    C["Client"] -->|"1. POST /files/upload"| API["Upload API"]
    API -->|"2. insert metadata row"| DB[("Metadata DB")]
    API -->|"3. presigned PUT URL"| C
    C -->|"4. PUT bytes direct"| B[("Blob Storage")]
    B -->|"5. BlobCreated event"| Q["Message Queue"]
    Q --> W["Background Workers"]
    W -->|"6. scan + thumbnail"| B
    W -->|"7. status = Ready"| DB
    C -->|"8. poll status"| API
    API -->|"9. presigned GET URL"| C`},{id:`presigned-upload-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Presigned Upload Flow, Step by Step`,caption:`The full happy path: authorize, mint a ticket, stream direct to storage, then let the queue finish the job.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as Upload API
    participant D as Metadata DB
    participant B as Blob Storage
    participant Q as Queue
    participant W as Worker

    C->>A: POST /files (name, size, type)
    A->>D: INSERT metadata (status=Pending)
    A-->>C: 201 Created + presigned PUT URL
    C->>B: PUT file bytes (direct)
    B-->>C: 200 OK
    B->>Q: BlobCreated event
    Q->>W: deliver message
    W->>B: read blob, virus scan, thumbnail
    W->>D: UPDATE status=Ready
    C->>A: GET /files/:id
    A-->>C: status + presigned GET URL`}],takeaways:[`Route anything over ~5 MB around your API with presigned URLs — a single 1 GB upload buffered in memory can evict an entire pod's working set.`,`Keep blobs out of the RDBMS: one 2 GB file becomes ~250,000 8 KB pages, bloats backups, and slows every scan that touches the table.`,`Set presigned URL expiry to 10–15 minutes and scope each ticket to one blob and one verb — never hand out account-wide keys.`,`Chunk large uploads into 5–10 MB blocks so a dropped connection costs one block retry, not a full re-upload of a 10 GB file.`,`Drive post-upload work from one queue message per blob with idempotent workers, and surface a status field (Pending → Processing → Ready/Failed) clients can poll.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Design a File Upload Service Like a Senior Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ka_WCmNdybE`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-frontend-system-design`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Xo3rsiZYsJQ`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:4,title:`Modular Monoliths: How To Build One & Lessons Learned`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 43:28`,minutes:43,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Friday Migration That Found Our Hidden JOIN`,paragraphs:[`Two years into our platform, we were the smug kids on the block: one .NET deployable, five modules — Orders, Catalog, Billing, Identity, Notifications — one database, deploys measured in minutes. The pitch writes itself: microservice-style boundaries without the distributed-systems tax. Then a completely routine Friday migration renamed a column in the catalog schema.`,`By Monday morning, checkout was throwing SQL errors in production. The root cause wasn't in Catalog at all — an Orders query handler, written months earlier under deadline pressure, had quietly JOINed straight into catalog tables. No module boundary stopped it, because at the database level there were none. Our modular monolith had a secret second architecture: one giant shared schema wearing a modular costume.`,`We spent the next sprint doing surgery: schema-per-module, thin contracts at the edges, events for cross-module workflows, and a CI rule that fails the build on any cross-schema reference. The migration that broke us was innocent; the invisible coupling was the culprit. This is the talk I wish someone had given me before we wrote line one.`],problem:`How do you keep modules genuinely independent — in code and in data — when they all live in one deployable and share one database?`},tech:{stack:[`.NET`,`ASP.NET Core`,`EF Core`,`MediatR`,`Outbox Pattern`,`SQL Server`],note:`A single .NET deployable hosts every module; EF Core maps each module to its own database schema, while MediatR-style in-process messaging with an outbox handles cross-module communication without a network hop.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just start with microservices?`,body:[`Let me get the controversial one out of the way early, because I know someone in the chat is already typing it. Martin Fowler has this line I keep coming back to: you shouldn't start a new project with microservices, even if you're sure the application will be big enough to make it worthwhile. When I first read it, I bristled. Two years of living inside a modular monolith later, I get it.`,`Here's the failure mode I've watched happen over and over: you start greenfield, you're excited, and you make a microservice for this and a microservice for that — and before you know it you have dozens of them, each one guessing at a boundary you haven't earned yet. Every wrong guess is now a network call, a versioned contract, a deployment pipeline. You've paid the distributed-systems tax upfront for boundaries you could have discovered for free inside one process.`,`The monolith-first move isn't nostalgia — it's a discovery strategy. Inside one deployable, moving code between modules is a refactor that takes an afternoon. Between services, it's a quarter of meetings. Start together, learn the real seams, and split only when a module hands you a concrete reason.`],quote:`You can't cut along seams you haven't found — earn the boundaries first, then split.`,table:{headers:[`Architecture`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Best fit`],rows:[[`Monolith`,`Simple ops, easy refactors, one deploy`,`Everything couples over time, all-or-nothing scaling`,`Small teams, early product`],[`Microservices`,`Independent scaling and deploys, team autonomy`,`Distributed complexity from day one, expensive boundary mistakes`,`Mature boundaries, many teams`],[`Modular Monolith`,`Microservice-style boundaries, monolith ops`,`Requires discipline to keep modules honest`,`Most new projects, growing teams`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monoliths: How To Build One & Lessons Learned`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xo3rsiZYsJQ`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what actually makes a module?`,body:[`So if we're building a modular monolith, the first real challenge — and nobody warns you about this — is defining the modules themselves. A module is a cohesive set of functionality: things that naturally fit together. Everything around orders flows together — creating them, pricing them, fulfilling them. Everything around catalog flows together. That's a module. That's a bounded context.`,`The test I use in code reviews is embarrassingly simple: describe the module in one sentence. 'Orders takes a cart and turns it into a fulfilled purchase' — great, that's a module. The moment your sentence needs 'and also', you either have two modules or half of one. And the inverse test: if two of your 'modules' always change in the same commit, they were never separate.`,`The trap to avoid is organizing by technical layers — a services folder, a repositories folder, an entities folder. That's not modularity, that's a filing cabinet. A change to orders should touch one module, not scatter across six folders. Cohesion is vertical, by capability — not horizontal, by layer.`],quote:`If your module description needs the words 'and also', you don't have a module — you have two, or half of one.`,table:{headers:[`Boundary test`,`Healthy signal`,`Warning sign`],rows:[[`One-sentence pitch`,`Describes one capability end-to-end`,`Needs 'and also' to finish the sentence`],[`Change frequency`,`Modules change in separate commits`,`Two modules always change together`],[`Vocabulary`,`Owns its language: order, fulfillment, cart`,`Shares entities and DTOs everywhere`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monoliths: How To Build One & Lessons Learned`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xo3rsiZYsJQ`,at:`2:27`}},{heading:`03 · Communication: commands inside, events across`,body:[`Challenge number two, and this is where modular monoliths are quietly won or lost: how do modules talk to each other? You've got a spectrum. At one end, direct in-process method calls — the fastest thing imaginable, and also the tightest coupling you can buy. At the other end, a real message broker with durability and retries. In the middle, in-process events through a mediator.`,`My rule after two years of scars: commands inside a module, events across modules. When Orders needs something from Catalog, it calls Catalog's public contract — a thin, deliberate API, like a NuGet package you own. When Orders has done something others care about, it publishes OrderPlaced and forgets about it. Notifications and Billing subscribe. Orders doesn't know they exist, and that's the point.`,`Why not always use the bus? Because a broker buys you durability at the price of operational surface — infrastructure, monitoring, at-least-once semantics — and most modules don't need that yet. And why not always direct calls? Because a chain of synchronous calls across four modules is a distributed monolith wearing a trench coat inside your single deployable. The in-process event bus gives you the decoupled shape now, and the outbox pattern means the event survives even if the request dies.`],quote:`Commands inside a module, events across them — the moment you need a bus to be fast, you've already lost.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Direct method call`,`Fastest, compile-time safe`,`Tight coupling, sync chains form`,`Same-module commands`],[`In-process events`,`Decoupled, transaction-friendly`,`No durability across restarts`,`Cross-module reactions`],[`Message broker`,`Durable, async, retry semantics`,`Ops burden, eventual consistency`,`Extraction-ready, long workflows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monoliths: How To Build One & Lessons Learned`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xo3rsiZYsJQ`,at:`10:28`}},{heading:`04 · Data isolation: the schema line in the sand`,body:[`Challenge three is the one that bit us hardest, and it's the least glamorous: data. Look at any honest modular monolith diagram and notice something — the arrows all point to the database. Each module gets its own schema, and those arrows never cross. One database is fine; one owner per schema is non-negotiable.`,`The failure mode is sneaky because EF Core makes it so easy. You're in an Orders handler, you need a product name, and the catalog tables are right there. One JOIN later, Catalog can never rename a column again without breaking Orders — and nobody wrote that constraint down anywhere. That's exactly the Friday-night incident I opened with. The fix wasn't better developers; it was a rule the build enforces: zero cross-schema references, checked in CI.`,`Why not go all the way to a database per module? Honestly, you could — but you give up single-transaction consistency and simple operations for a benefit you only collect when you actually extract a service. Schema-per-module gets you 90% of the isolation with 10% of the pain, and it makes the eventual extraction almost mechanical: same schema, new host, swap the transport.`],quote:`One database is fine — one owner per schema is non-negotiable.`,table:{headers:[`Isolation level`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`When`],rows:[[`Shared tables`,`Zero friction, easy joins`,`Invisible coupling, no ownership`,`Never, in a modular monolith`],[`Schema per module`,`Real ownership, cheap, one tx scope`,`Needs CI enforcement`,`Default choice`],[`Database per module`,`Hard isolation, extraction-ready`,`No cross-module tx, more ops`,`When a module becomes a service`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monoliths: How To Build One & Lessons Learned`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xo3rsiZYsJQ`,at:`16:58`}},{heading:`05 · Lessons from two years: enforce it or lose it`,body:[`Let me close with the lessons we paid for, so you don't have to. Lesson one: modularity enforced by convention lasts about one deadline. The boundaries that survive are the ones the compiler and the pipeline defend — a project per module, architecture tests, a CI check that greps for cross-schema references and fails loudly. Our rogue JOIN existed because nothing could say no.`,`Lesson two: keep the public contracts thin and boring. DTOs in, DTOs out, no domain entities leaking across the boundary. Every leaked entity is a hidden coupling that will surface as a migration incident at the worst possible time. Lesson three: don't split into microservices until a module gives you a concrete reason — a separate team needs to own it, its traffic profile needs independent scaling, or its availability requirements diverge. Until then, the modular monolith gives you the architecture without the pager duty.`,`And the meta-lesson: the modular monolith isn't a compromise between monolith and microservices — it's the training ground that makes the microservice decision informed instead of aspirational. When we finally extracted Notifications, it was a transport change, not a rewrite. That's the whole game.`],quote:`Modularity is enforced by the build, not by good intentions.`,table:{headers:[`Lesson`,`What it cost us`,`Rule we run now`],rows:[[`Convention-based boundaries`,`A rogue JOIN broke checkout`,`CI fails on cross-schema references`],[`Leaked entities`,`Migrations became minefields`,`DTO-only public contracts`],[`Premature splitting`,`Weeks of infra for zero gain`,`Split only on concrete triggers`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monoliths: How To Build One & Lessons Learned`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xo3rsiZYsJQ`,at:`20:52`}}],diagrams:[{id:`modular-monolith-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Modular Monolith Topology: One Deployable, Many Schemas`,caption:`Every module owns its schema; the only things crossing module lines are contracts and events — never tables.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph Host["Single .NET Deployable"]
        API["HTTP API"]
        subgraph Orders["Orders Module"]
            OC["Public Contract"]
            OL["Domain Logic"]
        end
        subgraph Catalog["Catalog Module"]
            CC["Public Contract"]
            CL["Domain Logic"]
        end
        subgraph Notif["Notifications Module"]
            NH["Event Handlers"]
        end
        Med["In-Process Mediator"]
    end
    API --> OC
    API --> CC
    OL -- "OrderPlaced event" --> Med
    Med --> NH
    OL --> OD[("orders schema")]
    CL --> CD[("catalog schema")]
    NH --> ND[("notifications schema")]`},{id:`order-placed-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request Path: OrderPlaced Without a Network Hop`,caption:`The command stays inside Orders; everything else reacts to an in-process event — same process, same transaction scope option, zero network.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant O as Orders Module
    participant M as Mediator
    participant N as Notifications
    participant DB as Orders Schema
    C->>O: POST /orders
    O->>DB: INSERT order - own schema only
    O->>M: publish OrderPlaced
    M-->>N: dispatch handler
    Note over N,M: same process - no network hop
    N-->>M: handler complete
    O-->>C: 201 Created`}],takeaways:[`Start monolith-first: don't create your first microservice until a module has a concrete trigger — a dedicated team, an independent scaling profile, or divergent availability needs.`,`Target 5-9 modules, not 30: if a module's one-sentence pitch needs 'and also', split it; if two modules always change in the same commit, merge them.`,`Enforce zero cross-module SQL: one schema per module, plus a CI check that fails the build on any cross-schema join or shared entity reference.`,`Default to in-process events for cross-module communication; adopt a real message broker only when a workflow must survive a restart or outlive the HTTP request.`,`Design every public contract for extraction — thin DTOs, no domain entities leaked — so turning a module into a service is a transport swap, not a rewrite.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monoliths: How To Build One & Lessons Learned`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xo3rsiZYsJQ`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-_ral_45_9XA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:5,title:`Microservices Architecture Project Setup From Scratch With .NET Aspire`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 26:18`,minutes:26,difficulty:3,tags:[`Docker`,`.NET`,`LLM`],story:{headline:`11:20 during end-of-month billing: The Note App Went Down Because a Tag Couldn't Load`,paragraphs:[`The pager went off at 16:45 on a pre-release Friday. Our note-taking app — the one the entire support team lived in — was returning 502s, and the culprit wasn't the database or the load balancer. It was an LLM vendor that had silently started rate-limiting us. Every 'save note' request was blocking on a tag-generation call inside the same process, the thread pool filled up, and the whole app fell over because a nice-to-have AI feature couldn't reach a third-party API.`,`We 'fixed' it that night by disabling tagging with a config flag and redeploying. The postmortem the next morning was short and brutal: the failure domain of a nice-to-have feature was identical to the failure domain of core note storage. Nothing was wrong with our code. Everything was wrong with our boundaries.`,`So we rebuilt it with a hard line between a Notes service and a Tags service — separate processes, separate databases, an explicit contract with a fallback path — and .NET Aspire to make running the entire topology locally a single F5 instead of a README the size of a novella.`],problem:`Why run a separate tag service at all — why not ship notes and tagging together as one tidy monolith and skip the operational overhead?`},tech:{stack:[`.NET Aspire`,`C#`,`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Docker`],note:`Aspire's AppHost orchestrates both APIs, provisions Postgres with persistent volumes, and injects connection strings and service discovery so the two services find each other without config sprawl.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just one deployable?`,body:[`Let's be honest about the default answer: a monolith. One project, one deploy, one debugger. If notes and tags lived in the same process, I'd have zero network hops, zero serialization contracts, zero 'which version is deployed where' conversations. For a two-feature app that's genuinely hard to beat, and anyone who tells you otherwise is selling something.`,`But look at where change and failure actually concentrate. Tagging is about to swing from a hand-rolled algorithm to an LLM call — a completely different rate of change, a different dependency surface, a different blast radius. Notes storage is boring and stable; tagging is volatile and experimental. When two parts of a system have different volatility, putting them in one deployable means the volatile part gets to take the stable part down with it. That's the 10:00 at peak checkout volume story.`,`So the split isn't 'microservices because scale.' It's microservices because failure isolation, independent deployment of the experimental part, and a security boundary — the tag service never needs PII, just the note content it's tagging.`],quote:`Split where volatility and failure concentrate — not where the architecture diagram looks impressive.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single monolith`,`One deploy, no network hops, easy debugging`,`Tagging outage takes notes down; AI churn redeploys everything`,`Tiny apps, stable features`],[`Two services (notes + tags)`,`Failure isolation, independent releases, PII stays out of tag path`,`Network hops, contracts, more moving parts`,`Volatile dependency next to a stable core`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Architecture Project Setup From Scratch With .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_ral_45_9XA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what crosses the notes → tags line?`,body:[`Once there are two services, the interesting question is the contract. The notes service calls the tags service with the note content and gets back a set of tags. That's it. No user identity, no account data — the tag service doesn't need PII to tag text, so it doesn't get PII. That's a security boundary you get for free the moment you draw the line.`,`And because the LLM lives behind the tag service, the fallback becomes a first-class design decision: if tags is down or the LLM is rate-limiting, the notes service saves the note anyway, just without tags. Degrade, don't fail. In the monolith that fallback was a config flag we flipped at 3 AM; here it's a code path we wrote on day one.`,`The cost is real, though: one more hop, one more serialization boundary, one more thing to version. You're paying latency and complexity to buy isolation. If tagging were stable and purely internal, I wouldn't pay it.`],quote:`A dependency you can't afford to lose is a dependency you must be able to run without.`,table:{headers:[`Coupling choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-process call (monolith)`,`Fast, transactional, simple`,`Shared failure domain`,`Stable internal logic`],[`Sync HTTP with fallback`,`Isolation, PII boundary, graceful degradation`,`Latency hop, partial failures to design for`,`Volatile external dependency (LLM)`],[`Async messaging`,`Best isolation, retries built in`,`Eventual consistency, infra overhead`,`High-volume, latency-tolerant flows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Architecture Project Setup From Scratch With .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_ral_45_9XA`,at:`2:03`}},{heading:`03 · Database-per-service on one server: the pragmatic middle`,body:[`Each service gets its own database — notes_db and tags_db — because shared tables mean shared schema migrations, shared performance fate, and a boundary you can't actually enforce. But I'm hosting both on the same Postgres server on the default port 5432, provisioned by the AppHost. Two databases, one server.`,`Is that cheating? A little. Separate servers buy you independent scaling and fault tolerance, and they cost you money, ops time, and connection-string sprawl. At this scale, the logical isolation of separate databases — separate EF Core contexts, separate credentials, no cross-database joins — captures most of the boundary value. The day notes_db needs a different scaling profile, lift it to its own server; the code doesn't change, because no service ever assumed a shared server.`,`The one rule I won't bend: no service ever opens a connection to the other's database. The moment notes queries tags_db directly, the contract dissolves and you're back to a distributed monolith with extra steps.`],quote:`Separate databases buy you a boundary; separate servers only buy you invoices — until the metrics say otherwise.`,table:{headers:[`Storage layout`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Shared database, shared schema`,`Simplest, easy joins`,`No boundary, coupled migrations, coupled performance`,`Prototypes only`],[`Two DBs, one server`,`Logical isolation, cheap, one thing to run`,`Shared server fate, shared scaling ceiling`,`Small-to-mid microservice systems`],[`Two DBs, two servers`,`Independent scale and fault domains`,`Cost, ops overhead`,`Divergent load or compliance needs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Architecture Project Setup From Scratch With .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_ral_45_9XA`,at:`5:16`}},{heading:`04 · What the AppHost does while you're not looking`,body:[`Here's where Aspire earns its seat. In the AppHost I call builder.AddPostgres, let it take the default port 5432, add .WithDataVolume() so data survives, and .WithLifetime(ContainerLifetime.Persistent) so the container outlives the debug session. Two lines, and I have a real Postgres with a dashboard, connection strings injected into whichever service references it, and service discovery so notes finds tags by name — no localhost-colon-random-port archaeology in appsettings.`,`Compare that to the old way: a docker-compose file, a wiki page of connection strings, a 'works on my machine' Slack thread every time someone's port collided. Aspire doesn't eliminate distributed systems — it eliminates the tax of running them locally. F5 boots the whole topology: both APIs, Postgres, the volumes, the wiring, with traces on the dashboard out of the box.`,`And it's honest about what it isn't: Aspire is orchestration for development and composition, not a production orchestrator. You still deploy to Kubernetes or App Service or whatever — but you develop against the real shape of the system, not a mocked-down approximation of it.`],quote:`Aspire doesn't remove the distributed tax — it stops you paying it twice, once in prod and once on your laptop.`,table:{headers:[`Local orchestration`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`docker-compose + wiki docs`,`Familiar, prod-adjacent`,`Manual connection strings, no dashboard, drifts from code`,`Legacy setups`],[`Aspire AppHost`,`Code-first resources, injected config, dashboard, persistent volumes`,`New dependency, .NET-centric`,`.NET microservices built from scratch`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Architecture Project Setup From Scratch With .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_ral_45_9XA`,at:`7:53`}},{heading:`05 · The first entity: boring on purpose`,body:[`The Note entity is deliberately unexciting: Id, Title, Content, CreatedAtUtc, UpdatedAtUtc. The _Utc suffix isn't pedantry — in a system where two services on two clocks write and read the same logical record, local time is how you get a tag that claims it was generated before the note existed. UTC everywhere, and the name reminds you at the type level.`,`Each service owns its DbContext and its migrations. The Notes API owns notes_db's schema; the Tags API owns tags_db's. Nobody shares a migration folder, so nobody's rename breaks somebody else's deploy. It's the same discipline as the service boundary, applied one level down.`,`This is the part of the talk where nothing catches fire, and that's the point. Get the boring substrate right — timestamps, contexts, migrations, volumes — and the interesting failures stay in the interesting places, like whether your LLM hallucinates a tag called 'definitely-not-sql-injection'.`],quote:`UTC timestamps and one migration owner per database — the cheapest insurance you will ever buy.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Architecture Project Setup From Scratch With .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_ral_45_9XA`,at:`11:48`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`System topology under the Aspire AppHost`,caption:`Two services, two databases on one Postgres server, and the LLM hidden behind the tag boundary — all booted by a single F5.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph aspire["Aspire AppHost — one F5 runs everything"]
        direction TB
        notes["Notes API<br/>EF Core + notes_db context"]
        tags["Tags API<br/>manual → LLM tagging"]
        pg[("Postgres :5432<br/>WithDataVolume · Persistent")]
        ndb[("notes_db")]
        tdb[("tags_db")]
    end
    client["Client"] --> notes
    notes -->|"tag request, fallback on failure"| tags
    tags --> llm["LLM Provider"]
    notes --> ndb
    tags --> tdb
    ndb --- pg
    tdb --- pg`},{id:`note-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Save-note request with graceful degradation`,caption:`The note always persists; tags are best-effort. If the LLM or the Tags API is down, the fallback path returns the note without tags instead of failing the request.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant N as Notes API
    participant T as Tags API
    participant L as LLM Provider
    participant D as notes_db
    C->>N: POST /notes
    N->>D: INSERT note (EF Core)
    N->>T: POST /tags {content}
    alt LLM healthy
        T->>L: generate tags
        L-->>T: tags[]
        T-->>N: 200 OK, tags[]
    else LLM or Tags API down
        N->>N: fallback — save without tags
    end
    N-->>C: 201 Created`}],takeaways:[`Split out a service only when it wins at least two of three: different failure mode, different change cadence, different data sensitivity — one out of three is just a distributed monolith with extra hops.`,`Give every service its own database from day one, even on a shared Postgres server — separate databases cost zero extra infrastructure and prevent the shared-schema rewrite later.`,`Set WithDataVolume() plus ContainerLifetime.Persistent on local dev databases — restarts then cost seconds instead of a reseed-and-rebuild cycle.`,`Name every timestamp with a _Utc suffix and store UTC only — one timezone collision between two services costs a full incident retro.`,`Design the fallback before the happy path ships: every cross-service call gets a documented degradation, so a note still persists even when the tag service and its LLM are completely down.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Architecture Project Setup From Scratch With .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_ral_45_9XA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-191CJFrvBxM`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:6,title:`Distributed Systems Made Easy: URL Shortener Architecture with .NET Aspire`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 24:50`,minutes:25,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The All-Hands Link That Took Down Postgres at 9:01 AM`,paragraphs:[`It was 8:55 AM on all-hands day. Comms had baked our URL shortener into the CEO's opening slide — one short link, about to be flashed at forty thousand employees. At 9:01 the redirects started timing out. Postgres was pinned at 100% CPU, the connection pool was exhausted, and every GET /{code} was paying the full database toll, because 'we'll add caching later' had never made it onto the board.`,`The 9:15 hotfix made it worse. We added a hits counter column on the URL row, which meant every redirect now did an UPDATE. We had turned our hottest read into a write, and hot rows don't share — every click on the viral link serialized behind the last one. Two outages in one morning, both self-inflicted, both avoidable with two tables and one cache.`,`Rebuilding it with .NET Aspire is what finally made the whole topology visible: Postgres and Redis declared as resources in the AppHost, telemetry and health checks wired by ServiceDefaults, and a dashboard showing exactly which hop was burning. This is that rebuild — from an empty .NET 9 Web API to a redirect path that survives a keynote.`],problem:`Design a URL shortener with two core flows — shorten a long URL into a unique 7-character code, and redirect GET requests via HTTP 302 to the original URL — while keeping the hot redirect path off Postgres, tracking visits without contending on hot rows, and handling short-code collisions race-free.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET Aspire`,`.NET 9 Web API`,`Postgres`,`Redis`,`Npgsql`,`ServiceDefaults`],note:`The AppHost declares Postgres and Redis as first-class resources and injects their connection strings; ServiceDefaults adds telemetry, health checks, service discovery and resilience, while a startup background service creates the shortened_urls and url_visits tables.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: a redirect is a read, so count the reads`,body:[`Let's start with what we're actually building. Two endpoints. POST /shorten takes a long URL and hands back a short code. GET /{code} should respond with a 302 and send the browser to the original long URL. Now ask yourself: how many links do people create versus how many do they click? The write path is a rounding error. The read path is the entire product.`,`The naive version — and I've shipped it — does a SELECT WHERE short_code = @code against Postgres on every redirect. At demo traffic, fine. At all-hands traffic, every redirect is a connection checkout, a query, a round trip. And the moment you also want visit tracking on that same request, you're doing a write per read, which is the real killer.`,`So the design question isn't 'does Postgres work?' It's 'what fraction of redirects can we serve without touching Postgres at all?' That one number basically decides the whole architecture.`],quote:`Nobody shares a link they never click — design for the click, not the create.`,table:{headers:[`Endpoint`,`Traffic share`,`Latency budget`,`Failure mode`],rows:[[`POST /shorten`,`~1% of requests`,`100–300 ms, user is waiting anyway`,`Slow onboarding, merely annoying`],[`GET /{code} → 302`,`~99% of requests`,`< 50 ms, browsers retry aggressively`,`Connection pool exhaustion, full outage`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Systems Made Easy: URL Shortener Architecture with .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=191CJFrvBxM`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: two tables, and why visits live apart`,body:[`On startup, a database initializer background service creates the database if it doesn't exist, then two tables. shortened_urls holds the short code — with a unique constraint — plus the original URL we redirect to. url_visits records one row per successful visit for a given short code.`,`Here's the trap I fell into live on stage: why not just a hits counter column on shortened_urls? Because then every redirect is an UPDATE on the exact row everyone is reading. One viral link and you serialize every single click through one row version. Insert-only visit rows are lock-free, they're a free audit log, and you can aggregate them whenever you actually need the number.`,`And notice the unique constraint on short_code is doing double duty. It's the integrity guarantee, yes — but as we'll see in a minute, it's also the referee for collisions. One constraint, two jobs. That's the kind of bargain I'll take every time.`],quote:`Your hottest read should never also be a write.`,table:{headers:[`Visit tracking`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Counter column on URL row`,`One row, trivial to query`,`UPDATE per redirect, row contention on hot links`,`Avoid`],[`Insert-only url_visits table`,`Lock-free, full audit trail, aggregatable later`,`Table grows over time`,`Best fit`],[`In-memory counter`,`Fastest possible`,`Lost on restart, zero history`,`Demos only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Systems Made Easy: URL Shortener Architecture with .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=191CJFrvBxM`,at:`1:49`}},{heading:`03 · The cache question: why Redis, and why cache-aside`,body:[`GetOriginalUrl becomes three moves: try Redis first. Hit? Return it. Miss? SELECT from Postgres, SET the result into Redis with a TTL, then return. That's cache-aside, and the entire contract fits in three lines of code — which is exactly why I reach for it first.`,`Why not write-through and populate the cache at shorten time? You can, and it's free on the write path — but you still need the miss path regardless, because Redis restarts, evicts, and expires. Cache-aside is the only strategy where a cold cache is merely slow instead of wrong. And why not cache forever? Links get retired and edited; a TTL bounds both staleness and memory.`,`The honest wart is the stampede: many concurrent misses on the same hot code all hit Postgres at once. At this scale that's a handful of duplicate SELECTs — annoying, not fatal. Single-flight or a lock is the next rung on the ladder, not day one.`],quote:`Cache-aside is the only cache strategy where a cold cache is just slow — never wrong.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`No cache`,`Always fresh, zero extra infra`,`Every redirect hits Postgres`,`Dev only`],[`Cache-aside + TTL`,`Simple, self-healing, cold = slow not wrong`,`Miss penalty, brief stampedes`,`Best fit`],[`Write-through`,`Cache always warm after create`,`Still needs a miss path; couples writes`,`Nice-to-have on top`],[`Write-behind`,`Low write latency`,`Data-loss risk on crash`,`Overkill here`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Systems Made Easy: URL Shortener Architecture with .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=191CJFrvBxM`,at:`6:55`}},{heading:`04 · Collisions: let the unique constraint be the referee`,body:[`The short code is 7 characters drawn from lowercase, uppercase, and digits — a 62-symbol alphabet, so 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion combinations. We build it with Enumerable.Range over seven slots, picking RandomShared.Next from the alphabet each time. Sounds collision-proof, right? Birthday math disagrees: at roughly 265,000 live codes you're already at about 1% odds of a collision on a given insert. It will happen. Plan for it.`,`The wrong way is check-then-insert: two concurrent requests can generate the same code between your SELECT and your INSERT, and now you have a race. The unique constraint on short_code is the only referee that can't be raced. So we catch PostgresException filtered with a when clause on SqlState == PostgresErrorCodes.UniqueViolation — that's error 23505 — log it, regenerate a new code, and try again.`,`And why not sequential base62 IDs, which never collide at all? Because /1000001, /1000002 is an enumerable directory of everyone's links. Randomness isn't aesthetics here — it's cheap access control. I'll pay one retry loop for that trade every single time.`],quote:`Don't try to prevent the collision — recover from it in one retry.`,table:{headers:[`Code strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Random 7-char, 62-symbol alphabet`,`~3.5T space, non-enumerable`,`Needs a collision retry loop`,`Best fit`],[`Sequential base62`,`Zero collisions ever`,`Guessable, enumerable links`,`Internal tools only`],[`Hash of the URL`,`Deterministic, idempotent`,`Same URL collides with itself, still needs handling`,`Avoid`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Systems Made Easy: URL Shortener Architecture with .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=191CJFrvBxM`,at:`12:05`}},{heading:`05 · Why Aspire earns its keep`,body:[`Here's the part nobody tells you: the shortener itself is maybe two hundred lines. The distributed part — two backing services, connection strings, health checks, telemetry, resilience — is where all the time actually goes. With Aspire, the AppHost declares Postgres and Redis as resources in a couple of lines and injects the connection strings into the API. No appsettings archaeology, no docker-compose drift between machines.`,`ServiceDefaults scaffolds the good stuff into the API project out of the box: telemetry, health checks, service discovery, resilience. When the dashboard shows the Redis hop going red while Postgres stays green, you're debugging a topology, not grepping a log file. That's the real product of this lesson — press F5 and the whole system comes up as one thing you can actually see.`],quote:`Aspire doesn't make your system distributed — it makes it visible while it is.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Systems Made Easy: URL Shortener Architecture with .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=191CJFrvBxM`,at:`15:19`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two paths, two backing services`,caption:`The write path with its collision-retry loop, and the read path with cache-aside — Postgres is the source of truth, Redis is the shock absorber.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph W["Write path - POST /shorten"]
        W1["Long URL arrives"] --> W2["Generate 7-char code<br/>from 62-char alphabet"]
        W2 --> W3["INSERT INTO shortened_urls"]
        W3 -->|"23505 unique_violation"| W4["Log collision"]
        W4 --> W2
        W3 -->|"success"| W5["Return short URL"]
    end
    subgraph R["Read path - GET /{code}"]
        R1["Redirect request"] --> R2["GET code from Redis"]
        R2 --> R3{"Cache hit?"}
        R3 -->|"yes"| R4["302 to original URL"]
        R3 -->|"no"| R5["SELECT original_url<br/>FROM Postgres"]
        R5 --> R6["SET code with TTL"]
        R6 --> R4
        R4 --> R7["INSERT url_visits row"]
    end
    W3 --> P[("Postgres<br/>shortened_urls + url_visits")]
    R5 --> P
    R2 --> RD[("Redis cache")]
    R6 --> RD
    R7 --> P`},{id:`redirect-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The hot path, hop by hop`,caption:`A single redirect request: Redis answers the warm majority, Postgres only sees the cold minority — and the visit is recorded as an insert, never an update.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant B as Browser
    participant A as Shortener API
    participant R as Redis
    participant P as Postgres
    B->>A: GET /xK7fQ2a
    A->>R: GET xK7fQ2a
    alt cache hit
        R-->>A: original URL
    else cache miss
        R-->>A: null
        A->>P: SELECT original_url WHERE short_code = xK7fQ2a
        P-->>A: original URL
        A->>R: SET xK7fQ2a with TTL
    end
    A-->>B: 302 Found, Location: original URL
    A->>P: INSERT INTO url_visits`}],takeaways:[`Budget the hot path: one Redis GET is ~1 ms, one Postgres round trip is ~5–20 ms — cache-aside with a 5–15 minute TTL keeps p99 redirects under ~50 ms.`,`7 characters over a 62-symbol alphabet gives 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion codes, but birthday math puts ~1% collision odds at just ~265k live codes — ship the retry loop on day one.`,`Enforce uniqueness in the database, not in code: unique constraint on short_code plus catching PostgresException when SqlState == UniqueViolation (23505), capped at 3 retries — race-free by construction.`,`Record visits as insert-only rows in url_visits, never an UPDATE counter on the URL row — one viral link otherwise serializes every click through a single hot row.`,`Declare Postgres and Redis as AppHost resources and let ServiceDefaults wire telemetry, health checks and resilience — the dashboard should tell you which hop failed in seconds, not which log file to open.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Systems Made Easy: URL Shortener Architecture with .NET Aspire`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=191CJFrvBxM`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-distributed-systems`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj--Sq7QnUb4IE`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:7,title:`YARP vs Nginx Performance Showdown (Surprising Results!)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:17`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`YARP`,`Nginx`,`Docker`],story:{headline:`The proxy we never thought to measure`,paragraphs:[`It's 2 AM, three weeks before Black Friday. Our load test against the new checkout service keeps flatlining at the same throughput no matter what we tune, and the whole war room is pointing at the backend. Then an intern quietly asks the question nobody wants to hear: 'Has anyone ever actually measured the proxy?'`,`We hadn't. Nginx had been sitting at our edge since before any of us joined, and 'Nginx is fast' was folklore nobody had numbers for. So that night we spun up a hello-world API, put YARP in front of it, then Nginx, and pointed k6 at both — no TLS, same Docker network, identical virtual users.`,`The managed .NET proxy traded blows with the C++ legend. The room went quiet, then somebody laughed. We didn't rip Nginx out that night — but we stopped treating the hop itself as free.`],problem:`We were tuning the backend while an unmeasured proxy hop sat in the critical path of every single request.`},tech:{stack:[`YARP`,`Nginx`,`K6`,`Docker Compose`,`ASP.NET Core`],note:`A hello-world ASP.NET Core API sits behind two candidate proxies — YARP running in-process as a NuGet library, Nginx as an nginx:alpine sidecar — load-tested head-to-head with k6 over plain HTTP on a shared Docker network.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: aren't all reverse proxies the same thing?`,body:[`Here's the mistake most of us make on day one: we say 'reverse proxy' like it's one thing. It isn't. Crack open the OSI model — seven layers, physical up to application — and proxies live at either layer 4 or layer 7. Layer 4 is shuffling TCP bytes between sockets. Layer 7 is actually reading HTTP: matching paths, rewriting headers, making routing decisions.`,`Nginx can play at both layers. YARP is unapologetically a layer-7, HTTP-aware proxy written in managed .NET code. So before we benchmark anything, we have to agree on what we're measuring: raw byte-pushing, or smart HTTP routing? The surprise at the end of this talk only makes sense once that distinction clicks.`],quote:`A proxy isn't a pipe — the OSI layer it lives on decides what it costs.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Layer 4 proxy`,`Layer 7 proxy`],rows:[[`What it sees`,`TCP streams`,`Full HTTP requests`],[`Routing basis`,`IP and port`,`Paths, headers, methods`],[`Typical overhead`,`Very low`,`Slightly higher, far more control`],[`Examples`,`HAProxy TCP mode`,`Nginx, YARP`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — YARP vs Nginx Performance Showdown (Surprising Results!)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Sq7QnUb4IE`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what does one hop actually cost?`,body:[`Let's walk a request together. Client hits the proxy, the proxy opens a connection upstream, forwards the bytes, streams the response back. Every hop you insert is another socket pair, another set of buffers, another scheduling boundary. The question I couldn't shake: what does the hop itself cost, independent of the app behind it?`,`And here's the architectural wrinkle that changes everything — YARP isn't a separate box. It's a library living inside an ASP.NET Core host. One fewer network hop, one fewer container, and you can intercept the request in C# before it ever leaves the process. Nginx is a sidecar: rock solid, tiny footprint, but always another network stop on the way.`],quote:`The fastest hop is the one you delete.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — YARP vs Nginx Performance Showdown (Surprising Results!)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Sq7QnUb4IE`,at:`1:18`}},{heading:`03 · Building a benchmark rig that's actually fair`,body:[`A fair fight means controlled variables. One hello-world API with a single endpoint, sitting behind whichever proxy is on trial. Same Docker network, same host, and — critically — no HTTPS. TLS handshakes would dominate the numbers and tell us nothing about the proxy itself. Raw HTTP only.`,`Then k6 hammers each setup with the same virtual user profile while we watch throughput and latency. Yes, hello-world isn't your real workload — but that's the point. It isolates the proxy's overhead from your app's. If a proxy is slow with a hello-world behind it, it'll be worse behind your 300-millisecond handler.`],quote:`Benchmark the empty road before you blame the traffic.`,table:{headers:[`Variable`,`Choice`,`Why`],rows:[[`TLS`,`Disabled`,`Isolate raw proxy cost`],[`Backend`,`Single hello-world endpoint`,`Minimize app noise`],[`Load tool`,`k6, fixed VUs`,`Reproducible, comparable runs`],[`Network`,`Shared Docker network`,`Remove cross-host latency`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — YARP vs Nginx Performance Showdown (Surprising Results!)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Sq7QnUb4IE`,at:`2:25`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring it up: JSON in your repo versus a mounted conf file`,body:[`Wiring YARP is almost embarrassingly small. AddReverseProxy into the service container, MapReverseProxy onto the app pipeline, then a config block: a route with a cluster ID, a catch-all path wildcard, and a destination pointing at hello-api:8080 on the Docker network. That's the whole setup — config as code, versioned with the repo.`,`Nginx is the same story in a different dialect: an nginx:alpine container, exposed on port 3001, and the one step everyone trips over — mounting your nginx.conf into the container's file system so the proxy_pass actually points somewhere. Forget the volume mount and you're benchmarking the default welcome page. Ask me how I know.`],quote:`Two proxies, two philosophies: routes as JSON in your repo, or a conf file bolted into a container.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — YARP vs Nginx Performance Showdown (Surprising Results!)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Sq7QnUb4IE`,at:`4:36`}},{heading:`05 · Reading the numbers: the part nobody expected`,body:[`Here's the punchline: YARP didn't just keep up. In this layer-7 benchmark it traded blows with Nginx — matching or beating it on throughput and tail latency. The managed .NET proxy, the one half the internet assumed was a toy, held the line against the C++ veteran.`,`But don't over-rotate on one benchmark. Nginx still wins on operational maturity, layer-4 mode, and two decades of edge-case hardening. The real lesson is that for a .NET shop that wants routing decisions in code — transforms, health checks, canary destinations — YARP is no longer the risky pick. It's the boring one. And that's the highest compliment infrastructure can earn.`],quote:`The 'toy' proxy benchmarked like a peer — measure before you inherit folklore.`,table:{headers:[`Metric`,`YARP`,`Nginx`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Throughput (L7)`,`Matches or leads`,`Matches`,`Tie at layer 7`],[`p99 latency`,`Comparable`,`Comparable`,`Tie`],[`Setup effort`,`NuGet + JSON config`,`Image + conf volume mount`,`YARP for .NET teams`],[`Ops maturity`,`Growing fast`,`Decades deep`,`Nginx`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — YARP vs Nginx Performance Showdown (Surprising Results!)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Sq7QnUb4IE`,at:`6:28`}}],diagrams:[{id:`bench-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Benchmark rig topology`,caption:`One hello-world backend, two challenger proxies, one load generator — all on a single Docker network.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Rig["Benchmark rig - Docker Compose"]
        K6["k6 load generator"] -->|"GET /hello"| Y["YARP proxy :5000"]
        K6 -->|"GET /hello"| N["Nginx :3001"]
        Y -->|"proxy_pass"| API["hello-api :8080"]
        N -->|"proxy_pass"| API
    end`},{id:`yarp-request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path through YARP`,caption:`The catch-all route matches, the cluster resolves the destination, and the response streams straight back — no extra container hop.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant K as k6
    participant P as YARP in-process
    participant A as hello-api
    K->>P: GET /hello
    P->>P: Match catch-all route to cluster
    P->>A: Forward to destination hello-api:8080
    A-->>P: 200 OK
    P-->>K: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Benchmark proxies over plain HTTP first — TLS handshakes can cut measured throughput by 50%+ and bury the proxy's real cost.`,`An in-process proxy like YARP removes a full network hop; expect roughly 20-30% lower p99 than an equivalent sidecar on the same hardware.`,`Compare p99, not averages, at fixed concurrency (e.g. 500 VUs) — averages hide exactly the tail latency a proxy adds.`,`Each extra layer-7 hop costs on the order of a millisecond; keep exactly one proxy hop between client and service.`,`Pick Nginx for layer-4 work and operational maturity; pick YARP when routing must live in C# — then re-benchmark on your own workload, not folklore.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — YARP vs Nginx Performance Showdown (Surprising Results!)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Sq7QnUb4IE`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-nginx`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-8aG410nmjtQ`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:8,title:`First Look at .NET Aspire - Distributed Applications in .NET 8`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:57`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`Redis`,`Docker`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The demo that died on port 5001`,paragraphs:[`It's 11 PM the night before a customer demo. The stack is 'simple': one Web API, one Blazor front end, a Redis cache. Three projects, three launchSettings.json files, one README that says 'start Docker, then the API, then the site — check the ports.' I'd followed my own README a hundred times. That night, step two failed, because a teammate's PR had bumped the API to a new port and updated his appsettings — but not mine.`,`Forty minutes of port archaeology later, the API was up. Then the front end threw on startup: the Redis connection string pointed at a container name from a compose file I'd deleted in March. I fixed that, refreshed, and watched the page render with no cache headers — output caching was silently off because the connection name didn't match. Every fix I made was in a file that existed only so the other files could find each other.`,`The demo went fine. Barely. Two weeks later, .NET 8 shipped with Aspire, and I scaffolded the same stack from the starter template: Blazor front end, Web API backend, optional Redis. One AppHost project, one F5. The frontend's HttpClient pointed at a name — 'apiservice' — not a port. I sat there waiting for the part where I edit connection strings. That part never came.`],problem:`Distributed .NET apps drown in hand-wired plumbing — ports, connection strings, container startup order, telemetry — duplicated across projects, and every one of those seams fails independently at the worst possible moment.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET Aspire`,`.NET 8`,`AppHost`,`Service Defaults`,`Blazor`,`Redis`,`Docker`,`OpenTelemetry`],note:`The pieces Aspire scaffolds: an AppHost that orchestrates your projects and containers, a ServiceDefaults project that wires OpenTelemetry and service discovery, and the app projects themselves — a Blazor frontend, a .NET 8 Web API, and a Redis container in Docker.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does a three-service demo need an orchestrator?`,body:[`So .NET 8 just landed, and the thing I keep coming back to is Aspire — a stack for building cloud-native distributed applications in .NET. And my first reaction was, honestly, skepticism. A three-service demo doesn't need an 'orchestrator', right? You run the API, you run the frontend, you start Redis. Three commands. How hard can it be?`,`Then I counted what those three commands actually cost. Two launchSettings files, two appsettings files, a connection string, a port agreement between two codebases, and a README that says 'start Docker first.' That's five or six hand-maintained seams, and every one of them can fail independently. The Aspire starter template — Blazor front end, Web API backend, optional Redis — is exactly this stack, except the seams are generated for you.`,`You get two templates: an empty Aspire app and a starter. I picked the starter because I wanted to see the opinion. Four projects land in the solution — the two apps you'd expect, plus AppHost and ServiceDefaults, which are the actual thesis of the whole thing.`],quote:`A distributed app isn't five projects — it's five projects plus the wiring. Aspire puts the wiring in source control.`,table:{headers:[`Wiring approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`README + launchSettings`,`Zero new tooling`,`Drifts instantly, tribal knowledge`,`Solo, single project`],[`docker-compose`,`Declarative, familiar`,`Parallel source of truth, no typed refs`,`Polyglot teams`],[`Aspire AppHost`,`One F5, typed, injects config`,`New convention to learn`,`Distributed .NET apps`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — First Look at .NET Aspire - Distributed Applications in .NET 8`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8aG410nmjtQ`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the AppHost is C#, not YAML`,body:[`Open Program.cs in the AppHost and it reads like a topology diagram: create a DistributedApplicationBuilder, add a Redis container, add the API project under the name 'apiservice', add the Blazor frontend with a reference to the API. That's the whole file. This is our startup project now — F5 here and it starts Redis in Docker, then the API, then the frontend, in order, with the right environment injected.`,`Why not docker-compose? I asked myself the same thing. Compose is fine, but it's a parallel source of truth: names and ports live in YAML while the code lives in C#, and they drift. Here the project references are typed — rename ApiService and the AppHost fails at compile time, not at 11 PM before a demo. The orchestration is code, so it gets refactoring, code review, and tests.`,`And notice what the AppHost is not: it's not a scheduler or a service mesh. It's the dev-time composition root. That framing matters for everything else we're about to look at.`],quote:`Your topology should be code you can F5, not YAML you can mis-indent.`,table:{headers:[`AppHost capability`,`What it replaces`,`Failure mode without it`],rows:[[`AddProject with typed refs`,`Manual startup order`,`Half the stack not running`],[`AddRedis container`,`Hand-run docker pull/run`,`Dependency missing on fresh clone`],[`Reference injection`,`Copy-pasted env vars`,`Config drift between projects`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — First Look at .NET Aspire - Distributed Applications in .NET 8`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8aG410nmjtQ`,at:`1:09`}},{heading:`03 · Killing localhost:5001 with service discovery`,body:[`Here's the line that made me sit up. In the Blazor frontend, we configure an HttpClient for the weather API, and the base address is just... 'apiservice'. No localhost, no port. Same pattern for Redis: the output cache is added with a connection name of 'cache'. Service discovery — wired up by ServiceDefaults — resolves those logical names to real endpoints at runtime, injected by the AppHost.`,`Why not environment variables? Because someone has to maintain them, per machine, per environment, and they rot silently. Why not an API gateway? Overkill for the inner loop, and it doesn't fix config drift — it just relocates it. A logical name resolved at startup kills the entire class of 'which port is it today' bugs.`,`But be honest about the trade: you're buying into Aspire's naming conventions. 'apiservice' and 'cache' aren't magic — they're contracts between the AppHost and your apps. Document them like API contracts, because that's exactly what they are.`],quote:`A hardcoded port is config debt with a countdown timer on it.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Hardcoded localhost:port`,`Dead simple`,`Breaks on every port change`,`Throwaway code only`],[`Manual env vars`,`Flexible`,`Rots, per-machine, undocumented`,`Small stable teams`],[`Aspire logical names`,`Resolved at startup, injected`,`Tied to Aspire conventions`,`Distributed apps`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — First Look at .NET Aspire - Distributed Applications in .NET 8`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8aG410nmjtQ`,at:`1:26`}},{heading:`04 · ServiceDefaults: the boring 80%, done once`,body:[`The fourth project, ServiceDefaults, is the least glamorous and possibly the most valuable. Every app calls AddServiceDefaults, and that one extension method wires OpenTelemetry for metrics, traces, and logs, plus service discovery and health/resilience defaults. Before Aspire, every team copy-pasted this from the last project — half the services had tracing, half didn't, and you couldn't tell which, because, well, no traces.`,`This is also why the dashboard is more than a pretty process list: every project emits OpenTelemetry by default, so the distributed trace across Blazor → API → Redis just shows up. The orchestration is observable, not merely automated. And because it's a shared project in your solution, the defaults are versioned and reviewable like any other code.`],quote:`Observability you have to remember to add is observability you won't have at 3 AM.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Before ServiceDefaults`,`With ServiceDefaults`],rows:[[`Metrics, traces, logs`,`Per-project copy-paste, uneven`,`One AddServiceDefaults call`],[`Service discovery`,`Hand-rolled config`,`Wired by the same call`],[`Health & resilience`,`Often skipped entirely`,`Defaulted for every project`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — First Look at .NET Aspire - Distributed Applications in .NET 8`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8aG410nmjtQ`,at:`3:44`}},{heading:`05 · Where I'd still be careful`,body:[`Let me be honest about the edges. This is a first look on preview tooling — you need the Visual Studio preview with the Aspire templates — so I'd pin expectations before adopting it on a team. What Aspire nails is the local inner loop: orchestration, service discovery, telemetry defaults, a dashboard. What it does not do is replace your production story. Kubernetes, CD pipelines, secrets, scaling — still yours.`,`Second edge: polyglot systems. The typed project references shine when the whole graph is .NET; anything else joins as a container, which works but loses some of the magic. And third: those logical names are conventions — if the next dev can't grep where 'cache' comes from, you've traded one kind of tribal knowledge for another. Keep the AppHost readable and it stays an asset, not a new priesthood.`],quote:`Aspire deletes the local-dev tax; it doesn't cancel your production bill.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Aspire covers it`,`Still your job`],rows:[[`Local orchestration & inner loop`,`Yes — AppHost + dashboard`,`—`],[`Telemetry emission`,`OTel by default`,`Backend & sampling choices`],[`Production deployment`,`No`,`k8s, CD, secrets, scaling`],[`Polyglot services`,`Partly, as containers`,`Non-.NET integration depth`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — First Look at .NET Aspire - Distributed Applications in .NET 8`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8aG410nmjtQ`,at:`9:38`}}],diagrams:[{id:`aspire-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Aspire starter topology — one AppHost, four projects`,caption:`The AppHost composes a Redis container in Docker plus two .NET projects; ServiceDefaults wires service discovery and OpenTelemetry into every app.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph APPHOST["AppHost — startup project"]
        B["DistributedApplicationBuilder"]
    end
    subgraph DOCKER["Docker runtime"]
        REDIS[("Redis container — name 'cache'")]
    end
    B -->|"AddRedis → container"| REDIS
    B -->|"AddProject → apiservice"| API["apiservice — .NET 8 Web API + ServiceDefaults"]
    B -->|"AddProject → webfrontend"| FE["webfrontend — Blazor + ServiceDefaults"]
    FE -->|"service discovery reference"| API
    FE -->|"output caching → 'cache'"| REDIS
    API -.->|"OTel metrics, traces, logs"| DASH["Aspire Dashboard"]
    FE -.->|"OTel metrics, traces, logs"| DASH`},{id:`weather-request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Weather request with output caching`,caption:`The frontend resolves 'apiservice' by name and reads or writes the Redis output cache — no localhost and no port anywhere in the code.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant U as Browser
    participant FE as webfrontend Blazor
    participant R as Redis cache
    participant API as apiservice
    U->>FE: GET /weather
    FE->>FE: resolve apiservice via service discovery
    alt output cache hit
        FE->>R: GET cached page
        R-->>FE: cached HTML
        FE-->>U: render from cache
    else cache miss
        FE->>API: GET /weatherforecast
        API-->>FE: JSON forecast
        FE->>R: SET output cache entry
        FE-->>U: rendered page
    end
    Note over FE,API: logical names only — no localhost, no port`}],takeaways:[`One F5 rule: if booting your distributed app takes more than one step, model the startup in an AppHost — the starter template wires 4 projects and a Redis container from a single entry point.`,`Zero hardcoded hosts: replace every localhost:port with a logical name ('apiservice', 'cache') resolved at startup — that deletes an entire class of config-drift bugs.`,`Telemetry in under 10 lines: one AddServiceDefaults call per project gives you OpenTelemetry metrics, traces, and logs — if your setup takes longer, you're copy-pasting.`,`Dependencies as code: declare Redis (or any dependency) as a container in the AppHost so a fresh clone is running in minutes, not an afternoon of README archaeology.`,`Know the boundary: Aspire nails local orchestration and observability defaults, but production deployment (k8s, CD, secrets) stays yours — budget for that seam on day one.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — First Look at .NET Aspire - Distributed Applications in .NET 8`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8aG410nmjtQ`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-P1wAvBKriVs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:9,title:`Stop Sacrificing Performance for "Pure" Domain Models`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 10:41`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`DDD`],story:{headline:`The Rule Was Five. The Query Loaded Five Thousand.`,paragraphs:["It started as a Tuesday standup note: 'Users can have at most five active high-priority to-dos — make it configurable.' Our most DDD-devout engineer lit up like it was Christmas. A real invariant! He built it the textbook way: a static `Create` factory on `TodoItem`, a `Result` return type, the limit check living right inside the domain, zero out-of-process references. Pure. Complete. Merged by Thursday with a string of fire emojis on the PR.","The factory needed one input: the user's existing active high-priority to-dos, passed in as a read-only list. So the handler fetched them with EF Core — every not-completed to-do with priority High or Top, fully materialized into memory. For the demo account with six to-dos, nobody noticed. Then, at 2:57 AM, a power user with a five-year backlog hit the create endpoint, and a request that should cost 20 milliseconds was dragging thousands of entities through the change tracker just to call `.Count` on them. PagerDuty made sure the whole team attended the retrospective.",`The kicker came in the postmortem: we were materializing entire aggregates to answer a question whose answer is a single integer. The rule was five. The query loaded five thousand.`],problem:`How do you enforce a cross-entity invariant — at most five active high-priority to-dos — inside a pure, complete domain model, without materializing every entity the rule mentions?`},tech:{stack:[`.NET`,`EF Core`,`DDD`,`Result Pattern`,`Static Factory`,`Scalar Count Query`],note:"A .NET command handler feeds a pure `TodoItem.Create` factory the facts its invariant needs — a scalar EF Core count instead of materialized entities — so the five-high-priority-to-dos rule stays complete, pure, and fast."},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the impossible triangle`,body:[`Let me set the table, because this entire talk balances on three words that Vladimir Khorikov put together, and once you see them you cannot unsee them. Purity means your domain model references no out-of-process dependencies — no database calls, nothing you'd have to mock. Completeness means all the business logic lives inside the model; the command handler is just a dumb courier. And performance means you don't drag data you don't need through memory. Simple words. Brutal combination.`,"Here's the trap. Completeness demands our new rule — at most five active high-priority to-dos, High or Top on the priority enum, configurable — lives inside `TodoItem.Create`. But that rule needs to know about the user's existing to-dos, and those live in the database. Purity forbids the model from calling the database. So the caller has to supply the data — and the naive way to supply it is to load all of it. That's the triangle: purity, completeness, performance. Pick two — and as I'll show you, for most rules you can actually negotiate all three."],quote:`Purity, completeness, performance — you pick two per rule, not two per codebase.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`What it means`,`What it costs`],rows:[[`Purity`,`No out-of-process dependencies inside the model`,`Caller must supply every fact the rule needs`],[`Completeness`,`All business logic enforced inside the model`,`Invariants need data, sometimes lots of it`],[`Performance`,`Load the minimum needed to decide`,`Something gives — usually purity or completeness`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Sacrificing Performance for "Pure" Domain Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P1wAvBKriVs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what purity actually costs`,body:["So version one — the one you'd show at a DDD workshop. A static `Create` factory on `TodoItem` takes the priority plus `IReadOnlyList<TodoItem> existingHighPriorityTodos`. Inside: if the priority is High or Top and the existing count is greater than or equal to the max, return `Result.Failure(TodoItemErrors.HighPriorityLimitReached)`. Otherwise return the new item. The unit test is a list literal and an assertion. No mocks, no database, no infrastructure. This is what pure and complete buys you, and it's genuinely great.",`Now the bill arrives. The handler has to satisfy that contract, so it runs an EF Core query: this user's to-dos where not completed and priority in High or Top — materialized, full entities, straight into the change tracker. Demo user, six rows, invisible. Real user with a five-year backlog: every single create drags the entire active backlog through memory to answer a yes/no question. And before anyone reaches for lazy loading — no. Lazy loading is an out-of-process dependency sneaking in through the back door while your model keeps pretending it's pure.`],quote:`A pure model doesn't fetch its own data — it makes the caller confess the cost.`,table:{headers:[`You get`,`You pay`],rows:[[`Invariant unit-tested with zero mocks`,`Full entities materialized on every create`],[`Rule visible in one factory method`,`Change-tracker bloat grows with the backlog`],[`No hidden I/O inside the model`,`Handler owns an extra query per command`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Sacrificing Performance for "Pure" Domain Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P1wAvBKriVs`,at:`1:42`}},{heading:`03 · The count trick: ship the number, not the entities`,body:["Here's the move that gets the headline: look at what the rule actually consumes. It reads `.Count`. That's it. So change the factory signature — `TodoItem.Create(priority, int existingHighPriorityCount, int max)` — and have the handler run `CountAsync` with the same predicate: not completed, priority High or Top. One scalar crosses the wire. No entities, no change-tracker churn, and the comparison still lives inside the domain model where completeness demands it. For this shape of rule, you get all three: pure, complete, and fast.",`One asterisk, and I'd be lying if I skipped it: the model now reasons about a number, not the entities. The day this rule evolves — 'unless one is pinned', 'unless created today' — a count stops being enough. That's when I reach for Khorikov's extension-method pattern: keep the factory pure, put the database-aware half in an extension method beside it, so the decision point stays in domain-shaped code without injecting repositories into your entities. The count trick isn't a religion; it's the first tool out of the bag.`],quote:`If your invariant reduces to an integer, ship the integer — not the entities.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Materialized list into factory`,`Full context, trivially testable`,`Loads N entities per create`,`Tiny datasets`],[`Scalar count into factory`,`One value on the wire, model stays pure`,`Rule limited to countable facts`,`Count-shaped limits — most rules`],[`Extension method with DB access`,`Model stays pure, rule stays near the model`,`Completeness split across files`,`Rules needing entity detail`],[`DB constraint or trigger`,`Fastest, race-safe`,`Rule leaves the model, painful to test`,`Backstop, not the brain`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Sacrificing Performance for "Pure" Domain Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P1wAvBKriVs`,at:`2:21`}},{heading:`04 · Why not just push it into the database?`,body:[`Now the heresy question, which someone asks every single time: why not just push the whole check into the database and move on? Two reasons it's not free. First, expressiveness: a filtered index can't say 'at most five rows matching this predicate' — you're into triggers or indexed views, and your configurable five just moved from app settings into schema. Second, completeness: the invariant has left the domain model entirely, and I promise the next engineer will re-implement it slightly differently in a handler — now the business rule lives in two places and disagrees.`,`And here's the race nobody mentions until it's 3 AM: check-then-insert is not atomic. Two concurrent creates both read a count of four, both pass, and you have six high-priority to-dos while your rule swears there are five. If you enforce in the database, wrap it in a serializable transaction or take a lock. If you enforce in the model, be honest that the count check is advisory under concurrency, and decide deliberately whether drift is acceptable or needs that transaction. My stance: the model decides, the database backstops.`],quote:`A database constraint is a great seatbelt — and a terrible steering wheel.`,table:{headers:[`Enforcement point`,`Race safety`,`Testability`,`Config-friendly`],rows:[[`Domain factory with count`,`Needs a transaction`,`Pure unit tests`,`Yes — parameter`],[`Handler-side check`,`No`,`Integration tests`,`Yes`],[`DB trigger`,`Yes`,`Painful`,`Schema change`],[`Serializable txn + factory`,`Yes`,`Unit plus integration`,`Yes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Sacrificing Performance for "Pure" Domain Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P1wAvBKriVs`,at:`3:28`}},{heading:`05 · Choosing per rule, not per religion`,body:[`So let me land the plane. Stop asking 'is our domain model pure?' like it's a religion with one answer, and start asking it per rule. Count-shaped rules get the scalar count. Rules that need entity detail get the extension-method pattern. Money-and-audit invariants get a domain check with a database backstop underneath. The triangle isn't a verdict — it's a dial, and you turn it for every invariant you meet.`,`And here's the bar I actually enforce in code review: if the set of entities an invariant needs could exceed a hundred rows in production, it does not get materialized — find the scalar, the projection, or the extension method that carries the same decision. The tell is always testability: the moment you can't unit-test a business rule without standing up a database, that rule has already left the domain model, whatever the class diagram claims.`],quote:`The triangle is a dial, not a verdict — turn it per rule.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Sacrificing Performance for "Pure" Domain Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P1wAvBKriVs`,at:`7:03`}}],diagrams:[{id:`seq-create-todo`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Create to-do: the count-based request path`,caption:`One scalar crosses the wire; the domain factory still owns the decision — including the 409.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant H as CreateTodoHandler
    participant DB as EF Core / SQL
    participant D as Domain Model
    C->>H: CreateTodo command — priority High
    H->>DB: CountAsync — active, priority High/Top
    DB-->>H: scalar count = 4
    H->>D: TodoItem.Create — priority, existingCount 4, max 5
    alt count below max
        D-->>H: Result.Ok(todo)
        H->>DB: Add + SaveChanges
        H-->>C: 201 Created
    else count at or above max
        D-->>H: Result.Failure — HighPriorityLimitReached
        H-->>C: 409 Conflict
    end`},{id:`flow-purity-boundary`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The purity boundary: data crosses, dependencies don't`,caption:`The handler queries, the database counts, and only plain data crosses into the pure domain model.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph APP["Application layer"]
        CMD["CreateTodo command"] --> H["CreateTodoHandler"]
        DB[("Database")]
    end
    subgraph DOM["Pure domain model"]
        F["TodoItem.Create factory"]
        ERR["HighPriorityLimitReached"]
    end
    H -- "1. CountAsync active High/Top" --> DB
    DB -- "2. scalar count" --> H
    H -- "3. Create priority, count, max" --> F
    F -- "4. Result Ok / Failure" --> H
    F -.- ERR
    F -. "never touches the DB" .- DB`}],takeaways:[`Pick two of three — purity, completeness, performance — per invariant, not per project; for count-shaped rules you can negotiate all three.`,"If a rule only reads `.Count`, pass an int: one `CountAsync` scalar instead of N materialized entities — a 5,000-to-do backlog becomes a single integer on the wire.","Keep the comparison (`existingCount >= max`) inside the domain factory so the invariant stays unit-testable with zero mocks and zero database.",`Check-then-insert is a race: two concurrent creates can both see count = 4 and both insert — wrap in a serializable transaction or consciously accept and reconcile drift.`,`Set a materialization budget: if an invariant's entity set could exceed ~100 rows in production, don't load it — reach for a scalar, a projection, or Khorikov's extension-method pattern.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Sacrificing Performance for "Pure" Domain Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P1wAvBKriVs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-wi_wsw5Gp6Q`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:10,title:`Use This Pattern To Design a Rich Domain Model With DDD`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 9:31`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`DDD`],story:{headline:`The €0.00 Invoice: A Guard That Lived in the Wrong Place`,paragraphs:[`02:47, invoice-run night. Finance drops a screenshot into the on-call channel: order #8841 has zero line items, and the invoicing job just emitted a €0.00 invoice to a customer who definitely didn't order nothing. Git blame lands on a three-week-old PR titled 'nightly cleanup job' — a second call site for RemoveLineItem, written by someone who had no way of knowing the command handler was quietly running a guard before every removal.`,`Because the rule did exist. 'You can never remove the last line item on an order.' It was enforced — as an if-statement in the RemoveLineItem command handler, right before order.RemoveLineItem(lineItemId): orderRepository.HasOneLineItem(order). One line, perfectly correct, and completely invisible to anyone who didn't read that exact handler before touching the aggregate.`,"The postmortem fix wasn't a checklist or another review gate. It was making the rule impossible to bypass: pull it out of the handler, push it down into the Order aggregate, and let the aggregate call back into its repository mid-method with `this` — double dispatch. The handler got smaller. The invariant got structural."],problem:`How do you enforce an invariant that depends on data the aggregate didn't load — 'is this the last line item?' — without trusting every future caller to remember a two-step dance?`},tech:{stack:[`C# / .NET`,`DDD`,`Double Dispatch`,`Rich Domain Model`,`Repository Pattern`,`Domain Events`,`Unit of Work`],note:`An Order aggregate that owns its invariants, a repository it can call back into mid-method, a unit of work to persist, and a domain event marking the mutation — double dispatch is the glue between the first two.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the guard in the handler fails`,body:[`Let's start where the code starts. The RemoveLineItem command handler is dead simple: one call to the order repository fetches the order plus the line item we're removing, we bail if the order is null, we call order.RemoveLineItem(lineItemId), we persist with SaveChangesAsync. Ship it. Then the business walks in: 'you can only remove a line item if the order has more than one.' First instinct — the lazy one, and I've written it a dozen times — is to add a HasOneLineItem method on the order repository and slot the check into the handler right before the RemoveLineItem call.`,`It compiles, it passes the test, and it's a landmine. Look at what just happened: the handler now knows a secret — you must call HasOneLineItem before you're allowed to call RemoveLineItem. That's coupling between two method calls, and nothing in the type system enforces the order. The next caller — a cleanup job, a different handler, a migration script — copies the RemoveLineItem call and skips the check, because the check isn't theirs. It's nobody's. That's exactly how the €0.00 invoice happened.`],quote:`A rule that lives in the caller is a suggestion; a rule that lives in the aggregate is a law.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Guard in handler`,`Zero domain changes, quick`,`Every caller must remember the dance; compiler won't help`,`Never — demo only`],[`Load all items, count in aggregate`,`Pure aggregate, no infra dependency`,`Reads N rows to answer a yes/no question`,`Tiny collections`],[`Double dispatch to repository`,`Invariant unskippable; reads minimal data`,`Domain touches a repository interface`,`This lesson's case`],[`Domain service`,`Aggregate stays dependency-free`,`Still a second object callers must invoke correctly`,`Cross-aggregate rules`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Use This Pattern To Design a Rich Domain Model With DDD`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wi_wsw5Gp6Q`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what double dispatch actually is`,body:[`Double dispatch sounds like compiler folklore, but the C# version is two lines long. First dispatch: the handler calls RemoveLineItem on the Order — the runtime picks the method by the receiver's type. Second dispatch: inside that method, the Order calls orderRepository.HasOneLineItem(this) — passing itself, the current object instance, into another object. That's the whole trick. The repository receives an Order instance and decides what it needs to do with it to answer the question — in our case, a COUNT query.`,`So we widen the signature: RemoveLineItem(lineItemId, IOrderRepository orderRepository). Inside, if HasOneLineItem(this) evaluates to true, we return — the last line item doesn't move. Otherwise we fetch the line item by ID, return if it's null, remove it from the list of line items, and raise a LineItemRemoved domain event. The guard and the mutation now live in the same method body, and the handler's check deletes entirely — the handler just passes a repository instance in to satisfy the signature. One call site, zero secrets.`],quote:"Pass `this`, and the object vouches for itself — no caller can forget a step that no longer exists.",source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Use This Pattern To Design a Rich Domain Model With DDD`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wi_wsw5Gp6Q`,at:`2:48`}},{heading:`03 · The objection in the room: a repository inside the domain?`,body:[`Every time I show this, someone says it: 'you've polluted the aggregate with infrastructure.' Fair challenge — let's take it seriously. The pure alternative is loading every line item and counting in memory. That keeps the aggregate dependency-free, but you're shipping N rows over the wire to answer a boolean. For an order with 200 line items, that's 200 entities materialized so your ORM can tell you what a COUNT would have said for free.`,`The other classic answer is a domain service. Also legitimate — but notice what it does to our original problem: now the caller must remember to invoke the service check before the aggregate method, which is the exact two-step dance we just killed. Unless the aggregate itself calls the service — which is double dispatch wearing a nicer suit. The pragmatic line I draw: inject the narrowest possible abstraction, an IOrderRepository interface exposing the one query you need, so the domain depends on a contract — not on EF Core, not on SQL, not on a connection string.`],quote:`Purity is a dial, not a switch — spend it where the invariant is actually at risk.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Domain purity`,`Rows read for the guard`,`Bypassable?`],rows:[[`Count in memory`,`Full — no dependencies`,`N (all line items)`,`No`],[`Domain service called by handler`,`Full`,`1 (COUNT query)`,`Yes — caller must remember`],[`Double dispatch to repository interface`,`Contract-only dependency`,`1 (COUNT query)`,`No — guard is inside the method`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Use This Pattern To Design a Rich Domain Model With DDD`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wi_wsw5Gp6Q`,at:`4:53`}},{heading:`04 · Load and traffic: fetch the minimum, then watch it run`,body:[`Now the performance detail people miss. When the handler fetches the order, the LineItems collection contains exactly one item — the one we're removing — even if the database holds fifty. That's not a bug; it's deliberate. We load the minimal amount of information necessary to perform this business operation. The 'is this the last one?' question never touches the collection — it's answered by the repository's query inside the double dispatch.`,`Watch it run: DELETE request from Postman, breakpoint in the handler, the order arrives with a single loaded line item. Step into RemoveLineItem — there's the double dispatch, the Order calling HasOneLineItem(this) back into the repository. Guard says false, the item leaves the collection, LineItemRemoved fires, and SaveChangesAsync persists. One command, one aggregate method, one place where the rule can't be skipped.`],quote:`Ship the question to the database — don't ship the whole collection to the application.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Rows read`,`Where the count happens`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Load all line items`,`N`,`In memory (aggregate)`,`Tiny orders, prototypes`],[`Load target + COUNT query`,`1 + 1`,`Repository via double dispatch`,`Production orders`],[`Denormalized item counter`,`1`,`Projection / read model`,`Very hot paths, eventual consistency risk`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Use This Pattern To Design a Rich Domain Model With DDD`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wi_wsw5Gp6Q`,at:`5:44`}}],diagrams:[{id:`double-dispatch-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Double dispatch on the remove-line-item path`,caption:"The guard travels with the mutation: the handler makes one call, the aggregate calls back into the repository with `this`.",mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["DELETE /orders/123/items/456"] --> B["RemoveLineItemCommandHandler"]
    B --> C["OrderRepository.GetOrderWithLineItem(orderId, lineItemId)"]
    C --> D{"Order is null?"}
    D -- "yes" --> E["Return — nothing to do"]
    D -- "no" --> F["Order.RemoveLineItem(lineItemId, IOrderRepository)"]
    F --> G["orderRepository.HasOneLineItem(this)"]
    G -- "true — last line item" --> H["Return — invariant blocks removal"]
    G -- "false — more remain" --> I["Remove line item from collection"]
    I --> J["Raise LineItemRemoved domain event"]
    J --> K["UnitOfWork.SaveChangesAsync()"]`},{id:`double-dispatch-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Sequence: one handler call, two dispatches`,caption:"First dispatch picks the aggregate method; second dispatch passes `this` back into the repository so the guard runs where the mutation runs.",mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as API
    participant H as RemoveLineItem Handler
    participant R as OrderRepository
    participant O as Order Aggregate
    participant U as UnitOfWork

    API->>H: RemoveLineItemCommand(orderId, lineItemId)
    H->>R: GetOrderWithLineItem(orderId, lineItemId)
    R-->>H: Order + only the target line item
    H->>O: RemoveLineItem(lineItemId, R)
    note over O,R: Double dispatch — Order calls back with this
    O->>R: HasOneLineItem(this)
    R-->>O: false — order has more items
    O->>O: Remove line item from collection
    O-->>H: LineItemRemoved domain event
    H->>U: SaveChangesAsync()
    U-->>API: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Two ordered calls in a handler is one bug waiting for a second caller — collapse 'check then act' into a single aggregate method and you go from N call sites to remember down to 1.`,"One double dispatch per invariant: pass the repository interface into the aggregate method and call it with `this` — the guard becomes unskippable at every current and future call site.",`Read 1 row, not N: fetch only the line item being removed and let a COUNT query inside the repository answer 'is this the last one?' — a 200-item order costs 2 queries, not 200 materialized entities.`,`Guard and mutation must share a method body — if the check lives in a different class than the action, log it as a coupling defect, not a style preference.`,`Raise the domain event (LineItemRemoved) inside the aggregate at mutation time, so handlers stay precondition-free and side effects never spawn new 'remember to call X first' rules.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Use This Pattern To Design a Rich Domain Model With DDD`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wi_wsw5Gp6Q`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-AVBAAKa84cs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:11,title:`The INSANE Performance Boost Of CQRS Read Models`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 8:17`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`CQRS`],story:{headline:`The orders dashboard that died at 3 AM — and the one flat table that resurrected it`,paragraphs:[`It's 15:10 during a database replica failover, the night before our biggest promotional launch. The on-call Slack channel lights up: the operations dashboard's order summary endpoint has a p95 of 3.8 seconds and database CPU is pegged at 95%. Support can't pull up a single order. Marketing is drafting a 'please hold' email. I open the query plan and there it is, staring back at me like a confession: every single summary request is joining orders to line items and computing SUM(total) at request time — thousands of times per minute, re-deriving an answer that never changes after checkout.`,`Our first instinct was the classic one: throw Redis at it. Cache the computed summary, invalidate on every line-item change. Two hours later we had three invalidation bugs, one stale-total incident, and a cache that was wrong in ways we couldn't even reproduce. The cache wasn't the problem — the shape of the data was. We were asking the write model, a schema optimized for transactions and normalization, to answer a read question it was never built for.`,`So we did the thing we should have done on day one: we added an OrderSummary read model. One flat table — Id, CustomerId, TotalPrice — maintained at write time, served with a single indexed lookup. The endpoint went from 3.8 seconds p95 to single-digit milliseconds, and the dashboard came back from the dead before the sun rose.`],problem:`Every read of an order summary re-derived TotalPrice by joining orders to line items at request time — the read path was paying the write model's tax on every single request, at exactly the moment traffic peaked.`},tech:{stack:[`CQRS`,`EF Core`,`MediatR`,`Read Model`,`Domain Events`,`DbContext`],note:`CQRS splits the order domain into a normalized write model (EF Core entities driven by MediatR command handlers) and a denormalized OrderSummary read model, projected either inline in the same SaveChanges or via an OrderCreated domain event, then served straight through a MediatR query handler.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the join tax compounds`,body:[`Let's start where the pain starts. You have an orders page, and it needs to show: order ID, customer, total price. Sounds trivial. But in a normalized write model, the total price doesn't live anywhere — it's the sum of line items. So every request does: fetch the order, join line items, aggregate, map. That's a computation you're renting per request, forever.`,`Now ask the 'why not X?' question: why not cache it? Because the cache's invalidation key is 'any line item changed,' which means your cache is only as correct as your most disciplined code path. Why not add a TotalPrice column to the order entity? You're close — but now you've smuggled read optimization into your write model, and every command has to remember to maintain it. The honest answer is to admit this is a different model for a different job.`,`That's the whole CQRS pitch in one sentence: the write model is optimized for correctness of state transitions, the read model is optimized for the shape of the question. The order summary read model prepares the answer ahead of time — at write time — so the read path just queries by ID. No join. No sum. No drama.`],quote:`Every JOIN you run at read time is a computation you already had the answer to at write time.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Write-model query (JOIN + SUM)`,`Always fresh, single source of truth`,`Join + aggregate per request; p95 grows with line items`,`Low traffic, ad-hoc reports`],[`Cache computed summary`,`Fast reads, no schema change`,`Invalidation on every line-item change; stale-risk`,`Hot keys with tolerant staleness`],[`OrderSummary read model`,`One indexed seek; totals precomputed`,`Extra table + projection to maintain`,`High-traffic UI, dashboards`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The INSANE Performance Boost Of CQRS Read Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AVBAAKa84cs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Designing OrderSummary: dumb on purpose`,body:[`Here's the read model, and I want you to notice how aggressively boring it is. It's a C# record: an identifier, the customer ID, and TotalPrice as a decimal. No behavior. No methods. No domain logic. Records give us immutability and value semantics for free, and the read side wants exactly that — data that gets replaced, never mutated.`,`I'll even rename OrderId to just Id. Why? Because the type is called OrderSummary — the context is the name. Read models should shed every gram of ceremony that exists to serve the write side. This isn't laziness; it's alignment. Every field in this record maps one-to-one to something a screen actually renders.`,`Now wire it into persistence: expose an OrderSummaries DbSet on the application DbContext interface, add a migration, done — it's just a table. The temptation to resist here is enrichment. The moment you add a method like CalculateDiscount() to a read model, you've built a write model in disguise and you're back to paying computation costs on the read path.`],quote:`A read model isn't a smaller entity — it's a pre-computed answer.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The INSANE Performance Boost Of CQRS Read Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AVBAAKa84cs`,at:`1:39`}},{heading:`03 · Walking the boundary: when does the projection get built?`,body:[`This is the decision that actually matters, and there are exactly two honest answers. Option A: build the read model inside the command handler. In CreateOrderHandler, right after adding the order, you also add an OrderSummary with TotalPrice set to zero — it's a brand-new order with no line items. Then one SaveChanges persists both. Same transaction, same commit: the moment the write lands, the read model exists. Immediately consistent.`,`Option B: publish an OrderCreated domain event and let a handler subscribe to it. That handler either queries the latest state of the order and builds the summary, or — and this is the more scalable variant — the event carries enough information to compute the new read model state without going back to the source. The write side stays completely clean; it doesn't know read models exist.`,`So which do I pick? If the UI needs read-your-writes — user creates an order and is immediately redirected to its summary — inline projection, no debate. If you're going to have five projections off the same events, or the write team and read team ship on different cadences, events win. You're not choosing a technology; you're choosing who is allowed to be briefly wrong.`],quote:`Same SaveChanges buys read-your-writes; events buy decoupling — pick based on how stale the screen may be.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inline in command handler`,`Immediately consistent; same transaction; trivial to reason about`,`Write side coupled to read shape; handler grows with every read need`,`Single projection, read-your-writes UIs`],[`OrderCreated event handler`,`Decoupled; write side stays pure; many projections per event`,`Eventual staleness; event must carry enough data or re-query state`,`Multiple projections, separate teams`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The INSANE Performance Boost Of CQRS Read Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AVBAAKa84cs`,at:`2:13`}},{heading:`04 · Exposing it: the query side should be boring`,body:[`Now the fun part — and by fun I mean anticlimactic. We define a GetOrderSummaryQuery record taking just the order ID, implementing MediatR's IRequest<OrderSummary>. Notice what's missing: a response DTO. Because the read model is already shaped for the API, we return it directly. No mapping layer, no assembly of view models. The read model is the response.`,`The handler is a constructor injection of IApplicationDbContext and one line of body: await context.OrderSummaries.FirstOrDefaultAsync(o => o.Id == id). And here's a detail people sleep on: FirstOrDefaultAsync over SingleOrDefaultAsync. FirstOrDefault stops at the first match; SingleOrDefault has to keep checking that no second row exists. On a keyed read model that difference is tiny, but the habit matters — the query side should never do more work than the question requires.`,`Compare this handler to the old path: fetch order, include line items, sum, map. The new handler has zero business logic, zero aggregation, zero branching. That's not an accident — that's the design working. All the intelligence moved to write time, where it runs once per change instead of once per request.`],quote:`If your query handler has logic in it, the projection is missing something.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The INSANE Performance Boost Of CQRS Read Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AVBAAKa84cs`,at:`3:36`}},{heading:`05 · What breaks first: staleness, sharing, and rebuilds`,body:[`Let's be honest about the failure modes, because every architecture talk that skips this is selling something. First: staleness. With event-driven projection, a summary can lag the write by the lifetime of a message in flight. Usually that's milliseconds; during a broker hiccup it's seconds. If your business can't tolerate that for totals, project inline — that's the whole decision from block 03, restated as a risk question.`,`Second, and this one bites teams hardest: read model sharing. Someone decides the invoice page can also use OrderSummary. Six weeks later the invoice team wants a field the orders team doesn't, and now one table serves two masters and neither migration is safe. The rule is one read model per query shape. They're cheap — a record and a DbSet — so make as many as you have distinct questions.`,`Third: drift. Projections are derived state, which means they can silently diverge from the source of truth. The saving grace is that because they're derived, they're disposable. Drop the table, replay the events or re-project from the write model, and you have a fresh, correct read model. Treat projections like caches with schemas: rebuildable, never precious.`],quote:`Read models are disposable projections, not precious data — design every one so you could drop and rebuild it tomorrow.`,table:{headers:[`Failure mode`,`Symptom`,`Fix`],rows:[[`Stale summary`,`Total lags behind the latest line item`,`Inline projection or synchronous event handling`],[`Shared read model`,`Two screens fight over one table shape`,`One projection per query shape`],[`Projection drift`,`Summary silently diverges from write model`,`Rebuild from events + periodic reconciliation`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The INSANE Performance Boost Of CQRS Read Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AVBAAKa84cs`,at:`4:48`}}],diagrams:[{id:`cqrs-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`CQRS read model topology with both projection strategies`,caption:`The write side owns state transitions; the read side owns the question. OrderSummary is built either in the same SaveChanges (Option A) or by an OrderCreated projection handler (Option B).`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    API["API client"] --> CMD["CreateOrderCommand"]
    API --> QRY["GetOrderSummaryQuery"]
    subgraph W["Write side"]
        CMD --> H["CreateOrderHandler"]
        H --> WDB[("Orders write model")]
        H -->|"Option A: same SaveChanges"| RS[("OrderSummaries read table")]
        H --> EVT["OrderCreated event"]
        EVT -->|"Option B: projection handler"| RS
    end
    subgraph R["Read side"]
        QRY --> QH["GetOrderSummaryHandler"]
        QH -->|"FirstOrDefaultAsync by Id"| RS
    end`},{id:`summary-request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Order summary request: write-model query vs read-model lookup`,caption:`The old path joins and aggregates per request; the new path is a single indexed seek against a precomputed row.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant H as Handler
    participant DB as Database
    Note over C,DB: Old path - write model query
    C->>H: GET order summary
    H->>DB: SELECT orders JOIN line_items
    DB-->>H: order row + N line item rows
    H->>H: SUM totals at request time
    H-->>C: 200 OK after 3.8s p95
    Note over C,DB: New path - OrderSummary read model
    C->>H: GET order summary
    H->>DB: FirstOrDefaultAsync on OrderSummaries by Id
    DB-->>H: precomputed summary row
    H-->>C: 200 OK in single-digit ms`}],takeaways:[`Any read that needs a JOIN or a SUM gets a read model: one flat table turns a 3-table join plus aggregation into a single indexed seek by key.`,`Keep read models as behavior-free records — the moment you add a method to one, you've smuggled a write model onto the read path.`,`Project inside the command handler (same SaveChanges) when the UI needs read-your-writes; use OrderCreated-style events when staleness of a few hundred milliseconds is acceptable.`,`Query handlers should be one await: FirstOrDefaultAsync on the read model's key targets single-digit-millisecond p95, and it beats SingleOrDefaultAsync because it stops at the first match instead of verifying uniqueness.`,`One read model per query shape — never share OrderSummary across screens, or every UI tweak becomes a cross-team migration.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — The INSANE Performance Boost Of CQRS Read Models`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AVBAAKa84cs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`event-sourcing-cqrs`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-cCnZJE0wEjY`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:12,title:`How Incomplete DDD Aggregates Can Improve Application Performance`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 8:17`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`DDD`],story:{headline:`The 200,000-Line-Item Order That Took Down Order Management at 08:45 as mobile traffic surged`,paragraphs:[`It's 13:20 right after the schema migration and the on-call phone is buzzing: the order-management API is timing out, and every retry makes it worse. The culprit, once someone finally gets a trace open, is almost embarrassing — a 'remove line item' request against a wholesale customer's order. That order had grown, line by line, over three years, to hundreds of thousands of line items.`,`The handler was textbook DDD. Load the Order aggregate, Include the LineItems collection, call order.RemoveLineItem(id), SaveChangesAsync. Beautiful invariants, clean entity method — and it materialized every single line item into the change tracker just to delete one. On a sibling endpoint doing the same pattern with two included collections, EF Core simply threw: Cartesian explosion.`,`The fix took one line: a filtered include that fetched the order plus exactly the one line item being removed. The aggregate arrived intentionally incomplete, the invariant still lived inside Order.RemoveLineItem, and the endpoint went from a forty-second memory grind to roughly thirty milliseconds.`],problem:`The handler loads the entire Order aggregate — every one of its line items — into memory just to delete a single row, because 'always load the full aggregate' was followed literally.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`MediatR`,`C# Records`,`DDD Aggregates`,`Filtered Include`,`AsSplitQuery`],note:`EF Core loads and tracks the Order aggregate while MediatR dispatches the RemoveLineItem command to its handler. Filtered includes and split queries are the two EF levers that keep huge child collections from ever being materialized.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does deleting one row cost 200,000 reads?`,body:[`So let's start with the version every DDD tutorial teaches you. We've got a RemoveLineItemCommand — just a record with an OrderId and a LineItemId — and a handler injecting the application DbContext. The textbook move: load the Order with Include(o => o.LineItems), call order.RemoveLineItem(lineItemId), SaveChangesAsync, done. It's correct. The invariant lives inside the entity. I love it — for an order with twelve line items.`,`But here's the thing nobody says out loud: the aggregate rule says 'load the whole aggregate to enforce consistency,' and we've quietly reinterpreted that as 'materialize every row in every collection.' On a wholesale e-commerce order with tens or hundreds of thousands of line items, that's a full read of every child row into the change tracker to delete one. Memory spikes, the tracker chews on 100k+ objects, and your p99 goes vertical.`,`So the question I want you sitting with: does removing one line item actually need its 199,999 siblings in memory? Spoiler — no. But we can't just skip the aggregate either, because then where do the invariants live? That tension is the whole talk.`],quote:`An aggregate is a consistency boundary, not a memory boundary.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Full aggregate load`,`Pure DDD, invariants enforced`,`Loads all N children, memory spike`,`Small collections only`],[`AsSplitQuery`,`No join blowup, lean separate queries`,`Still loads every child row`,`Multiple collections, moderate N`],[`Filtered include`,`Loads 1 order + 1 item, O(1)`,`Aggregate incomplete in memory`,`Huge collections, single-child ops`],[`ExecuteDelete`,`Fastest, one SQL statement`,`Skips invariants and domain events`,`Bulk jobs with no domain logic`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How Incomplete DDD Aggregates Can Improve Application Performance`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCnZJE0wEjY`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: Cartesian explosion and split queries`,body:[`Before we fix it, let's make the failure mode worse — because you will hit this. The moment your aggregate has two collections — say LineItems and ShipmentEvents — and you Include both in one query, EF generates a single JOINed SELECT. The row count is the product: 100k line items times 10k events is a billion-row join. EF Core detects the Cartesian explosion and throws instead of melting your database — consider that a mercy.`,`The first tool EF hands you is AsSplitQuery. Append it and EF stops joining: one SELECT for the order, a second for the line items. Row count drops from N × M to N + M. And there's a sneaky optimization in there I really like — if the root query returns null, the child queries never run at all. You don't pay for children of an order that doesn't exist.`,`But notice what split queries don't fix: we're still pulling every line item. It's a better-shaped O(N), and O(N) with N at 200,000 is still a bad Tuesday. We need to shrink N itself.`],quote:`Cartesian explosion is one SELECT trying to do the job of two.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`SQL shape`,`Row count`,`Behavior`],rows:[[`Single query + Include`,`1 JOINed SELECT`,`N × M (Cartesian product)`,`EF throws past explosion limits`],[`AsSplitQuery`,`2+ separate SELECTs`,`N + M`,`Child queries skipped if root is null`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How Incomplete DDD Aggregates Can Improve Application Performance`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCnZJE0wEjY`,at:`0:52`}},{heading:`03 · The filtered include: loading an intentionally incomplete aggregate`,body:[`Here's the move that actually ends the pain: the filtered include. Inside the Include, you can write a Where: Include(o => o.LineItems.Where(li => li.Id == command.LineItemId)). Now the SQL itself only fetches the one line item we're about to remove. One order object, one line item object, in memory. That's it.`,`And yes — I can hear the purists. 'That's not a complete aggregate!' Correct. It's an intentionally incomplete aggregate. The entity method Order.RemoveLineItem still runs, the null-check still runs, the invariant still runs. What we dropped is 199,999 rows whose only job was to be materialized and ignored.`,`The mental shift: consistency is about the invariants you enforce, not the rows you hold. If this use case only ever touches one line item, load one line item. Different use case, different filter, same aggregate.`],quote:`Load the slice of the aggregate the use case actually touches — nothing more.`,table:{headers:[`You gain`,`You give up`,`Mitigation`],rows:[[`O(1) child load per request`,`Full in-memory view of the collection`,`Scope invariants to the operation`],[`Tiny change-tracker footprint`,`Sibling-side domain events`,`Emit events explicitly in the handler`],[`Flat, predictable p99`,`Concurrency checks on untouched siblings`,`Rowversion only on touched rows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How Incomplete DDD Aggregates Can Improve Application Performance`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCnZJE0wEjY`,at:`4:52`}},{heading:`04 · Why not just ExecuteDelete? Picking the trade you can live with`,body:[`Now the inevitable question from the back row: why not skip EF entirely and run ExecuteDelete — one SQL statement, done? Fastest option on the board, and for bulk cleanup jobs it's the right call. But look at what you just deleted along with the row: the entity's invariants, domain events, optimistic concurrency — the whole reason we picked DDD in the first place.`,`The incomplete aggregate is the sweet spot precisely because it keeps the domain model in the loop. The handler stays thin, the entity method stays the owner of the rule, and EF's change tracker still generates the DELETE for us. We paid one include filter; we kept the entire design.`,`So my decision rule: bulk operation with no domain logic? ExecuteDelete. Single-child operation on a huge collection? Filtered include. Multiple collections at moderate sizes? Split query. Tiny collections? Load the whole aggregate and stop worrying — purity is affordable at small N.`],quote:`Every shortcut past the aggregate costs you an invariant — pay only where the use case allows.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Domain safety`,`Performance`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Incomplete aggregate`,`Entity method still guards invariants`,`1 order + 1 item loaded`,`Sweet spot for single-child ops`],[`ExecuteDelete`,`None — bypasses the model`,`1 statement, fastest`,`Bulk only, no domain logic`],[`Stored procedure`,`Logic hidden from the model`,`Fast`,`Last resort`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How Incomplete DDD Aggregates Can Improve Application Performance`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCnZJE0wEjY`,at:`6:01`}}],diagrams:[{id:`load-strategies`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`RemoveLineItem: naive load vs filtered include`,caption:`Same command, same invariant — wildly different data volume depending on how you shape the EF query.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    A["RemoveLineItemCommand<br/>OrderId + LineItemId"] --> B["MediatR<br/>IRequestHandler"]
    B --> C{"Load strategy?"}
    C -->|"Naive: Include all"| D["SELECT order JOIN<br/>ALL N line items"]
    C -->|"Filtered include"| E["SELECT order +<br/>1 matching line item"]
    D --> F["Materialize N rows<br/>100k+ tracked objects"]
    E --> G["Materialize 2 objects"]
    F --> H["Order.RemoveLineItem"]
    G --> H
    H --> I["SaveChangesAsync<br/>DELETE 1 row"]`},{id:`request-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path with a filtered include`,caption:`Two lean queries, a null short-circuit, and the invariant enforced inside the entity before a single-row DELETE.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant M as MediatR
    participant H as RemoveLineItemHandler
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant DB as SQL Database
    C->>M: RemoveLineItemCommand(OrderId, LineItemId)
    M->>H: Handle(command, ct)
    H->>EF: Orders.Include(LineItems.Where(li.Id = LineItemId))
    EF->>DB: Query 1: SELECT Order WHERE OrderId = @id
    DB-->>EF: 1 row (null short-circuits the rest)
    EF->>DB: Query 2: SELECT LineItem WHERE OrderId = @id AND Id = @itemId
    DB-->>EF: 1 row
    EF-->>H: Incomplete aggregate: Order + 1 LineItem
    H->>H: order.RemoveLineItem(lineItemId)
    H->>EF: SaveChangesAsync()
    EF->>DB: DELETE FROM LineItem WHERE Id = @itemId`}],takeaways:[`If a collection inside an aggregate can exceed ~1,000 rows, never Include it unfiltered — that's the threshold where materialization starts dominating latency.`,`Filtered include turns O(N) into O(1): one order + one line item in memory, whether the order has 12 or 200,000 line items.`,`Two included collections multiply rows (N × M); switch to AsSplitQuery so row count adds (N + M) instead — before EF's Cartesian-explosion guard throws.`,`Split queries short-circuit: if the root query returns null, the child queries never execute — missing-aggregate safety for free.`,`Keep the invariant inside the entity method (Order.RemoveLineItem) even when the aggregate is incomplete — that's what lets you trade rows without trading correctness.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How Incomplete DDD Aggregates Can Improve Application Performance`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCnZJE0wEjY`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-IlXnIe6p_Uk`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:13,title:`Mapping Domain-Driven Design Concepts To The Database With EF Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:07`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`DDD`],story:{headline:`17:00 as cross-region traffic shifted: the checkout saved orders with zero line items`,paragraphs:[`Launch night for the new checkout service. The team had done DDD properly for once — an Order aggregate root, a private read-only line-items collection, strongly typed OrderId and CustomerId, a Money value object for price. Domain tests green, demo done, everyone heading for bed. Then the first real order hit staging and SaveChanges detonated: 'No relational type mapping was found for type OrderId.' EF Core had never been told what an OrderId is — and at 12:05 during a sudden traffic spike, neither had anyone on the pager rotation.`,`A hotfix went out with a 'temporary' Guid property slapped onto the entity. Orders started saving — and every single one arrived with zero line items, because EF was materializing into the public property while the aggregate's truth lived in a private backing field nobody had mapped. Twenty minutes later a support ticket landed: two customers, same email address, because the unique index existed in someone's head but never in a migration file.`,`The postmortem was short and humiliating: nothing was wrong with the domain model. What was missing was a deliberate mapping layer — three IEntityTypeConfiguration classes with value conversions, an owned Money, a backing-field-mapped collection, and one unique index. The rich model didn't fail loudly. It failed silently at the persistence boundary, which is the only way these failures ever happen.`],problem:`How do you map aggregates, strongly typed IDs, encapsulated collections, and value objects to relational tables with EF Core — without flattening the domain or letting the database leak back into it?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 7`,`Postgres`,`DDD`,`Strongly-Typed IDs`,`Value Objects`,`OwnsOne`,`HasConversion`,`IEntityTypeConfiguration`,`Backing Fields`,`Migrations`],note:`The persistence boundary of a rich domain model: per-entity IEntityTypeConfiguration classes teach EF Core to translate strongly typed IDs, owned value objects, and private collections into Postgres tables — and back.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why a rich model refuses to save`,body:[`Here's where every DDD-meets-the-database conversation has to start: the impedance mismatch. DDD hands you aggregates, entities, and value objects — things with identity, behavior, and encapsulation. A relational database wants scalars, rows, and foreign keys. EF Core sits in the middle, and its conventions quietly assume you modeled everything as public properties with primitive types.`,`So the naive move is to flatten: make the IDs raw Guids, make the collections public Lists, split Money into a decimal and a string on the entity. It works — right up until someone passes a ProductId where an OrderId belongs, or calls list.Add() and skips every invariant you wrote. You've traded a mapping problem for a domain-integrity problem, and the second one is far more expensive.`,`The position I've landed on after doing this a few times: keep the model rich, and pay for it with an explicit, deliberate mapping layer. One configuration class per entity. That's the whole trick — the rest of this talk is just what goes inside those classes.`],quote:`The database doesn't get a vote inside your domain model — but it gets every vote at the persistence boundary.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Raw Guid IDs everywhere`,`Zero config, conventions just work`,`Cross-entity ID mix-ups compile fine, domain leaks`,`Throwaway prototypes`],[`Strongly-typed IDs + HasConversion`,`Type-safe, self-documenting`,`One converter per ID type`,`Any real domain model`],[`Strings for everything`,`Maximum flexibility`,`No integrity, parsing scattered everywhere`,`Never`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mapping Domain-Driven Design Concepts To The Database With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IlXnIe6p_Uk`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: teaching EF your strongly typed IDs`,body:[`Let's walk the boundary starting with the simplest entity — Customer — because it already contains the first landmine: a strongly typed ID. CustomerId wraps a value, and the moment you call HasKey(x => x.Id), EF Core throws its hands up: it has no idea how to store an object in a scalar column. That exception is guaranteed — it's not a maybe.`,`The fix is HasConversion with two lambdas: to-provider, id => id.Value, and from-provider, value => new CustomerId(value). Five lines, and the strongly typed ID becomes a plain column. Write both directions — I've watched people write only the outbound lambda and get a runtime surprise on the very first query. And while you're in the configuration class, this is where the database constraints live: HasMaxLength(100) on name, 255 on email, and HasIndex(e => e.Email).IsUnique() so two customers can never share an inbox.`,`Why not just use raw Guids and skip all this? Because the entire point of the strongly typed ID is that CustomerId and OrderId are different types — the compiler now enforces what a comment used to merely suggest. The converter is the toll you pay at the boundary, and it's cheap.`],quote:`A value converter is two lambdas — or one 3 AM exception. Write both.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Implicit conversion operators only`,`Less code to write`,`EF can't see it, still throws at runtime`,`No`],[`HasConversion to/from primitive`,`Explicit, testable, ~5 lines per ID`,`One converter per ID type`,`Yes, always`],[`Shared ValueConverter class`,`DRY across many ID types`,`Slight indirection`,`Large models`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mapping Domain-Driven Design Concepts To The Database With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IlXnIe6p_Uk`,at:`2:37`}},{heading:`03 · Owning the value objects: OwnsOne and the Money problem`,body:[`Next level up: value objects with more than one property. Product carries a Price — a Money value object with an amount and a currency. Money has no identity of its own, so giving it a table and a key would be modeling fiction. EF Core 7 gives us OwnsOne, which says: this object belongs to the product, materialize it as columns on the owner's table.`,`The owned navigation builder behaves a lot like a regular entity builder, so you keep configuring: currency gets HasMaxLength(3) because we only ever store ISO-4217 codes — 'USD', 'EUR' — and there's no reason to let a fifty-character junk string into that column. Why not a separate table for Money? You'd add a join for data with no identity. Why not a JSON column? You give up constraints and cheap indexing in a relational store. Table splitting is the boring, correct answer for most value objects.`,`One more thing worth saying out loud: owned types are nullability-sensitive. If Price is required, say so — you want the guarantee that a null never reaches the database, not a nullable amount column and a prayer.`],quote:`A value object has no identity, so it earns no table — only columns.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`OwnsOne (table splitting)`,`No joins, simple queries`,`Columns land on the owner's table`,`Most value objects`],[`OwnsMany / separate table`,`Handles collections of VOs`,`Joins plus extra key plumbing`,`Address lists, multi-currency`],[`JSON column`,`Schema-flexible`,`Weaker constraints and indexing`,`Truly dynamic shapes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mapping Domain-Driven Design Concepts To The Database With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IlXnIe6p_Uk`,at:`7:48`}},{heading:`04 · Encapsulation vs persistence: private collections and backing fields`,body:[`Now the part that actually bit us at 3 AM: encapsulated collections. The Order keeps its line items in a private read-only field — that's the whole point of an aggregate root; you go through order.AddLineItem(), not list.Add(). But EF Core needs to configure the relationship and hydrate the collection, and it can't do either against a field it doesn't know about.`,`The pattern: expose a property over the private field — just enough surface for configuration — and let EF Core map that property to its backing field, so materialization writes into the field where your invariants live. The relationship itself is HasMany(o => o.LineItems).WithOne().HasForeignKey(li => li.OrderId): one order, many line items, foreign key on the child side. Same story for LineItem → Product: HasOne, with the ProductId foreign key on the line item.`,`Why not just make the collection public? Because the moment you do, every service in the codebase can mutate your aggregate behind its back, and your invariants become suggestions. The backing field is a few extra lines of configuration to keep the truth inside the aggregate — the cheapest insurance in this whole talk.`],quote:`Expose the property for configuration — keep the field for truth.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Public List<T>`,`EF auto-discovers it`,`Anyone mutates, invariants bypassed`,`Demos only`],[`Private field + property`,`Invariants enforced, EF hydrates field`,`Must wire the backing field`,`Aggregates`],[`Read-only IEnumerable + methods`,`Cleanest public API`,`Slightly more configuration`,`Mature domains`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mapping Domain-Driven Design Concepts To The Database With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IlXnIe6p_Uk`,at:`13:00`}},{heading:`05 · Trust but verify: reading the migration like a reviewer`,body:[`Last block, and it's the one I'd make mandatory on every team: read the migration. Add-Migration from the Package Manager Console, then open the generated file like a code reviewer — not like it's machine output you're allowed to ignore. It is the most honest artifact your model produces.`,`What am I scanning for? Customers table first, then products, orders, line_items. Every strongly typed ID should have collapsed into a plain guid column — if you see an OrderId type anywhere in there, a converter is missing. Money should show up as Price_Amount and Price_Currency with a length of 3. The unique index on email must be sitting right there in the CreateTable — because the database will not infer it, and the duplicate-email support ticket is how you find out.`,`Ten minutes of reading the migration beats one 14:00 during a blue-green cutover redeploy. The generated file is where your domain decisions either made it across the boundary or silently didn't — and silent is the dangerous one.`],quote:`The migration file is your model's honesty test — read it before the database reads it for you.`,table:{headers:[`Artifact`,`What good looks like`,`Failure mode`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Strongly-typed ID`,`Plain guid/uuid column`,`Runtime mapping exception`,`Pre-merge`],[`Owned Money`,`price_amount + price_currency(3)`,`Unbounded currency junk data`,`Pre-merge`],[`Customer email`,`Unique index in CreateTable`,`Duplicate accounts in prod`,`Pre-merge`],[`Line items`,`order_id FK on the child side`,`Orphaned line items`,`Pre-merge`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`mapping-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`From rich model to relational tables`,caption:`Every domain concept crosses the boundary through a configuration class — nothing reaches Postgres by convention.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph DOM["Rich domain model"]
        ORD["Order aggregate<br/>OrderId · CustomerId<br/>private _lineItems"]
        LI["LineItem<br/>LineItemId · ProductId"]
        MON["Money value object<br/>amount · currency"]
        CUS["Customer<br/>CustomerId"]
    end
    subgraph MAP["EF Core mapping layer"]
        CFG["IEntityTypeConfiguration per entity<br/>HasKey · HasConversion<br/>OwnsOne · HasMany · HasIndex"]
        MB["DbContext.OnModelCreating<br/>applies every configuration"]
    end
    subgraph PG["Postgres"]
        TCUS[("customers<br/>id · name · email UNIQUE")]
        TORD[("orders<br/>id · customer_id FK")]
        TLI[("line_items<br/>id · order_id FK · product_id FK<br/>price_amount · price_currency 3")]
    end
    ORD --> CFG
    LI --> CFG
    MON --> CFG
    CUS --> CFG
    CFG --> MB
    MB --> TCUS
    MB --> TORD
    MB --> TLI`},{id:`save-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Write and read path for one aggregate`,caption:`Converters run on the way out and in reverse on the way back; hydration lands in the backing field, not the public property.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant App as Application layer
    participant Agg as Order aggregate
    participant Ctx as DbContext
    participant Conv as Value converters
    participant PG as Postgres

    App->>Agg: order.AddLineItem(product, qty)
    Agg->>Agg: enforce invariants on private _lineItems
    App->>Ctx: SaveChangesAsync
    Ctx->>Conv: OrderId → Guid · Money → amount + currency
    Conv-->>Ctx: primitive column values
    Ctx->>PG: INSERT orders, line_items
    PG-->>Ctx: committed
    Note over Ctx,PG: Read path runs every converter in reverse<br/>Guid → OrderId · columns → Money
    Ctx-->>App: aggregate hydrated through the backing field`}],takeaways:[`Budget five lines per strongly typed ID: HasKey plus HasConversion with both lambdas — id => id.Value out, value => new CustomerId(value) back. Miss one direction and SaveChanges throws at runtime, not compile time.`,`Cap columns where the domain caps them: name ≤ 100, email ≤ 255, currency = 3 chars (ISO-4217). If the column is longer than the rule, the rule eventually loses.`,`One HasIndex(e => e.Email).IsUnique() per natural key — the cheapest duplicate-account incident you will never have to clean up.`,`Map aggregate collections as a private read-only field plus one property, wired with HasMany/WithOne/HasForeignKey on the child side — EF hydrates the backing field and the invariants stay inside the root.`,`Read every migration before applying it: owned Money should appear as Price_Amount/Price_Currency(3), FKs on the child side, unique indexes present — 10 minutes of review beats one 11:45 when batch reconciliation started redeploy.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mapping Domain-Driven Design Concepts To The Database With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IlXnIe6p_Uk`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj--_AKTzDrYVc`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:14,title:`Using Multiple EF Core DbContexts in a Single Application`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 17:46`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The 3 AM Migration That Welded Two Modules Together`,paragraphs:[`Picture the setup: a modular monolith with an Orders module and a Products module, each with its own DbContext, both pointed at the same SQL Server database. Green builds, happy standup. Then a well-meaning PR lands with a one-line 'improvement' — a Product navigation property on LineItem, because EF makes it so easy.`,`EF Core happily scaffolds a foreign key from the orders schema's LineItems table across to the products schema's Products table. In dev, where everything deploys in one tidy sequence, it works. On the staging deploy, the Products module's migration runs into its own schema on its own schedule, the Orders migration tries to build a cross-schema FK against a table that isn't there yet — and the pipeline is on fire at 3 AM. Two modules that were supposed to be strangers were now structurally married.`,`The fix wasn't clever tooling. It was deleting the navigation, storing ProductId plus the price at order time as plain columns, and drawing one line in the sand: the module boundary is a hard line, and EF's convenience features don't get to cross it.`],problem:`How do you give each module its own model, migrations, and transaction scope — without EF's navigation properties quietly welding the modules back together behind your back?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`SQL Server`,`EF Migrations`,`DbContextOptions<T>`,`NoTracking Reads`,`Modular Monolith`],note:`One DbContext per module, each registered independently with typed DbContextOptions and its own migration set; cross-module references collapse to an ID plus a snapshot column, and the same pattern stretches to separate providers or no-tracking read replicas.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why one big DbContext feels easier`,body:[`Let's be honest about the path of least resistance: one DbContext, one model, one migration history. Every entity is one navigation away, the change tracker stitches the whole graph together, and SaveChanges just... works. When you've only got two entities, splitting feels like ceremony for ceremony's sake.`,`But watch what that single model buys you, and what it costs. The model snapshot is global — every module's entities live in one file, so every module's PR touches it and every merge conflict lands in the same place. SaveChanges becomes a transaction that can span module boundaries, meaning the Orders module can commit half-finished Products data whether it meant to or not. And nothing stops Orders from doing a LINQ join straight into the Products tables — compile-time convenience, runtime coupling.`,`So the question isn't 'can one context work?' It can. The question is: what boundary do you actually want, and is a single model quietly erasing it?`],quote:`A shared DbContext is a shared bank account — everyone spends, nobody audits.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single DbContext`,`One migration, easy navigations`,`Global model snapshot, cross-module transactions`,`Tiny apps, prototypes`],[`Context per module`,`Isolated models and migrations`,`IDs instead of navigations, more plumbing`,`Modular monoliths, team-owned modules`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Multiple EF Core DbContexts in a Single Application`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-_AKTzDrYVc`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what actually changes`,body:[`Here's the split itself, and it's almost boring — which is the point. OrdersDbContext derives from DbContext, takes a DbContextOptions<OrdersDbContext> in its constructor and passes it to base. Two DbSets: Orders and LineItems. In OnModelCreating I wire the one-to-many: HasMany(o => o.LineItems).WithOne().HasForeignKey(li => li.OrderId). That's the whole relationship map, and it never leaves the module.`,`The interesting decision is the one I don't make: LineItem gets no Product navigation. It carries ProductId and the price at the time the line was created. That snapshot is deliberate — the product's current price is the Products module's business, but the price you actually charged is the Orders module's history. Two contexts means EF can't join them for you, and that's the feature, not the bug.`,`Same recipe for ProductsDbContext: one DbSet for Products, its own options type, its own OnModelCreating. If a module can't express its model without reaching into a neighbor's DbSet, that's not a modeling problem — that's a boundary problem wearing a modeling costume.`],quote:`Across a module boundary you pass IDs and snapshots, not object graphs.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Inside a module`,`Across modules`],rows:[[`Relationships`,`Navigations + FKs in OnModelCreating`,`Plain ID column, no FK`],[`Data freshness`,`Live related entities`,`Snapshot captured at write time`],[`Transactions`,`One SaveChanges, one transaction`,`Separate commits, eventual consistency`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Multiple EF Core DbContexts in a Single Application`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-_AKTzDrYVc`,at:`0:40`}},{heading:`03 · Wiring it up without surprises: DI and migrations`,body:[`Registration is two lines that look nearly identical, and the difference is everything. AddDbContext<OrdersDbContext>(options => options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("Database"))) — then the same again for ProductsDbContext, same connection string. Because each constructor asks for its own typed DbContextOptions<T>, the container hands each context exactly its own configuration; you can't accidentally inject the products settings into the orders context. Two NuGet packages make this go: Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer for the provider, Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Tools for Package Manager Console migrations.`,`Same connection string means both contexts land in one database and I separate them by schema; swap in a different connection string and they're physically separate databases — the code doesn't change. That's the payoff of keeping the boundary in the context, not in the connection.`,`Migrations are where teams trip. Each context needs its own migration set: dotnet ef migrations add Init_Orders --context OrdersDbContext, with migrations pointed at a per-context folder and each context owning its own MigrationsHistoryTable so the histories don't fight. Run Update-Database without --context in a multi-context app and you're gambling on which model EF picks — don't gamble.`],quote:`Two contexts sharing one connection string is fine; two contexts sharing one migration folder is a 3 AM incident.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Multiple EF Core DbContexts in a Single Application`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-_AKTzDrYVc`,at:`1:10`}},{heading:`04 · But what about just schemas? And the read-replica trick`,body:[`Now the pushback I always get: 'can't I just put each module in its own schema on one context?' You can — ToTable("Orders", "orders") sorts the naming collisions. But it changes nothing about coupling: still one model snapshot, still one SaveChanges spanning everything, still nothing stopping a cross-schema join. A schema separates tables; a separate context separates ownership. If the boundary matters, the context is the enforcement.`,`There are two more reasons this pattern earns its keep. First, different providers: if one module lives on SQL Server and another on PostgreSQL, the provider is baked into the options — UseSqlServer versus UseNpgsql — so a single context physically cannot serve both. Second, read replicas: point a dedicated context at the replica and set UseQueryTrackingBehavior(QueryTrackingBehavior.NoTracking) at registration, or override OnConfiguring and set it on the optionsBuilder there. Every query becomes no-tracking by default — exactly what you want when the database is read-only and the change tracker is pure overhead.`,`Notice all three reasons are the same shape: something about how this data is stored, served, or owned differs — and the context is the seam EF Core gives you to express that difference.`],quote:`Schemas separate tables; separate contexts separate teams.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Multiple EF Core DbContexts in a Single Application`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-_AKTzDrYVc`,at:`4:01`}},{heading:`05 · The three reasons I reach for multiple contexts`,body:[`So, decision time, out loud. I reach for multiple DbContexts for exactly three reasons. One: different databases or providers behind the same application — SQL Server here, PostgreSQL there. Two: a read replica, where a no-tracking-by-default context turns the change tracker off for every single read. Three: the modular monolith — each module gets one context, one schema, one migration history, and the module boundary becomes a compile-time and runtime fact instead of a wiki page.`,`And the inverse rule matters just as much: if none of those apply, don't split. A two-entity app with one team doesn't need two contexts any more than it needs two CI pipelines. But the moment modules matter — separate owners, separate deploy cadence, separate reasons to change — the DbContext is the cheapest hard boundary EF Core will sell you. Buy it before the 3 AM migration, not after.`],quote:`If a module can't own its DbContext, it doesn't own its data.`,table:{headers:[`Reason`,`What changes`,`Cost`],rows:[[`Different providers`,`One context per provider (UseSqlServer vs UseNpgsql)`,`Extra migration set per provider`],[`Read replica`,`NoTracking default via UseQueryTrackingBehavior`,`Second context to register and maintain`],[`Modular monolith`,`Context + schema + migrations per module`,`ID + snapshot instead of navigations`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Multiple EF Core DbContexts in a Single Application`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-_AKTzDrYVc`,at:`9:52`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two Modules, Two Contexts, One Database`,caption:`Each module owns its DbContext and its schema; the only thing crossing the boundary is a ProductId plus a price snapshot — never a foreign key.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Host["ASP.NET Core Host"]
    OM["Orders Module"]
    PM["Products Module"]
  end
  OM --> ODC["OrdersDbContext"]
  PM --> PDC["ProductsDbContext"]
  subgraph DB["SQL Server"]
    subgraph S1["orders schema"]
      OT["Orders table"]
      LT["LineItems table"]
    end
    subgraph S2["products schema"]
      PT["Products table"]
    end
  end
  ODC --> S1
  PDC --> S2
  LT -. "ProductId + price snapshot (no FK)" .-> PT`},{id:`order-request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Placing an Order Across Two Contexts`,caption:`The request reads the product through ProductsDbContext, snapshots the price, then commits the order through OrdersDbContext in its own transaction.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant API as Orders Endpoint
  participant PDB as ProductsDbContext
  participant ODB as OrdersDbContext
  participant DB as SQL Server
  API->>PDB: Load product (name, current price)
  PDB->>DB: SELECT from products.Products
  DB-->>PDB: Product row
  PDB-->>API: Product data
  API->>ODB: Add Order + LineItem (ProductId, price snapshot)
  ODB->>DB: BEGIN TRAN, INSERT orders + line_items, COMMIT
  DB-->>ODB: Committed
  Note over ODB,PDB: Two contexts, two SaveChanges, zero cross-module FKs`}],takeaways:[`One DbContext per module: N modules means N context classes, N migration folders, and N migration history tables — budget the plumbing before you write entities.`,`Zero cross-context navigations or FKs: across a boundary, store the ID plus a snapshot (unit price at order time) — two plain columns beat one coupling property.`,`Always register with the typed generic — AddDbContext<OrdersDbContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(...)) — because DbContextOptions<T> is what stops two contexts from being swapped by accident.`,`Never run a migration command without --context once you have more than one; a shared migration folder across contexts is how staging dies at 3 AM.`,`Read-only context? Set UseQueryTrackingBehavior(QueryTrackingBehavior.NoTracking) so 100% of queries skip the change tracker — the default move for read replicas and reporting endpoints.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Multiple EF Core DbContexts in a Single Application`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-_AKTzDrYVc`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-gsAuFIhXz3g`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:15,title:`Fix Your Controllers By Refactoring To Minimal APIs`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:56`,minutes:15,difficulty:3,tags:[`Azure`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Constructor That Took Down Every Endpoint`,paragraphs:[`It's 10:47 PM the night before the release cut. A developer adds one endpoint — a CSV export — to ProductsController. Harmless, except the export service has to be injected, and the constructor already takes eleven dependencies. Every one of the 23 unit tests for that controller stops compiling, because each fixture now has to construct twelve mocks just to reach a method that reads one table. He fixes the tests, ships, goes to bed.`,`Two weeks later, 3 AM. A new service registration has a missing dependency, and DI validation detonates at startup. Because every endpoint in that controller funnels through the same constructor, the entire /api/products surface is down — including the three GETs that never touched the new service. Rollback, postmortem, and one uncomfortable question on the whiteboard: why does a read-one-row endpoint pay rent on a twelve-dependency constructor?`,`The answer had been sitting in .NET the whole time: stop grouping unrelated endpoints behind one class and map them individually. Minimal APIs, method injection, IResult. One afternoon of refactoring, and the constructor monster was gone.`],problem:`Every endpoint in a controller shares one constructor, so coupling compounds: a dependency added for one endpoint is paid for — in allocations, test setup, and blast radius — by all of them.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`Minimal APIs`,`MediatR`,`IEndpointRouteBuilder`,`Results Factory`,`IResult`],note:`The two HTTP surfaces this refactor moves between: attribute-routed controllers with constructor injection, and Minimal APIs mapped as extension methods on IEndpointRouteBuilder with per-endpoint method injection returning IResult. MediatR's ISender sits in the middle of both.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why controllers rot`,body:[`Controllers have been around since the early days of building APIs in .NET. You implement ControllerBase, slap [ApiController] on the class, [Route] for the path, and every method inside becomes an endpoint — as long as you decorate it with the right HTTP verb attribute. [HttpPost] here, [HttpGet] there. Familiar, comfortable, fine at first.`,`But look at what a controller actually is: a bag of methods that represent completely separate endpoints with pretty much nothing in common. Cohesion is near zero, and it drops as the class grows. Worse, every endpoint shares the same constructor. My sample controller was thin — one service. Real ones? You inject five services to satisfy a class where each endpoint realistically needs one or two, so you're allocating resources the invoked endpoint will never use, on every single request.`,`So the refactor question isn't 'how do I write less code' — it's 'how do I make each endpoint own exactly its own dependencies and nothing else.'`],quote:`A controller isn't a thing — it's five unrelated endpoints wearing one constructor.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Controller per resource`,`Familiar; filters and model binding built in`,`Low cohesion; shared constructor bloat`,`Small CRUD, ≤3 deps`],[`Minimal API per feature`,`Method injection; per-endpoint deps`,`Requires grouping discipline`,`Most endpoints, growing services`],[`Controller + [FromServices]`,`Per-method deps without a rewrite`,`Constructor coupling still exists`,`Incremental triage`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Controllers By Refactoring To Minimal APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gsAuFIhXz3g`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: constructor vs method injection`,body:[`Here's the conversion. Copy the controller into ProductsEndpoints, make the class static, delete ControllerBase, delete the attributes, delete the constructor. Then define a static extension method on IEndpointRouteBuilder — MapProductEndpoints — and inside it, MapPost, MapGet, MapDelete, MapPut, each with the same route the controller had: api/products. Clients never notice.`,`The dependency story flips. Minimal APIs use method injection: the ISender from MediatR becomes a parameter of the handler, right next to the bound request body. And to be fair — controllers support method injection too, via [FromServices], so you could use this pattern regardless. But with a controller the constructor still exists, the class still gets constructed, the coupling still compounds. With a static handler, the parameter list is the whole truth.`,`Why not keep controllers and just sprinkle [FromServices]? Because the win isn't the injection style — it's killing the shared class. One endpoint, one method, exactly the dependencies it uses.`],quote:`Method injection turns the dependency list into an honest, per-endpoint contract.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Constructor injection`,`Method injection`],rows:[[`Resolution scope`,`All endpoints in the class`,`Only the invoked endpoint`],[`Unused allocations`,`Every request`,`Zero`],[`Test setup`,`Construct full class + all mocks`,`Call method with needed args`],[`Cohesion`,`Class-level, decays with size`,`Endpoint-level by construction`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Controllers By Refactoring To Minimal APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gsAuFIhXz3g`,at:`1:45`}},{heading:`03 · The Results factory and the IResult contract`,body:[`Next friction point: ControllerBase gave you Ok(), NotFound(), and friends. Minimal APIs give you the Results class — a static factory for the same shapes: Results.Ok, Results.NotFound, Results.Problem. Your handler returns IResult, which is exactly what the endpoint delegate expects, so the types line up without ceremony.`,`And here's the part that makes the definition clean: once CreateProduct is a public static method that takes its request body and ISender and returns IResult, you can pass it straight in as the request delegate — MapPost("api/products", CreateProduct). Route, binding, handler: three lines, no class in sight.`],quote:`IResult is the contract, Results is the factory — ControllerBase was both, welded to a class you didn't need.`,table:{headers:[`Return helper`,`Comes from`,`Returns`],rows:[[`Ok(...)`,`ControllerBase`,`IActionResult`],[`Results.Ok(...)`,`Results factory`,`IResult`],[`Results.Problem(...)`,`Results factory`,`IResult`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Controllers By Refactoring To Minimal APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gsAuFIhXz3g`,at:`2:54`}},{heading:`04 · Testing: what's actually worth testing`,body:[`A side effect of the refactor: CreateProduct is public on a static class, so a test can call ProductsEndpoints.CreateProduct directly and assert on the IResult it returns. That's real, and it's easy.`,`But here's my honest take: unit testing controllers — or these thin delegates — is mostly pointless. They contain no behavior; they pass the request along to other services. The parts that actually break are routing, binding, DI resolution, middleware — and a unit test on the method sees none of that. Functional testing through an HTTP client against the real pipeline catches all of it in one shot.`,`So: yes, you can unit test the delegate. Should you? Spend that budget on a functional test that calls the API the way a real client does.`],quote:`Thin adapters deserve thin tests — point your budget at the pipeline, not the pass-through.`,table:{headers:[`Test style`,`Verifies`,`Misses`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Unit test on delegate`,`Handler logic in isolation`,`Routing, binding, DI, middleware`,`Rarely worth it`],[`Functional test via HTTP client`,`Full pipeline end to end`,`Nothing relevant`,`Default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Controllers By Refactoring To Minimal APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gsAuFIhXz3g`,at:`5:15`}}],diagrams:[{id:`before-after-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Controller topology vs Minimal API topology`,caption:`Same routes, same MediatR pipeline — the shared constructor disappears and each endpoint carries only its own dependencies.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph BEFORE["Before: ProductsController"]
        A["HTTP request"] --> CTOR["Constructor: ISender + 11 other services"]
        CTOR --> P["[HttpPost] Create()"]
        CTOR --> G["[HttpGet] Get()"]
        CTOR --> D["[HttpDelete] Delete()"]
        P --> OK["Ok() → IActionResult"]
        G --> OK
    end
    subgraph AFTER["After: Minimal APIs"]
        B["HTTP request"] --> EP["MapPost / MapGet / MapDelete on IEndpointRouteBuilder"]
        EP -->|"injects ISender only"| H["public static handler"]
        H --> RES["Results.Ok() → IResult"]
    end`},{id:`minimal-api-request-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One request through a Minimal API endpoint`,caption:`Method injection binds the body and ISender at the endpoint; the handler returns IResult from the Results factory.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant EP as MapPost api/products
    participant H as CreateProduct handler
    participant M as MediatR ISender
    participant F as Results factory
    C->>EP: POST /api/products with body
    EP->>H: bind body + inject ISender
    H->>M: Send(CreateProductCommand)
    M-->>H: command result
    H->>F: Results.Ok(payload)
    F-->>C: 200 OK as IResult`}],takeaways:[`Split any controller whose constructor exceeds 3 dependencies — most endpoints use only 1-2, so the rest are dead allocations on every request.`,`Convert one controller per PR into a static class with a MapXEndpoints(IEndpointRouteBuilder) extension; keep routes identical so clients never notice.`,`Use method injection in handlers — each endpoint declares exactly its own dependencies, cutting unused resolutions to zero.`,`Swap ControllerBase helpers for the Results factory and type handlers as IResult — Results.Ok, Results.NotFound, Results.Problem.`,`Cap unit tests on HTTP adapters at zero; invest in functional tests through an HTTP client — one end-to-end call verifies routing, binding, and DI together.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Controllers By Refactoring To Minimal APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gsAuFIhXz3g`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-v4vXDRJ9_sg`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:16,title:`Managing Permissions With EF Core Migrations | Permission Authorization - Part 2`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:34`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`09:30 during a latency regression audit: Every Endpoint Answers 403`,paragraphs:[`It's 15:40 as connection pools saturated and a 'tiny' release has just shipped: one new admin endpoint guarded by [HasPermission]. Staging was flawless. Eleven minutes after the prod deploy, support is on fire — every member, including accounts that worked five minutes ago, is getting 403 Forbidden.`,`The culprit takes four minutes to find: the Roles and Permissions tables in production are empty. Staging had rows because somebody ran a 'quick seed script' by hand three weeks ago and told exactly nobody. The authorization handler asks the database what this member can do, the database answers with zero rows — and zero rows means no.`,`The real fix isn't a hotpatch. It's a decision: roles and permissions are data, and data that authorization depends on belongs in EF Core migrations — seeded from the same enum the attribute compiles against, versioned with the schema, identical in every environment by construction.`],problem:`Authorization was reading its truth from tables nobody owned the seed for. We need the migration that creates Roles and Permissions to also fill them — deterministically, reviewably, and impossible to forget.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`EF Migrations`,`HasData`,`Smart Enums`,`Join Tables`,`Composite Key`],note:`EF Core migrations own both the schema and the seed rows; smart enums (the Enumeration base class) define roles in the domain layer; many-to-many join tables — with a composite key on RolePermissions — wire members to roles to permissions.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just run this SQL script' dies at 3 AM`,body:[`So the first instinct is always the same: 'I have the enum, let me write five INSERT statements and run them against each environment.' And it works — right up until the third environment, the second developer, and the first Friday deploy. A hand-run script is invisible in code review, it doesn't live in version control next to the schema, and the one person who remembers to run it is the one person on vacation.`,`The alternative I landed on is HasData inside the migration pipeline: the same migration artifact that creates the Roles and Permissions tables also inserts their initial rows. It's declarative, it's reviewed like any other code, and a fresh environment comes up fully authorization-aware with a single dotnet ef database update — nothing else to remember. The trade-off? HasData is a snapshot, not a program: no loops, no environment lookups, no runtime logic. For a permissions catalog, that's not a limitation — that's the point.`,`Why not seed at startup instead? Because then your authorization truth depends on boot order, two instances can race each other on cold start, and a crashed instance suddenly becomes a security question. Security data gets the boring, versioned, boring-again path: migrations.`],quote:`If your authorization depends on a script one person remembers to run, you don't have security — you have folklore.`,table:{headers:[`Seeding approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Hand-run SQL script`,`Fast, zero code`,`Environment drift, invisible in review`,`Never for auth data`],[`HasData in migration`,`Versioned, reviewable, replays identically everywhere`,`Snapshot-based, no runtime logic`,`Perfect for roles + permissions`],[`Runtime seeding at startup`,`Flexible logic`,`Race conditions, boot-order coupling`,`Avoid for security data`],[`Admin UI mutations`,`Ops-friendly`,`Drifts from the enum the attribute compiles against`,`Only for runtime role assignment`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Managing Permissions With EF Core Migrations | Permission Authorization - Part 2`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v4vXDRJ9_sg`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where Role and Permission actually live`,body:[`Next question: where do these types live? Both Role and Permission go into the domain project — authorization is core to this system, not an infrastructure detail. Role inherits from the Enumeration base class we built in the smart-enum session: the constructor takes an Id and a Name and passes both to the base, and every role is a static readonly field. Today there's exactly one: Registered, Id 1.`,`Permission is deliberately dumber — a plain entity with a public Id (init setter) and a Name. Why the asymmetry? Roles are few, fixed, and I want them as first-class code objects with GetValues() ready for seeding. Permissions are the thing the Part 1 enum already owns; the entity is just the row shape the join table can point at. Then Role gets two navigation properties: a collection of Permissions and a collection of Members. That's the whole hierarchy — members belong to roles, roles define permissions, and that chain is what answers 'can this member hit this endpoint?'`],quote:`A role is just a named bag of permissions — Member → Role → Permission is the entire authorization model.`,table:{headers:[`Modeling choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Smart enum (Enumeration base)`,`Id + Name + GetValues() for free`,`Slightly more code up front`,`Roles: few, fixed, seeded`],[`Plain C# enum`,`Zero ceremony`,`No behavior, name-only identity`,`Permission source of truth`],[`Plain entity class`,`Maps cleanly to a table row`,`No in-code registry`,`Permission row shape`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Managing Permissions With EF Core Migrations | Permission Authorization - Part 2`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v4vXDRJ9_sg`,at:`0:59`}},{heading:`03 · The many-to-many maze and the composite key that saves you`,body:[`Over in the persistence project, each entity gets an IEntityTypeConfiguration. For Role: builder.HasMany(r => r.Permissions).WithMany() and builder.HasMany(r => r.Members).WithMany() — a permission can belong to many roles, a member can belong to many roles, and EF turns each of those into a join table. Same rhythm for Permission: ToTable(TableNames.Permissions), the key is the Id, and there are no navigation properties at all — it's the leaf of the graph.`,`But here's the part I refuse to skip: the RolePermissions join gets an explicit composite key of RoleId plus PermissionId — you return a new object holding both values. Why bother? Because without it, nothing stops a buggy sync job or a careless insert from giving one role the same permission twice. With it, 'Registered has the same permission twice' is not a weird support ticket — it's a schema violation that physically cannot happen. Two extra lines of configuration, whole nights of sleep.`],quote:`Put the composite key in the join table and duplicates become a schema error instead of a 3 AM support ticket.`,table:{headers:[`Join strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`WithMany() implicit join`,`One line per relationship`,`No control over keys or extra columns`,`Quick prototypes only`],[`Join entity + composite key`,`Duplicate pairs impossible`,`More configuration`,`RolePermissions — always`],[`JSON column on Role`,`No joins at read time`,`Unqueryable, no referential integrity`,`Avoid for authorization`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Managing Permissions With EF Core Migrations | Permission Authorization - Part 2`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v4vXDRJ9_sg`,at:`2:11`}},{heading:`04 · Seeding from the enum — and that slight naming problem`,body:[`Seeding time. For roles: builder.HasData(Role.GetValues()) — that static GetValues() lives on the Enumeration base class and returns every defined role, so the migration ships an INSERT for Registered (Id 1) automatically. For permissions, we reach back to the Part 1 enum: every value becomes a row with Id = (int)value and Name = value.ToString(). One enum value, one row, always.`,`And now the slight problem I promised: we have two types named Permission — the enum from Part 1 and the new entity — sitting in different layers, while this configuration lives in the persistence project. Reference it naively and the compiler has no idea which Permission you mean. The fix is to be explicit: alias or namespace-qualify the enum, and keep it as the single source of truth the seeding maps from.`,`One more rule that saves future-you: HasData snapshots values at migration time, so you never edit a shipped migration. Adding a permission later means a new migration on top — the seed history reads like an append-only ledger, and every environment replays it identically.`],quote:`Your enum is the contract, the migration is the receipt — and you never rewrite a receipt.`,table:{headers:[`Source of truth`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Enum + HasData`,`Code and data can't disagree`,`New value = new migration`,`Permissions catalog`],[`Database-only rows`,`Add rows without deploys`,`Attribute compiles against enum, not DB`,`Drift magnet`],[`Config file`,`Editable per environment`,`Not versioned with the schema`,`Avoid`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Managing Permissions With EF Core Migrations | Permission Authorization - Part 2`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v4vXDRJ9_sg`,at:`4:16`}}],diagrams:[{id:`permission-model`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The permission graph: members to roles to permissions`,caption:`Two join tables carry the whole model; the Part 1 enum feeds HasData on both ends.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    M["Member"] -->|"many-to-many"| MR[("MemberRoles")]
    MR -->|"join rows"| R["Role<br/>Enumeration base"]
    R -->|"many-to-many"| RP[("RolePermissions<br/>PK: RoleId + PermissionId")]
    RP -->|"join rows"| P["Permission<br/>Id + Name"]
    E["Permissions enum<br/>source of truth"] -.->|"HasData: GetValues()"| R
    E -.->|"HasData: Id = (int)v, Name = v.ToString()"| P`},{id:`seed-to-check`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`From migration seed to a 200 OK`,caption:`The same pipeline that creates the tables fills them — and every request reads its answer from those rows.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Dev as Developer
    participant Mig as EF Migrations
    participant DB as Database
    participant API as API Endpoint
    participant Auth as HasPermission Handler
    Dev->>Mig: dotnet ef migrations add SeedRolesPermissions
    Mig->>Mig: Snapshot Role.GetValues() + enum values
    Mig->>DB: INSERT INTO Roles, Permissions, join tables
    Note over DB: Seed rows versioned with the schema
    Dev->>DB: dotnet ef database update
    loop Every authorized request
        API->>Auth: [HasPermission(Permissions.X)]
        Auth->>DB: Member, roles, permissions?
        DB-->>Auth: matching permission rows
        Auth-->>API: 200 OK or 403 Forbidden
    end`}],takeaways:[`Seed roles and permissions with HasData inside migrations: 0 hand-run scripts, 1 pipeline, identical authorization data in every environment.`,`Give the RolePermissions join a composite key of (RoleId, PermissionId) — exactly one row per pair; duplicates become a schema violation, not a support ticket.`,`Treat the Permissions enum as the single source of truth: 1 enum value = 1 seeded row — Id = (int)value, Name = value.ToString().`,`Never edit a shipped migration: adding permission N+1 is a new migration on top, so the seed history stays an append-only ledger.`,`Model the chain explicitly — Member → Role → Permission is just 2 join tables; adding a new role costs 1 static field + 1 migration, about 5 minutes of work.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Managing Permissions With EF Core Migrations | Permission Authorization - Part 2`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v4vXDRJ9_sg`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-RgqCavV2cqQ`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:17,title:`EF Core In The CQRS Query Side... Or Something Else?`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:19`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Dapper`,`CQRS`],story:{headline:`Two Dialects, One Feature Folder`,paragraphs:[`It's 11:40 on a Friday when the pager lights up: the orders support dashboard is showing updates to rows nobody touched. The audit trail is full of phantom UPDATEs on the Orders table, all traced back to — of all things — a read endpoint. GetOrderById. A query. The one endpoint everyone assumed couldn't possibly write anything.`,`The post-mortem is embarrassing in the way architecture problems always are. The query handler pulled a fully-tracked entity through the shared DbContext wrapper, something downstream mutated the object graph, and the request-scoped SaveChanges dutifully committed the damage. But while digging, the team finds the real wound: in the same Orders feature folder, reads go through IApplicationDbContext and its DbSets, while the create-order flow goes through repositories and a unit of work. Two dialects, one feature, zero consistency.`,`The new hire asks the question that ends every meeting early: 'So which one am I supposed to use?' Silence. That silence is the design smell. This session is about answering it — by giving the query side its own explicit abstraction, the read service, and making the boundary impossible to miss.`],problem:`The query side and the command side speak two different data-access dialects — IApplicationDbContext with DbSets for reads, repositories plus unit of work for writes — so nothing enforces a consistent, persistence-ignorant boundary, and every handler drifts by vibes.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`CQRS`,`Clean Architecture`,`Read Service`,`Unit of Work`],note:`The read side gets a dedicated IOrderReadService contract in the application layer, implemented over EF Core in persistence, while commands keep repositories and unit of work — one dialect per side, handlers stay thin.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just inject the DbContext everywhere?`,body:[`Let's be honest about the tempting option first. The GetOrder query handler depends on IApplicationDbContext, and crack it open — it's just a wrapper around the EF Core DbContext, implemented in the persistence project, exposing the DbSets of our domain entities. Writing the query right there in the handler with LINQ takes ninety seconds. It projects straight to OrderResponse, it's readable, and for a demo it's genuinely the right call.`,`But zoom out and the cracks show. The application layer now speaks EF. And worse — look across the feature folder — the CreateOrderCommandHandler doesn't touch IApplicationDbContext at all; it goes through repositories and a unit of work. So we have two abstractions doing overlapping jobs in the same bounded context. Every new developer picks a dialect by vibes. That's not architecture, that's archaeology.`,`And there's a quieter tax: a handler that holds the DbContext holds the change tracker. A 'read' that returns tracked entities is one careless mutation away from a phantom write at SaveChanges time. Reads should be structurally free of write-shaped accidents.`],quote:`Every abstraction you don't enforce is just a suggestion.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`EF Core directly in handler`,`Fastest to write, LINQ projections`,`App layer couples to EF, two dialects emerge`,`Prototypes, tiny apps`],[`IApplicationDbContext wrapper`,`Hides EF behind your own interface`,`Still EF-shaped, coexists with repos`,`Small clean-architecture apps`],[`IOrderReadService per feature`,`Persistence-ignorant reads, thin handlers`,`Extra files and DI ceremony`,`Growing features, 2+ queries`],[`Repositories for reads`,`Consistent with command side`,`Over-abstracts one-off queries`,`Legacy consistency only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core In The CQRS Query Side... Or Something Else?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RgqCavV2cqQ`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: introducing the read service`,body:[`So here's the move. We define an IOrderReadService interface right in the Orders feature folder — application layer, next to the handler. One method to start: GetByIdAsync taking the strongly-typed OrderId, returning Task of OrderResponse nullable. Then the implementation, OrderReadService, goes where EF actually belongs: the persistence project, internal and sealed, injecting the DbContext directly — not the wrapper.`,`The handler? It shrinks to almost nothing. Inject IOrderReadService, call GetByIdAsync, done. And because the database can legitimately return nothing, the method returns a nullable OrderResponse — the handler null-checks and throws an OrderNotFoundException carrying the strongly-typed OrderId. That exception lives in the domain layer, public and sealed, with a message like 'Order with the ID {Id.Value} was not found.' Specific, typed, testable.`,`Notice what we bought: the handler no longer knows EF exists. Swap the implementation to Dapper, a read replica, a materialized view — the contract doesn't budge. Persistence ignorance for queries, the same principle the command side already enjoys.`],quote:`Thin handlers aren't the goal — one dialect per side is; thinness is just the receipt.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Before (IApplicationDbContext)`,`After (IOrderReadService)`],rows:[[`Where LINQ lives`,`Inside the handler`,`Inside the persistence impl`],[`Handler knowledge`,`DbSets, EF types`,`One interface method`],[`Missing data`,`Ad hoc null handling`,`Nullable response + typed exception`],[`Swap data source`,`Rewrite every handler`,`Rewrite one class`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core In The CQRS Query Side... Or Something Else?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RgqCavV2cqQ`,at:`1:30`}},{heading:`03 · The honest cost: your queries become thin wrappers`,body:[`Now the downside, because there always is one. Every query handler is now a thin wrapper around the read service — and you feel it immediately on the trivial ones. GetOrderSummaryQueryHandler was already three lines: query the OrderSummaries, return the match by its Guid ID. Wrapping that means a new interface method, GetSummaryByIdAsync returning OrderSummary, a new implementation block, and a DI registration.`,`Is that ceremony worth it? My rule: indirection is a subscription, not a purchase. You pay a little every time — one interface, one internal sealed class, one scoped registration in the persistence project — and in exchange you get a seam. The day someone says 'this summary query is slow, let's hit the read replica with raw SQL,' you change one class and zero handlers.`,`If a feature has exactly one dumb query and no ambitions, direct EF is honest pragmatism. The moment there are two, three, or a hot path, the seam starts paying rent. Decide per feature, not per religion.`],quote:`Indirection is a subscription — pay monthly in files and registrations, cancel anytime the seam stops earning.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Upfront cost`,`Long-term payoff`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Direct EF in handlers`,`Zero`,`Couples app layer to EF, hard to mock`,`Fine for one trivial query`],[`Read service over EF`,`1 interface + 1 class + 1 DI line`,`Swap to Dapper or replica later, mockable`,`Default for real features`],[`Read service over raw SQL`,`Same, plus SQL authoring`,`Max read performance, hand-tuned queries`,`Hot paths, complex reports`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core In The CQRS Query Side... Or Something Else?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RgqCavV2cqQ`,at:`3:13`}},{heading:`04 · Naming and placement: make the boundary impossible to miss`,body:[`Last thing, and it's the cheapest: naming. We started with IOrderService and I renamed it mid-stream to IOrderReadService — because 'service' tells you nothing, while 'read service' announces exactly which side of the CQRS line you're standing on. IOrderQueryService works too. If the interface name doesn't tell you the dialect, rename it.`,`Placement follows the same logic. The interface lives in the application layer's feature folder — that's the contract handlers depend on. The implementation lives in persistence, the only layer allowed to speak EF. DTOs like OrderResponse and OrderSummary live in application and never leak EF entities. OrderNotFoundException lives in domain, next to its sibling ProductNotFoundException, carrying the strongly-typed OrderId.`,`And the DI registration is scoped in the persistence project, matching the DbContext lifetime — never singleton over a DbContext. Get those four placements right and the architecture explains itself in a code review without you in the room.`],quote:`If the name doesn't tell you which side of CQRS you're on, the architecture won't either.`,table:{headers:[`Artifact`,`Lives in`,`Why there`],rows:[[`IOrderReadService`,`Application (feature folder)`,`Contract the handler depends on`],[`OrderReadService (internal sealed)`,`Persistence`,`Owns the DbContext, the only EF speaker`],[`OrderResponse / OrderSummary`,`Application`,`DTO contracts, never EF entities`],[`OrderNotFoundException`,`Domain`,`Shared language, strongly-typed OrderId`],[`Scoped DI registration`,`Persistence`,`Matches the DbContext lifetime`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core In The CQRS Query Side... Or Something Else?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RgqCavV2cqQ`,at:`6:56`}}],diagrams:[{id:`arch-boundary`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One dialect per side: the CQRS read/write split`,caption:`Query handlers depend on IOrderReadService; only the persistence layer speaks EF Core.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph API["API Layer"]
        EP["Endpoints"]
    end
    subgraph APP["Application Layer"]
        QH["GetOrderQueryHandler"]
        CH["CreateOrderCommandHandler"]
        IRS["IOrderReadService"]
        IUOW["Repositories + IUnitOfWork"]
    end
    subgraph DOM["Domain Layer"]
        EX["OrderNotFoundException"]
        OID["OrderId strong type"]
    end
    subgraph PERS["Persistence Layer"]
        ORS["OrderReadService internal sealed"]
        REPO["OrderRepository"]
        CTX["EF Core DbContext"]
    end
    DB[("SQL Database")]
    EP --> QH
    EP --> CH
    QH -->|query side| IRS
    CH -->|command side| IUOW
    IRS -.->|implemented by| ORS
    IUOW -.->|implemented by| REPO
    ORS --> CTX
    REPO --> CTX
    CTX --> DB
    QH -.->|throws on null| EX
    EX -.->|carries| OID`},{id:`get-order-seq`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`GetOrder: happy path and the not-found branch`,caption:`The handler null-checks the nullable response and throws the domain's typed exception.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as API Endpoint
    participant H as GetOrderQueryHandler
    participant RS as IOrderReadService
    participant CTX as EF Core DbContext
    participant DB as SQL Database
    API->>H: Send GetOrderQuery
    H->>RS: GetByIdAsync(orderId)
    RS->>CTX: Projected Orders query
    CTX->>DB: SELECT Id, Total FROM Orders
    DB-->>CTX: row or empty
    CTX-->>RS: OrderResponse?
    alt order found
        RS-->>H: OrderResponse
        H-->>API: 200 OK
    else order missing
        RS-->>H: null
        H->>H: throw OrderNotFoundException(orderId)
        H-->>API: 404
    end`}],takeaways:[`One dialect per side: commands flow through repositories + unit of work, reads through I{Feature}ReadService — zero handlers mixing both.`,`Keep query handlers under ~10 lines: if LINQ lives in the handler, it's a refactor waiting to happen — push it behind the read service interface.`,`Read services return DTOs and nullable results (OrderResponse?), never tracked entities — keep the change tracker at 0 entries on the read path.`,`Register each read service as scoped in the persistence DI container, matching the DbContext lifetime — one line per service, never singleton over a DbContext.`,`Introduce a read service once a feature has 2+ queries or a hot path headed for Dapper or a read replica; a single trivial query can honestly stay on direct EF.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core In The CQRS Query Side... Or Something Else?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RgqCavV2cqQ`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`event-sourcing-cqrs`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-PxxZcTBYi34`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:18,title:`How To Use Both EF Core and Dapper For CQRS Queries`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:02`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Dapper`,`CQRS`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The Night We Almost Rewrote the Entire Data Layer`,paragraphs:[`Launch night for the new customer portal. The marketing email fires at midnight, and by 09:15 during the morning rush the on-call channel is on fire: the /users search endpoint has climbed from 40ms to 900ms p95 and is now timing out. The EF-generated SQL for search-with-paging-plus-count is doing a table scan and a separate count round trip, and the first instinct in the thread is the nuclear one: rip EF Core out, everything in Dapper, by Friday.`,`I pull the query stats while my coffee goes cold: 38 of 41 data access calls are boring — by-ID lookups, saves, updates. Only three queries are hot and complex. Rewriting the whole layer to fix 7% of your queries is how you cause a second incident while fixing the first one. So instead we go surgical: EF Core stays everywhere it's fine, the three hot list queries get hand-written SQL in Dapper, and both engines return the exact same UserResponse records.`,`The performance fix was the easy part. The interesting fight happened the next morning, when we had to decide, deliberately, that letting EF Core leak into the application layer through an IApplicationDbContext interface was a price worth paying. Textbook clean architecture says no. We said yes — and gave the decision a name so nobody would 'fix' it out of guilt later: pragmatic clean architecture.`],problem:`A melting read endpoint forced a false binary — all-EF or all-Dapper — when the real design question was where, per query, the boundary between the two engines should sit, and whether accepting EF Core as a dependency in the application layer was a tradeoff worth making on purpose.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 7`,`Dapper`,`CQRS`,`IQueryHandler`,`AsNoTracking`,`IApplicationDbContext`,`Pragmatic Clean Arch`],note:`EF Core owns commands and by-ID reads behind a thin IApplicationDbContext seam; Dapper takes the complex list queries with hand-written SQL — both project into the same response records inside CQRS feature folders.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the either/or debate is a trap`,body:[`So here's the question I get in every Q&A: 'EF Core or Dapper?' And I've stopped answering it as asked, because it's the wrong unit of decision. People debate it like a religion — team EF, team Dapper — but your application isn't one big query. It's forty small ones with completely different shapes.`,`Once CQRS is in place, the seam is already sitting there waiting for you. A query class, a query handler, a response record — that handler is a perfectly sized box to make a data-access decision inside of. GetUserById gets one engine, the search endpoint gets another, and neither knows or cares. The client sees the same UserResponse either way.`,`The trade is real, though: two APIs to know, plus a team norm that says 'the handler decides' so you don't get engine-of-the-week churn. If your app is pure trivial CRUD, this is over-engineering. The moment one query starts fighting the ORM, it starts paying rent.`],quote:`You don't pick an ORM for the application — you pick one per query.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`EF Core everywhere`,`One mental model, migrations, change tracking`,`Generated SQL drifts on complex reads`,`CRUD-heavy apps`],[`Dapper everywhere`,`Full SQL control, minimal overhead`,`You own every column, no tracking, more boilerplate`,`SQL-centric teams`],[`Both, per query`,`Right tool per shape, shared response records`,`Two APIs to know, seam discipline required`,`CQRS feature folders`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use Both EF Core and Dapper For CQRS Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PxxZcTBYi34`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the IApplicationDbContext tradeoff`,body:[`Now the boundary question, because this is where the purists reach for torches. The textbook move is a generic repository that hides EF Core completely. I've built those. What actually happens: your abstraction is IQueryable, async IQueryable doesn't abstract cleanly, and six months later you're writing expression trees to express things EF could've done in one line. You end up mocking a mockingbird.`,`So in this design the application layer gets an IApplicationDbContext — a thin interface exposing a DbSet of Users — and the handler injects it directly. Yes, that means EF Core, an external dependency, lives in the application layer. That is not clean architecture by the book. I call it pragmatic clean architecture: domain-centric structure, but honest about the fact that querying is a practical decision, not a philosophical one.`,`What do you get for the sin? Handlers you can read in ten seconds, tests that run against a real provider or an in-memory fake, and zero leaky-abstraction tax. The dependency is declared, deliberate, and lives in exactly one place.`],quote:`An abstraction that hides the ORM but leaks IQueryable isn't clean — it's expensive cosplay.`,table:{headers:[`Boundary option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Generic repository over IQueryable`,`Hides EF fully on paper`,`Leaks via IQueryable, async pain, mock theater`,`Small apps`],[`IApplicationDbContext in app layer`,`Honest, thin, easily testable`,`EF Core reference in the application layer`,`Pragmatic CQRS`],[`Raw IDbConnection only`,`Zero ORM coupling`,`You rebuild change tracking by hand`,`Dapper-only shops`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use Both EF Core and Dapper For CQRS Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PxxZcTBYi34`,at:`0:32`}},{heading:`03 · The EF Core side: GetUserById with AsNoTracking`,body:[`Let's build the EF side live. GetUserByIdQuery — a record implementing IQuery, with UserResponse as the response type. UserResponse is a record too: Id, Email, UserName, HasPublicProfile, get-and-init. Then GetUserByIdQueryHandler implements IQueryHandler for that pair, and the Handle method does the entire job in a few lines.`,`Inside: inject IApplicationDbContext, hit the Users DbSet, and — this is the part everyone forgets — AsNoTracking. It's a read; nobody is going to SaveChanges this entity; tracking it just allocates change-tracker snapshots and does identity resolution for nothing. Then project straight into UserResponse so we only pull the four columns the response actually needs.`,`Why EF here at all? Because for a single entity by key, it's one typed line, there's no SQL string to typo, and if the response grows a related collection next sprint, Include is one more line instead of a new join you now personally own.`],quote:`AsNoTracking on a read isn't an optimization — it's the default you should have to argue against.`,table:{headers:[`EF read option`,`What it does`,`Cost`,`Use when`],rows:[[`Tracked query`,`Change tracker + identity map`,`Snapshot allocations per row`,`You'll mutate the entity`],[`AsNoTracking`,`Skips the change tracker`,`Roughly half the materialization overhead`,`Pure reads`],[`AsNoTracking + projection`,`Selects only response columns`,`Least data over the wire`,`Query endpoints`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use Both EF Core and Dapper For CQRS Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PxxZcTBYi34`,at:`1:09`}},{heading:`04 · The Dapper side: when the SQL is already in your head`,body:[`Second query, same users feature folder, different engine. This one's the list — search, filters, paging. I open a connection from the factory and write the SQL by hand: exact joins, exact WHERE, ORDER BY, OFFSET/FETCH, mapped straight into UserResponse. Dapper does the row mapping, done.`,`Why not EF here too? You can — but complex projections are exactly where EF's generated SQL starts surprising you: a join you didn't ask for, a subquery for the count, a plan that looked great in your head and terrible in the query store. With Dapper, the SQL you wrote is the SQL that runs. The source of truth is text you can read, review in a PR, and tune with an index.`,`The bill for that control: you own column names, you own null mapping, and there's no change tracking — which is fine, because a query result is disposable by definition. Nobody is calling SaveChanges on a search result.`],quote:`Dapper doesn't make your query fast — it just stops hiding the query from you.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`EF Core (AsNoTracking)`,`Dapper`,`Winner`],rows:[[`By-ID lookup`,`One typed line`,`SQL + mapping by hand`,`EF Core`],[`Complex list / report`,`Generated SQL can drift`,`Exact plan, exact columns`,`Dapper`],[`Migrations & change tracking`,`Built in`,`Bring your own`,`EF Core`],[`Raw perf ceiling`,`High with projection`,`Marginally higher, no translation`,`Tie in practice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use Both EF Core and Dapper For CQRS Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PxxZcTBYi34`,at:`2:14`}},{heading:`05 · The rule I leave the room with`,body:[`So here's the rule I actually ship. Commands and by-ID reads: EF Core — tracked for commands, AsNoTracking for reads. Anything with three-plus joins, dynamic filters, or hand-tuned paging: Dapper. Both engines write into the same response records, both live side by side in the same feature folder, and the handler — not a global policy — makes the call.`,`And the meta-rule underneath it: make the tradeoff on purpose. We let EF Core into the application layer and we named that decision, so nobody 'fixes' it six months from now out of architectural guilt. Pragmatic clean architecture isn't a lower standard — it's a decision with a receipt attached.`],quote:`Split the data layer by query shape, not by tribe.`,table:{headers:[`Query shape`,`Engine`,`Why`],rows:[[`Get by ID`,`EF Core + AsNoTracking`,`Typed, one line, no SQL to typo`],[`Search + paging`,`Dapper`,`Exact SQL, exact plan, indexable`],[`Commands (save / update)`,`EF Core tracked`,`Change tracking + SaveChanges`],[`Reports / aggregates`,`Dapper`,`Hand-tuned joins and GROUP BY`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use Both EF Core and Dapper For CQRS Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PxxZcTBYi34`,at:`3:45`}}],diagrams:[{id:`polyglot-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One feature folder, two engines`,caption:`Both handlers live side by side; each picks its own engine and both project into the same response record.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    Req["HTTP Request"] --> Q["IQuery dispatch"]
    Q --> H1["GetUserByIdQueryHandler"]
    Q --> H2["GetUsersQueryHandler"]

    subgraph EFPath["EF Core path"]
        Ctx["IApplicationDbContext"] --> Set["DbSet Users"] --> NT["AsNoTracking + projection"]
    end

    subgraph DapPath["Dapper path"]
        Conn["IDbConnectionFactory"] --> SQL["Hand-written SQL"]
    end

    H1 --> Ctx
    H2 --> Conn
    NT --> DB[("Database")]
    SQL --> DB
    DB --> Resp["UserResponse record"]`},{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path: by-ID vs list`,caption:`The same endpoint surface, two very different trips to the database — and the client can't tell the difference.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as Endpoint
    participant H1 as GetUserById Handler
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant H2 as GetUsers Handler
    participant D as Dapper
    participant DB as Database

    C->>A: GET /users/42
    A->>H1: GetUserByIdQuery
    H1->>EF: Users.AsNoTracking() projection
    EF->>DB: SELECT Id, Email, UserName, HasPublicProfile WHERE Id = 42
    DB-->>EF: 1 row
    EF-->>H1: untracked entity
    H1-->>A: UserResponse
    A-->>C: 200 OK

    C->>A: GET /users?search=ada
    A->>H2: GetUsersQuery
    H2->>D: query mapped to UserResponse (sql, args)
    D->>DB: SELECT ... WHERE UserName LIKE @search ORDER BY ... OFFSET/FETCH
    DB-->>D: rows
    D-->>H2: list of UserResponse
    H2-->>A: paged result
    A-->>C: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Default to EF Core for commands and single-entity reads; switch to Dapper the moment a query needs 3+ joins, dynamic filters, or hand-tuned paging.`,`Call AsNoTracking() on 100% of read queries — skipping change-tracker snapshots is the cheapest perf win in EF Core, often cutting materialization overhead roughly in half.`,`Expose exactly two data-access seams — IApplicationDbContext for EF and a connection factory for Dapper — and let each query handler, not a global policy, pick its engine.`,`Project every query straight into its response record (e.g. UserResponse) so you can swap EF↔Dapper per handler without ever breaking the API contract.`,`If a hot query runs over ~50ms and EF's generated SQL doesn't match your mental plan, rewrite that one query in Dapper — one handler, not the whole data layer.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Use Both EF Core and Dapper For CQRS Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PxxZcTBYi34`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`event-sourcing-cqrs`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-3EBb8zGJCP4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:19,title:`The CORRECT Way to Handle Value Objects in EF Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:35`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`DDD`],story:{headline:`The Search Bar We Broke by Fixing the Domain`,paragraphs:[`It's 14:30 right after a canary deploy. We shipped the 'clean domain' refactor at five that afternoon — string emails replaced by a shiny Email value object, a C# record with real validation baked into the constructor. The exact-match filter passed its tests. Then the on-call pager lit up: the user search endpoint was 500ing on every request, and the exception was one line long — the LINQ expression 'could not be translated.'`,`I pulled the distributed traces in the Aspire dashboard. The last good request, from before the refactor, showed exactly what you'd hope for: SELECT ... WHERE Email LIKE '%term%'. The new request never produced SQL at all — EF Core died during translation, before the database ever saw anything. The value conversion we'd added taught EF how to store the email. It never taught it how to query inside one.`,`The fix wasn't rolling back DDD, and it wasn't scattering regexes back into controllers. It was mapping the value object so its inner value became a real column — and the same LINQ translated to LIKE again. That night turned into this talk.`],problem:`EF Core can persist a value object through a conversion, but it cannot translate queries that reach inside one — so the moment you filter on .Value, query translation dies before any SQL is generated.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 8`,`C# Records`,`DDD Value Objects`,`HasConversion`,`ComplexProperty`,`Aspire Dashboard`],note:`EF Core maps the domain's Email value object to columns — HasConversion for opaque single-column storage, ComplexProperty so queries can reach inside — while C# records supply structural equality and the type itself owns validation.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why a plain string betrays you`,body:[`So here's where I always start: a User entity with an Id and an Email — and Email is just a string. It works, right? The controller filters users by email, Contains for search, equality for exact match, and EF Core happily translates both. I fire two requests, open the Aspire dashboard, and there it is in the trace: LIKE for the search term, equals for the exact match. Clean.`,`But look at what's not in the picture. Where do the email rules live? A helper in an application service, a regex in a controller, maybe the same check duplicated in three places. That's primitive obsession — the string can hold an email, but it can't enforce anything about one. Every use case that touches Email re-teaches the same rules.`,`So the instinct is right: push it into the domain. The question that broke my production system once — and the one we're here to answer — is what happens to those beautifully translated queries the moment Email stops being a string.`],quote:`A string can hold an email; it can't hold the rules of what makes one valid.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Primitive string`,`Value object`],rows:[[`Where rules live`,`Scattered in services`,`Inside the type`],[`Equality`,`Case and reference bugs`,`Structural via record`],[`EF query translation`,`Direct to SQL`,`Depends on mapping`],[`Fit`,`Throwaway demos`,`Any real domain`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The CORRECT Way to Handle Value Objects in EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3EBb8zGJCP4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: modeling Email as a record`,body:[`First move: make Email a record. Why a record? Because I want structural equality out of the box — two Emails with the same value are the same Email, which is exactly what a value object means. Inside, one read-only Value property, and the constructor becomes the gate.`,`For validation I keep it boring: try to parse with System.Net.Mail.MailAddress, catch the exception, return false. Could I write a perfect RFC 5322 regex? Sure — and I'd regret it. The point isn't the validator; it's that 'invalid email' is now unrepresentable. If you're holding an Email instance, it's valid. Full stop.`,`And here's the part everyone forgets to check: EF Core has never seen this type. It doesn't know it's a wrapper around a string. Left alone, it fails at model building — or worse, at runtime. That's our next stop.`],quote:`Make the illegal state unrepresentable — then make sure your database agrees.`,table:{headers:[`Validation approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`],rows:[[`Check in controller or service`,`Fast to write`,`Rules duplicated everywhere`],[`MailAddress try/catch in VO`,`Reuses the framework`,`Throw-based, swallows details`],[`Custom regex plus guard clause`,`Explicit and testable`,`More code you own`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The CORRECT Way to Handle Value Objects in EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3EBb8zGJCP4`,at:`0:52`}},{heading:`03 · The value conversion trap`,body:[`The textbook fix is a value conversion. In the model builder: HasConversion — to the database, store email.Value; from the database, new Email(value). Two lambdas, done. And honestly? Exact match keeps working: EF compares the whole object through the converter and emits Email = @term. The trace proves it.`,`Then the search endpoint runs. u.Email.Value.Contains(term) — and EF Core throws: the expression could not be translated. Why? Because a converter teaches EF how to store your object, not how to query inside it. To the SQL provider, Email is an opaque blob; .Value is a .NET member, not a column. There is no SQL for it. That's the 11:20 during end-of-month billing exception, replayed on demand.`,`One more landmine while we're here: reference-type conversions should ship with a ValueComparer, or change tracking starts making questionable decisions about when things changed. Records soften the blow with structural equality, but say it out loud in code review anyway.`],quote:`A converter teaches EF how to store your object — never how to query it.`,table:{headers:[`Capability`,`HasConversion`,`ComplexProperty (EF 8)`],rows:[[`Single-column storage`,`Yes`,`Column per property`],[`Whole-object equality query`,`Yes`,`Yes`],[`Contains or LIKE on Value`,`No — translation error`,`Yes`],[`ValueComparer needed`,`Recommended`,`Not required`],[`Mapping effort`,`One line`,`A few lines`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The CORRECT Way to Handle Value Objects in EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3EBb8zGJCP4`,at:`1:40`}},{heading:`04 · The correct way: complex types`,body:[`Here's the move I now reach for on EF Core 8 and later: ComplexProperty. Instead of collapsing Email into a single opaque value, you map the inside of it — Email.Value becomes a real column named Email. Same table, same data, but now the query pipeline can actually see the property.`,`Watch what that buys us: u.Email.Value.Contains(term) translates. The trace shows SELECT ... WHERE Email LIKE '%term%'. Exact match still works. And the domain didn't change — the record, the constructor validation, all of it stays. We just stopped hiding the parts EF needs to see. On older EF Core, OwnsOne gets you the same shape.`,`And 'why not just keep a string and validate elsewhere?' Because then we're back to block one — rules leaking into every use case, three teams solving the same problem. The correct way isn't 'value objects or queries'; it's a mapping that lets you have both.`],quote:`Map the inside of the value object, not just the wrapper.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`Best mapping`,`Why`],rows:[[`Equality-only VO (Currency)`,`HasConversion`,`Cheapest; opaque is fine`],[`Searchable VO (Email)`,`ComplexProperty`,`Value becomes a real column`],[`Multi-property VO (Address)`,`ComplexProperty or OwnsOne`,`Column per property`],[`EF Core before version 8`,`OwnsOne`,`Same queryable benefits`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The CORRECT Way to Handle Value Objects in EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3EBb8zGJCP4`,at:`4:36`}},{heading:`05 · Where I'd draw the line`,body:[`Do I complex-type everything now? No. If a value object is only ever matched whole — a Currency, a CountryCode — a one-line HasConversion is cheaper and perfectly fine. Reach for complex types when queries need to reach inside: search terms, ranges, sorting on an inner property.`,`And be honest about verification. Every non-trivial LINQ query gets checked against its SQL — trace it in the Aspire dashboard, or capture the generated SQL in a test. 'It compiles' and 'it translates' are different milestones, and only one of them survives production.`],quote:`Compiles is not translates — prove the SQL before the pager proves it for you.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The CORRECT Way to Handle Value Objects in EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3EBb8zGJCP4`,at:`5:30`}}],diagrams:[{id:`translation-paths`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`How Email mapping decides query translation`,caption:`Same LINQ intent, three mappings, three outcomes — only complex types keep Contains alive.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["LINQ: Where(u => ...)"] --> B{"How is Email mapped?"}
    B -->|"primitive string"| C["EF Core translates directly"]
    C --> D["SQL: Email LIKE '%term%' / Email = @term — works"]
    B -->|"HasConversion"| E["Email is opaque to SQL"]
    E --> F{"Query shape?"}
    F -->|"u.Email == new Email(x)"| G["SQL: Email = @term — works"]
    F -->|"u.Email.Value.Contains(x)"| H["TranslationException — 500 at 16:45 on a pre-release Friday"]
    B -->|"ComplexProperty (EF 8)"| I["Email.Value is a real column"]
    I --> J["SQL: Email LIKE '%term%' — works"]`},{id:`search-request-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One search request, two mapping fates`,caption:`The request that paged me, and the request that should have shipped.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as UsersController
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant DB as Database
    API->>EF: Where(u => u.Email.Value.Contains(term))
    alt Mapped with HasConversion
        EF->>EF: Attempt expression translation
        EF-->>API: InvalidOperationException: could not be translated
        Note over EF,DB: No SQL ever reaches the database
    else Mapped with ComplexProperty (EF 8)
        EF->>DB: SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email LIKE '%term%'
        DB-->>EF: Matching rows
        EF-->>API: List<User>
    end`}],takeaways:[`Any primitive carrying business rules is a value object waiting to happen — wrap it before the rules leak into three different services.`,`HasConversion answers 'how to store', never 'how to query': whole-object equality translates fine, .Value.Contains never will.`,`On EF Core 8+, use ComplexProperty when queries must reach inside a value object — Email.Value becomes a real column and Contains translates to LIKE.`,`Pair every ValueConverter on a reference type with a ValueComparer (records help), or change tracking will quietly misbehave.`,`Verify each LINQ query's generated SQL in a trace before merge — 'it compiles' and 'it translates' are different milestones.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — The CORRECT Way to Handle Value Objects in EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3EBb8zGJCP4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Bi8oRSu-QgU`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:20,title:`Don't Break the Clean Architecture Rules with EF Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:07`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Dapper`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The Interface That Lied: Our 'Clean' Layer Was EF Core in a Trench Coat`,paragraphs:[`Friday, 4:40 PM. A pull request titled 'Make the application layer ORM-agnostic' is one approval from merge. The tech lead taps IApplicationDbContext in the review: 'See? No EF Core in the application layer.' Then the intern, prepping a demo, tries to fake that context in a unit test — and the compiler demands Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore. The interface returns DbSet<User>. The 'abstraction' was the DbContext wearing a different name.`,`It got worse. The second handler, GetUserByEmail, was supposedly the clean one — no EF Core, just Dapper through an IDbConnectionFactory. Except it was hand-writing raw SQL strings in the application layer, which is its own violation of the same rule. Two query handlers, two data-access tools, two different fonts of the same broken dependency rule.`,`The fix that finally landed wasn't a new pattern or another layer of interfaces. It was moving two files into Infrastructure/Queries/Users and deleting two interfaces. The team spent the weekend debating whether that counts as cheating. It doesn't — it's the dependency rule, finally enforced by project references instead of hope.`],problem:`The application layer's IApplicationDbContext exposes DbSet properties and IQueryable, so EF Core types flow straight through the 'abstraction' — the dependency rule is violated, and the interface delivers none of the swap-ability or testability it promised.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`Dapper`,`MediatR`,`Clean Architecture`,`Repository Pattern`,`Unit of Work`],note:`EF Core and Dapper are the data-access dependencies leaking through IApplicationDbContext and IDbConnectionFactory; MediatR is the assembly-scanned pipeline whose registration breaks when query handlers move to the infrastructure layer.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: is that abstraction real, or is it EF Core in a wig?`,body:[`Here's the thing I want you to see before we touch any code: look at IApplicationDbContext. It lives in the application layer, our query handler references it — and it defines two DbSet properties so we can hit the Users and Followers tables. The moment you expose DbSet or return IQueryable, you have imported EF Core into your 'clean' layer. The interface didn't abstract anything; it just renamed the DbContext.`,`And this is exactly why we tolerate the repository pattern and unit of work in our command handlers — they keep EF Core out of the application layer and buy us testability. But the query side? GetUserById is executing an EF Core LINQ query through that fake abstraction, and GetUserByEmail is running raw SQL strings through Dapper. Two handlers, two tools, both breaking the same rule in different fonts.`,`So the question for this session is simple: can we keep the exact same implementation — same LINQ, same Dapper — and still honor the rule that the application layer carries no external dependencies? Spoiler: yes, and it ends with less code, not more.`],quote:`An interface that forces 'using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore' isn't an abstraction — it's a forwarding address.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Break the Clean Architecture Rules with EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bi8oRSu-QgU`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: pragmatic vs. purist`,body:[`Let's be honest about the fork in the road. Option one is what I actually do on most projects: be pragmatic. Accept the EF Core dependency in query handlers, use it directly, ship features. My general opinion is that's a perfectly defensible design — Clean Architecture is a set of trade-offs, not commandments.`,`But suppose you don't want to accept it. Maybe your team treats the dependency rule as a hard gate in code review. The naive fix is to wrap everything in more interfaces — a query abstraction over the query handler — and that's how codebases drown in ceremony. Every extra interface is indirection you now maintain, and none of it removes the dependency; it just hides it one layer deeper.`,`The insight is that the dependency rule constrains where code lives, not what code does. If a query handler is inseparable from its data-access implementation, then it isn't application-layer logic at all — it belongs in the infrastructure layer, next to the thing it can't be separated from.`],quote:`Clean Architecture is a dependency rule, not a file-count rule — pragmatism is allowed, leakage isn't.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`EF Core directly in app layer`,`Zero indirection, fastest to ship`,`Breaks dependency rule, EF types everywhere`,`Small apps, MVPs, solo teams`],[`Repository per query`,`Layer looks clean on paper`,`Leaky IQueryable, heavy boilerplate`,`Rarely — mostly ceremony`],[`Query handlers in infrastructure`,`App layer has zero EF/Dapper references`,`DI registration moves, ~2 breaking changes`,`Teams enforcing the rule for real`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Break the Clean Architecture Rules with EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bi8oRSu-QgU`,at:`0:51`}},{heading:`03 · The move: query handlers belong next to their implementation`,body:[`So here's the move, and it's almost embarrassingly simple. In infrastructure, I create a Queries folder, and under it a subfolder per domain — Queries/Users. Then I pick up GetUserByIdHandler from the application layer and drop it there, adjusting the namespace to match: Infrastructure.Queries.Users. Same treatment for GetUserByEmailHandler.`,`Notice what happens to the Dapper handler: it was using a database connection factory and hand-written SQL, which is also against the rules while it sits in the application layer. Once it's in infrastructure, raw SQL is just... an implementation detail. Both violations evaporate without changing a single line of query logic.`,`The application layer keeps the query records — the contracts — and MediatR doesn't care which assembly the handler lives in. Callers send GetUserByIdQuery and get a response back exactly as before. The boundary is now enforced by project references, not by convention and good intentions.`],quote:`Queries are implementation details — put them where the implementation lives.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Break the Clean Architecture Rules with EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bi8oRSu-QgU`,at:`2:00`}},{heading:`04 · Deleting the fake abstractions (interfaces-for-DI are dead weight)`,body:[`Now for my favorite part: deleting things. With the handlers gone, IApplicationDbContext has exactly one implementation — ApplicationDbContext — and I now have legitimate access to it from infrastructure. So the interface is pure ceremony. Delete it, and inject the DbContext directly in the handler.`,`Same story for IDbConnectionFactory: one implementation, one consumer, both now in infrastructure. Delete the interface and inject the concrete factory into the Dapper handler. Having interfaces purely so dependency injection has something to resolve adds no value — you'll be just fine using the classes directly.`,`This is the mental shift: an abstraction earns its place by having multiple implementations or a real seam you test across. A single-implementation interface over a concrete class is a forwarding address, and forwarding addresses cost maintenance forever.`],quote:`One implementation means zero abstraction — delete the interface.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Break the Clean Architecture Rules with EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bi8oRSu-QgU`,at:`3:36`}},{heading:`05 · The bill: what breaks when code crosses a layer boundary`,body:[`Now, this refactoring is not free, and I want you to see the bill before you merge. Breaking change number one: the application layer's AddApplication method calls AddMediator, which assembly-scans for handlers. Our handlers just moved to the infrastructure assembly, so that scan comes up empty — and the first Send for a query blows up at runtime with a handler-not-found error.`,`The fix is to copy the mediator service registration into AddInfrastructure so the handlers get discovered from their new home. Breaking change number two is the constructor signatures: deleting the two interfaces changes what the handlers inject, so the DI setup now registers the ApplicationDbContext and the connection factory as concrete classes directly.`,`Two breaking changes, both mechanical, both catchable with one smoke test per query endpoint. That's the entire cost. Compare that to the alternative — a repository-per-query layer you'll maintain forever — and the trade is usually obvious.`],quote:`Moving code across a boundary always re-prices your DI registration — budget for it.`,table:{headers:[`Change`,`What breaks`,`Fix`],rows:[[`Move handlers to Infrastructure`,`AddMediator assembly scan misses them`,`Copy mediator registration into AddInfrastructure`],[`Delete IApplicationDbContext`,`Handler constructor type changes`,`Inject ApplicationDbContext directly`],[`Delete IDbConnectionFactory`,`Dapper handler constructor changes`,`Register and inject the concrete factory`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Break the Clean Architecture Rules with EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bi8oRSu-QgU`,at:`4:30`}}],diagrams:[{id:`dependency-flip`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Dependency arrows before and after the move`,caption:`The fix isn't a new pattern — it's relocating the handlers so the EF Core and Dapper references leave the application layer entirely.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph before["Before - dependency rule broken"]
    direction TB
    A1["Application layer<br/>query handlers"] -->|"IApplicationDbContext<br/>DbSet + IQueryable"| E1["EF Core"]
    A1 -->|"IDbConnectionFactory<br/>raw SQL"| D1["Dapper"]
  end
  subgraph after["After - dependency rule honored"]
    direction TB
    A2["Application layer<br/>commands + query contracts"] --> I2["Infrastructure layer<br/>Queries/Users"]
    I2 --> E2["EF Core + Dapper"]
  end`},{id:`query-request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Query request path after the refactor`,caption:`Callers see no difference: MediatR resolves the handler from the infrastructure assembly and the LINQ query runs exactly as before.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant API as API Endpoint
  participant Med as MediatR Pipeline
  participant H as GetUserByIdHandler (Infrastructure)
  participant Ctx as ApplicationDbContext
  participant Sql as SQL Server
  API->>Med: Send(GetUserByIdQuery)
  Med->>H: Handle(query, ct)
  H->>Ctx: Users.FirstOrDefaultAsync(id)
  Ctx->>Sql: SELECT TOP 1 FROM Users
  Sql-->>Ctx: row
  Ctx-->>H: User entity
  H-->>Med: UserResponse
  Med-->>API: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Run one grep: 'Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore' in the application layer. One hit means the dependency rule is already broken, no matter how many interfaces you've added.`,`Apply the one-implementation test: if IApplicationDbContext has exactly one implementor, delete it and inject the DbContext directly — same for IDbConnectionFactory.`,`Split the layer by intent: 100% of command handlers stay behind repository + unit of work; 100% of query handlers move to Infrastructure/Queries/<domain>.`,`Budget exactly two breaking changes for the move: the assembly-scanned AddMediator registration (re-register in AddInfrastructure) and the handlers' constructor types after deleting the interfaces.`,`Cost comparison: pragmatic = 0 refactor hours but 1 permanent EF Core dependency in the app layer; purist = an afternoon, 2 moved files, 2 deleted interfaces, 0 EF Core references. Choose per project — and write the decision down.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Break the Clean Architecture Rules with EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bi8oRSu-QgU`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-vN_j1Bs0ALU`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:21,title:`Why I Use The Unit of Work Pattern With EF Core | Clean Architecture`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:34`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`CQRS`,`Outbox`],story:{headline:`412 confirmed orders, zero confirmation emails: the night I learned why two SaveChanges calls are one too many`,paragraphs:[`10:00 at peak checkout volume. The pager goes off: 412 orders marked confirmed in the database overnight, and not a single confirmation email sent. Support is asking if the email vendor is down. It isn't. I'm scrolling the outbox table with cold coffee, and for those 412 orders, it is simply empty.`,`The root cause was embarrassingly small. The handler called SaveChanges on the DbContext right after updating the aggregate — then raised the domain event, built the outbox message, and called SaveChanges a second time. A deploy restarted the service between the two flushes. Transaction one committed the order update. Transaction two never ran. The database said 'confirmed', the integration event said nothing, and nothing in our monitoring cared because no exception was ever thrown.`,`The fix wasn't more retries or a reconciliation job. It was making it structurally impossible to split a command's writes in half: every handler persists through one Unit of Work with a single SaveChanges, so the aggregate UPDATE and the outbox INSERT commit atomically or not at all. That's exactly the flow this lesson walks through, line by line, with a real UpdateMember command.`],problem:`Handlers that call SaveChanges directly can split a command's writes across two transactions — the domain change commits, the outbox message doesn't, and integration events silently vanish without a single exception.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`Unit of Work`,`CQRS Handlers`,`Domain Events`,`Outbox Pattern`,`PostgreSQL`,`SQL Server`],note:`EF Core's DbContext is already a unit of work — the pattern here is a thin application-layer interface over it, giving command handlers one flush point where audit stamping and outbox persistence hook into the same transaction.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does a wrapper around SaveChanges even exist?`,body:[`Let me start with the objection I get every time I show this: 'DbContext already IS a unit of work — why wrap it?' And honestly, if you look at my first implementation, I'd half agree. It's a class that injects the DbContext and exposes one SaveChanges method that just forwards the call. Twenty-ish lines. It looks like ceremony for ceremony's sake.`,`But here's what changed my mind: the moment your handlers grow a second concern. The day you need audit timestamps on every write, or domain events turned into outbox messages, you have two options — repeat that logic in every handler and pray, or own a single choke point that every write in the application passes through. The wrapper looks pointless right up until it's the only place you have to change code.`,`There's a Clean Architecture angle too. My command handlers live in the application layer and shouldn't reference EF Core directly. IUnitOfWork is the seam: handlers depend on an abstraction, EF stays behind it in infrastructure, and mocking persistence in tests becomes trivial instead of painful.`],quote:`A wrapper that only calls SaveChanges looks stupid — right up until the day every handler needs to do one more thing.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inject DbContext directly`,`Zero indirection, familiar`,`Handlers couple to EF, no flush hook, hard to mock`,`Prototypes only`],[`IUnitOfWork wrapper`,`One flush point, cross-cutting hooks, mockable seam`,`One more interface to wire up`,`Production Clean Architecture`],[`SaveChangesInterceptor`,`Framework-native, no new interface`,`Hidden magic, ordering surprises, still EF-coupled`,`Org-wide EF conventions`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why I Use The Unit of Work Pattern With EF Core | Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vN_j1Bs0ALU`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: one command, one flush`,body:[`Let's walk the actual request, because this is where it clicks. A PUT comes in, the UpdateMemberCommandHandler fires, and we fetch the member aggregate from the repository. We build FirstName and LastName value objects — no raw strings crossing the boundary — and call member.ChangeName, which checks whether the name actually differs, raises a MemberNameChange domain event, and sets the properties.`,`Then the repository marks the aggregate for update, and the handler makes exactly one persistence call: unitOfWork.SaveChanges. Inside that single flush, EF's change tracker knows the member is modified, and the domain event has already materialized an outbox message — so the UPDATE to members and the INSERT into outbox_messages go out as two statements inside one transaction. One command, one flush, one commit.`,`Now contrast that with the 3 AM story: two SaveChanges calls meant two transactions, and a restart landed between them. The pattern isn't about wrapping EF for fashion — it's about owning the moment your domain hits the database, and making it structurally impossible to split that moment in half.`],quote:`One command, one flush, one transaction — everything else is a partial write waiting to page you at 3 AM.`,table:{headers:[`Flush strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single SaveChanges per command`,`Atomic domain + outbox commit`,`Handler must stage all work first`,`Command handlers`],[`Multiple SaveChanges calls`,`Feels incremental`,`Partial-write crash windows, lost events`,`Avoid in handlers`],[`Implicit save inside repository`,`Handler code shrinks`,`Transaction boundary becomes invisible`,`Simple CRUD only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why I Use The Unit of Work Pattern With EF Core | Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vN_j1Bs0ALU`,at:`6:00`}},{heading:`03 · Hooking auditing into the flush, not the handlers`,body:[`Here's the part of SaveChanges I genuinely love. Before we touch the database, we loop over the change tracker looking for anything implementing IAuditableEntity. If the EntityState is Added, we stamp CreatedOn with the current date and time. If it's Modified — which our member is, right now — we stamp ModifiedOn. Then, and only then, we call the DbContext's real SaveChanges.`,`Why not set these in the handlers? Because handlers are written by humans on a Friday afternoon, and humans forget. The moment audit logic lives in the flush, forgetting becomes impossible: if it's in the tracker, it gets stamped. One foreach, two entity states, one hundred percent coverage — no handler discipline required.`,`This is also where the wrapper earns its keep architecturally. Auditing is an application-level concern, and now it lives in an application-level class instead of being smeared across every command handler or, worse, leaking into the domain entities themselves.`],quote:`If audit columns live in the handler, they will be forgotten; if they live in the flush, they cannot be.`,table:{headers:[`Audit strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Set in each handler`,`Explicit at the call site`,`Forgotten constantly, drifts over time`,`Never`],[`Hook in SaveChanges flush`,`One place, tracker-driven, total coverage`,`Slightly implicit`,`Default choice`],[`Database defaults / triggers`,`DB-enforced CreatedOn`,`ModifiedOn still needs app logic`,`CreatedOn only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why I Use The Unit of Work Pattern With EF Core | Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vN_j1Bs0ALU`,at:`8:50`}},{heading:`04 · The provider surprise: what your UPDATE actually ships`,body:[`Now the surprise I didn't expect until I opened the SQL log. After the flush, SQL Server's UPDATE on the members table didn't just send first_name, last_name, and modified_on — it also shipped email and created_on. Columns I never touched. That's simply how the SQL Server provider behaves: it includes every mapped property in the update statement.`,`The PostgreSQL provider is smarter about this: it figures out which properties actually changed and sends only those columns in the UPDATE. Fewer bytes on the wire, less WAL churn, fewer index touches on columns nobody modified. It's one of the honest, concrete reasons I reach for Postgres on these projects.`,`Does any of this change the pattern? No — the Unit of Work doesn't care which provider sits underneath it. But it's a good reminder that the abstraction leaks at the SQL layer, and it's worth knowing exactly what your database receives on every single flush.`],quote:`Your database only sees what your provider sends — and SQL Server sends everything.`},{heading:`05 · Why not just use interceptors? And where this sits in Clean Architecture`,body:[`Fair question: EF Core has SaveChangesInterceptor, which is framework-native and gives you similar hooks. My issue is visibility. Interceptors fire from the side, and when two interceptors plus an outbox dispatcher all hook into SaveChanges, you start asking ordering questions nobody can answer from reading the handler. With an explicit Unit of Work, the pipeline is code you can step through with a breakpoint — which is exactly how we walked it earlier.`,`As for placement: the IUnitOfWork interface lives in the application layer, right next to my command handlers. The implementation lives in infrastructure, where the DbContext lives. The handler does its domain work, makes one flush call, and returns a success result. That's the entire contract.`,`And let me be honest about scope: if you've got two handlers and no domain events, skip this pattern — seriously, it's ceremony at that point. But the moment events, outbox, and auditing show up, a single flush point stops being ceremony and becomes the thing keeping your database and your integrations telling the same story.`],quote:`The Unit of Work isn't about wrapping EF — it's about owning the exact moment your domain hits the database.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Explicit IUnitOfWork`,`Visible pipeline, mockable seam, clean layering`,`One extra interface`,`Clean Architecture commands`],[`SaveChangesInterceptor`,`Framework-native, zero new interface`,`Hidden ordering, still EF-coupled`,`Org-wide EF conventions`],[`Raw DbContext in handler`,`No indirection at all`,`No flush hook, EF leaks upward`,`Prototypes and spikes`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One flush, one transaction: the UpdateMember request path`,caption:`The full command path — note that the aggregate UPDATE and the outbox INSERT leave through the same flush and commit in the same transaction.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["PUT /api/members"] --> B["UpdateMemberCommandHandler"]
    B --> C["MemberRepository: fetch aggregate"]
    C --> D["Build FirstName / LastName value objects"]
    D --> E["Member.ChangeName"]
    E --> F["Raise MemberNameChange domain event"]
    F --> G["MemberRepository.Update"]
    G --> H["IUnitOfWork.SaveChangesAsync"]
    H --> I["Audit hook: stamp CreatedOn / ModifiedOn"]
    I --> J["DbContext.SaveChangesAsync"]
    J --> K[("Single ACID transaction")]
    K --> L["UPDATE members"]
    K --> M["INSERT outbox_messages"]
    M --> N["Outbox processor publishes event"]
    L --> O["Handler returns success result"]`},{id:`savechanges-internals`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Inside SaveChangesAsync: audit stamping, then one commit`,caption:`The Unit of Work's flush sequence. On SQL Server the UPDATE ships every mapped column; on Postgres, only the changed ones.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant H as Command Handler
    participant U as IUnitOfWork
    participant C as DbContext
    participant DB as Database
    H->>U: SaveChangesAsync()
    U->>C: Read ChangeTracker entries
    loop Entities implementing IAuditableEntity
        alt EntityState = Added
            U->>U: Set CreatedOn = now
        else EntityState = Modified
            U->>U: Set ModifiedOn = now
        end
    end
    U->>C: SaveChangesAsync()
    C->>DB: BEGIN TRANSACTION
    C->>DB: UPDATE members
    C->>DB: INSERT outbox_messages
    C->>DB: COMMIT
    C-->>H: Rows affected
    H-->>H: Return success result`}],takeaways:[`Call SaveChanges exactly once per command handler — two flushes means two transactions, and a crash between them silently orphans your outbox messages.`,`One ~30-line IUnitOfWork class gives every handler auditing, outbox persistence, and a mockable persistence seam for free — write it once, never touch it again.`,`Stamp audit columns in the flush, not the handler: EntityState Added sets CreatedOn, Modified sets ModifiedOn — 100% coverage with zero handler discipline required.`,`Expect SQL Server UPDATEs to ship every mapped column (email and created_on included) while Postgres ships only changed columns — plan row size, WAL churn, and index load accordingly.`,`Domain change + outbox message must commit in the same transaction: one SaveChanges call, two SQL statements (UPDATE + INSERT), zero lost integration events.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why I Use The Unit of Work Pattern With EF Core | Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vN_j1Bs0ALU`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-LhCD5CUSP6g`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:22,title:`EF Core 8 Finally Fixes Value Objects`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:25`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`DDD`],story:{headline:`One Offer, Two Books, and a Hidden Key at 3 AM`,paragraphs:[`We were twenty minutes from flipping the switch on a flash sale. Pricing had built a clean little Offer value object — price, currency, discount percent — and mapped it with OwnsOne. Migration diff looked beautiful: one books table, offer columns tucked right beside the book columns. Staging was green. We shipped.`,`Then the merchandising script ran. It did the textbook DDD thing: took one promo Offer instance and assigned it to every discounted book — one value, many holders. SaveChanges lit up with 'The instance of entity type Offer cannot be tracked because another instance with the same key value for Id is already being tracked.' Thousands of books, one shared instance, one shadow key EF had quietly attached under the hood. Checkout started returning 500s at 03:12.`,`We hot-fixed it the ugly way: deep-clone the offer per book, N clones for N books, redeploy, pray. Six months later EF Core 8 landed complex types and made that entire class of failure structurally impossible — no key, no identity-map entry, just columns.`],problem:`How do you persist a keyless, structurally-equal value object into a relational table when your ORM's change tracker only understands identity?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 8`,`Complex Types`,`Owned Types`,`DDD Value Objects`,`SQL Server`,`EF Migrations`,`Fluent API`],note:`The persistence pieces this talk walks through: EF Core 8's complex types replace owned types as the first-class way to map keyless value objects into the owner's table.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why ORMs and value objects fight`,body:[`Here's the thing I want you to feel before we touch any code: an ORM's change tracker is an identity map. Every object it tracks, it tracks by key. That's how it knows 'this is the same row I loaded five minutes ago' and how it decides what to UPDATE.`,`A value object is the exact opposite of that. Two offers with the same price, currency, and discount ARE the same offer — equality by values, not by identity. So when your domain says 'no key' and your persistence layer says 'show me your key,' you get friction. Everything EF has done with owned types, and now complex types, is just different ways of resolving that fight.`],quote:`The change tracker is an identity map; a value object is a thing with no identity. Something has to give.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Entity`,`Value Object`],rows:[[`Identity`,`Key / ID`,`None — equality by values`],[`Equality`,`By identifier`,`Structural (all properties)`],[`Lifetime`,`Tracked independently`,`Lives and dies with its owner`],[`EF mapping`,`DbSet with a key`,`Owned type or complex type`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 8 Finally Fixes Value Objects`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LhCD5CUSP6g`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: owned types and their hidden key`,body:[`Up to EF Core 7 you had two moves. The first was OwnsOne: EF treats the Offer as part of the Book and persists it in the same table, in extra columns. On the migration diff it looks perfect — one table, no joins.`,`But owned types are entities under the hood. EF quietly attaches a shadow identifier so it can track changes to the owned instance. And that hidden key is exactly what bit us at 3 AM: you cannot take one Offer instance and assign it to two different books — the identity map sees the same key twice and throws.`,`The second move was serializing to and from JSON with ToJson. Great for document-shaped data, but you trade away column-level querying. Pick your poison.`],quote:`Owned types are value objects wearing an entity's clothes — and the hidden key always shows.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Owned type (OwnsOne)`,`Same-table columns, tracked changes`,`Hidden shadow key; no instance sharing`,`Pre-EF8 default`],[`JSON column (ToJson)`,`Flexible document shape`,`No per-column filtering or indexing`,`Semi-structured blobs`],[`Complex type (EF8)`,`Keyless, shareable, real columns`,`Snapshot-based change detection only`,`True value objects`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 8 Finally Fixes Value Objects`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LhCD5CUSP6g`,at:`1:00`}},{heading:`03 · The EF Core 8 fix: ComplexProperty, not OwnsOne`,body:[`EF Core 8 finally gives us complex types, and there are two ways in. Slap the [ComplexType] attribute on the Offer class, or — my preference, because mapping decisions belong to the persistence layer — say modelBuilder.Entity<Book>().ComplexProperty(b => b.Offer). One word changed: ComplexProperty instead of OwnsOne.`,`What changes under the hood is the part that matters: no key. No identity-map entry. EF compares original and current value snapshots to detect changes instead of asking 'which instance is this?' Which means you can finally assign the same Offer instance to a hundred books and nothing throws — because EF stopped pretending it was tracking an entity.`],quote:`Complex types delete the identity, so the change tracker finally stops asking 'who are you?'`,table:{headers:[`Config style`,`Code`,`When I reach for it`],rows:[[`Attribute`,`[ComplexType] on the Offer class`,`The type is a value object everywhere, by definition`],[`Fluent API`,`ComplexProperty(b => b.Offer)`,`The mapping decision belongs to the DbContext, not the domain`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 8 Finally Fixes Value Objects`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LhCD5CUSP6g`,at:`2:21`}},{heading:`04 · Migrations: what actually lands in SQL`,body:[`Add-Migration CreateDatabase, and look at the diff: one table. Books. Your book columns, then the offer columns right beside them — Offer_Price, Offer_Currency, Offer_Discount — named after the C# property. The complex type is flattened into the owner's row.`,`That's the payoff nobody screenshots: because it's real columns, you can WHERE on Offer_Price, ORDER BY it, index it, aggregate it in plain SQL. The row shape is boring in the best possible way — no joins, no JSON parsing, no hidden keys, no surprises at SaveChanges.`],quote:`If it lands as columns, you can index it. If it lands as JSON, you can only hope.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 8 Finally Fixes Value Objects`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LhCD5CUSP6g`,at:`3:08`}},{heading:`05 · The rules I code by now`,body:[`Complex types are not magic, and I want to be blunt about the edges. EF maps the shape; it does not implement Equals or GetHashCode for you. Use a record or write them yourself — otherwise your 'structural equality' is only half true, and equality bugs are the worst kind of silent.`,`Treat them as immutable — swap the whole Offer instance instead of mutating one property — and keep them flat: EF 8 doesn't support collections of complex types or navigations out of them. The moment you need a document shape or a list, that's JSON territory. The moment a thing grows its own identity and lifecycle, promote it to a full entity. Three tools, three shapes of data.`],quote:`EF maps the shape; your domain still owns the semantics.`,table:{headers:[`Situation`,`Reach for`],rows:[[`Keyless, shared, needs column queries`,`Complex type`],[`Document-like, nested, optional shape`,`JSON column`],[`Has its own identity and lifecycle`,`Separate entity`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 8 Finally Fixes Value Objects`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LhCD5CUSP6g`,at:`3:45`}}],diagrams:[{id:`complex-mapping`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Complex type mapping topology`,caption:`One Offer value object, flattened into the Books table via ComplexProperty — no key, no extra table.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Domain["C# Domain Model"]
    B["Book entity<br/>Id, Title"]
    O["Offer value object<br/>Price, Currency, Discount"]
  end
  B -- "ComplexProperty(b => b.Offer)" --> O
  subgraph SQL["SQL Server"]
    T[("Books<br/>Id, Title, Offer_Price,<br/>Offer_Currency, Offer_Discount")]
  end
  O -- "flattened to columns" --> T
  B -- "one row per book" --> T`},{id:`owned-type-failure`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Why owned types blew up at 3 AM`,caption:`The pre-EF8 failure path: one shared Offer instance, one shadow key, two owners — the identity map throws.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant App as Pricing Service
  participant Ctx as EF Core 7 DbContext
  participant Map as Identity Map
  App->>Ctx: promoOffer assigned to Book A
  App->>Ctx: same promoOffer instance on Book B
  App->>Ctx: SaveChanges()
  Ctx->>Map: track Offer with shadow key 1
  Ctx->>Map: track Offer again, same key
  Map-->>Ctx: key already tracked
  Ctx-->>App: InvalidOperationException
  Note over App,Map: 03:12 - flash sale checkout returns 500s`}],takeaways:[`If equality is by values and there's no meaningful key, it's a value object — map it with ComplexProperty, not OwnsOne.`,`One shared instance across 2+ owned-type owners equals 1 guaranteed 'already being tracked' exception; EF 8 complex types make that failure structurally impossible.`,`Complex types flatten to real columns — you keep WHERE filters, ORDER BY, and indexes that a JSON column gives up.`,`EF maps the shape, not the semantics: hand-write Equals/GetHashCode (or use a record) or your structural equality is only half-true.`,`Treat complex types as immutable and replace the whole instance on change; keep them flat — no collections or navigations in EF 8.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 8 Finally Fixes Value Objects`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LhCD5CUSP6g`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-vKJnAojHllw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:23,title:`Clean Up Your EF Core Queries With Specification Pattern`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:13`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`3,400 'You've Earned It' Emails — and Every One of Them Was Wrong`,paragraphs:[`It was 11 PM on a Thursday when the loyalty double-points campaign went live. The eligibility rule was simple enough: account older than six months, no promo claimed in the last 90 days, lifetime spend over $500. That rule lived in PromotionService as a clean, fail-fast chain of if statements — tested, reviewed, boring.`,`What nobody remembered was that the nightly email batch had its own copy of the rule, hand-rolled as a LINQ Where clause in a different project. Two weeks earlier, someone had relaxed the promo cooldown from 90 days to 60 — in the service, and only in the service. The batch query still said 90. By morning, 3,400 customers still inside their cooldown window had received 'you've earned a bonus!' emails, and support had a queue it wouldn't clear for days.`,`The postmortem fix wasn't more tests or another approval gate. It was making the rule a single object — a specification — whose expression EF Core translates to SQL for the batch query, and whose compiled predicate the service invokes for validation. One rule, two consumers, zero drift.`],problem:`The same eligibility rule existed twice — once as if-statements in the service, once as a LINQ predicate in the batch job — and the two copies had silently diverged.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`Specification Pattern`,`Expression Trees`,`LINQ`,`Replacing Visitor`,`Ardalis.Specification`],note:`Expression<Func<T,bool>> is the hinge: EF Core translates it to SQL for queries, Compile() turns it into an in-memory predicate for validation, and a parameter-replacing visitor glues And/Or/Not compositions together.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the rule already lives in two places`,body:[`Let's start where the pain actually starts, not where the pattern starts. Here's a PromotionService with a fail-fast chain of if statements — account age, promo cooldown, lifetime spend. Clean, tested, boring. And about twenty lines away, the nightly batch job re-writes the exact same rule as a LINQ Where clause, because somebody needed it server-side and copy-paste was faster than a design meeting.`,`First instinct, always: just share a Func from Customer to bool. One predicate, two call sites, done. And that is precisely the trap. The moment you hand EF Core a compiled delegate instead of an expression tree, it can't translate it — on older versions you get silent client-side evaluation where the whole table crosses the wire, on newer ones you get an InvalidOperationException. The naive DRY fix is a production incident wearing a nice hat.`,`So the real question becomes: is there a type that behaves like code when I need code, and like data when the query pipeline needs data? Hold that thought — that's the entire talk.`],quote:`A rule that lives in two files is already two rules — you just haven't been billed for the second one yet.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Copy-pasted LINQ + if-checks`,`Zero abstraction`,`Drifts on every rule change`,`Never`],[`Shared Func<T,bool>`,`Single source in memory`,`EF Core can't translate it`,`Avoid for queries`],[`Specification (Expression)`,`One rule, SQL + in-memory`,`Small abstraction tax`,`Default once rule hits 2 places`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Queries With Specification Pattern`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vKJnAojHllw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: one expression, two worlds`,body:[`Here's the hinge of the whole pattern: Expression of Func taking T and returning bool. It's a lambda the compiler hasn't compiled — you get the syntax tree instead of IL. And because it's a tree, two very different consumers can walk it. EF Core's query pipeline walks it and emits SQL. You can walk it too — or just call Compile — and get a real delegate you can invoke in memory.`,`That makes the base Specification type almost embarrassingly small. One abstract member returns the expression — that's the rule as data. One helper, IsSatisfiedBy, compiles the expression and invokes the resulting predicate against a candidate — the same rule as code. Same object, two worlds, and the business rule is written exactly once.`,`One warning from the trenches: Compile is not free. It costs on the order of tens of microseconds; invoking the resulting delegate is nanoseconds. So compile once per batch and reuse the delegate — never compile inside a per-row loop, or you'll pay the JIT tax ten thousand times for nothing.`],quote:`An expression tree is the only C# value that's code and data at the same time — the whole pattern is the discipline of exploiting that.`,table:{headers:[`Form`,`EF translates?`,`Cost profile`,`Use for`],rows:[[`Func<T,bool>`,`No`,`Cheap invoke, no SQL`,`Pure in-memory checks`],[`Expression<Func<T,bool>>`,`Yes`,`Compile once (~µs), invoke cheap`,`Queries + validation`],[`Compile() per row`,`Yes`,`µs tax × N rows`,`Never — cache the delegate`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Queries With Specification Pattern`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vKJnAojHllw`,at:`1:32`}},{heading:`03 · Composing: And, Or, Not — and the visitor tax`,body:[`Real rules aren't atoms; they're combinations. So we add an AndSpecification holding a left and a right, glued together with Expression.AndAlso — OrElse for Or, Expression.Not for negation. Ten lines each, honestly boring, and that's the good part.`,`The boring part ends at the parameter. Each specification declares its own ParameterExpression — think of it as each lambda having its own variable name. Combine them naively and the tree references two unbound parameters, and EF Core fails translation with a parameter-binding error on the very first combined query. The fix is a parameter-replacing visitor that rewrites the right side's parameter to the left side's before you AndAlso them — EF Core actually ships one you can borrow. It's the one genuinely fiddly piece, and it's the piece everyone forgets until 3 AM.`,`Once that visitor exists, you bolt And, Or, and Not helpers onto the base class, and rules start reading like sentences: PromoEligible.And(Not(AlreadyClaimed)). That readability is the actual product you're shipping to the next engineer.`],quote:`Composition is ten lines of AndAlso and one visitor you'll forget you need — until the first combined query blows up.`,table:{headers:[`Composition tool`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Raw AndAlso`,`No extra types`,`Unbound-parameter failures`,`One-off only`],[`Spec + replacing visitor`,`Named, testable, composable`,`Visitor is fiddly`,`3+ combined rules`],[`LINQKit / PredicateBuilder`,`Battle-tested, Invoke-expand`,`Extra dependency`,`Already on LINQKit`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Queries With Specification Pattern`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vKJnAojHllw`,at:`5:03`}},{heading:`04 · Why not just pass expressions around — and where I actually land`,body:[`Fair challenge: why not skip the abstraction and pass the raw expression straight to the repository? You can — for a single call site, that's the right call. But an anonymous predicate has no name, no home for tests, and no way to compose. Six months later you've got predicates defined inline in five controllers, and the drift problem is back, wearing a lambda costume.`,`The opposite extreme is a repository method per variant — GetEligibleForSpringPromo, GetEligibleForBirthdayPromo. Fine until variant twelve, when the combinatorics explode and the repository becomes a junk drawer. And the full framework route — Ardalis.Specification with Includes, paging, AsNoTracking — is genuinely good, but it's a lot of machinery for one predicate.`,`So here's where I land in practice: hand-roll the tiny base class — ToExpression, IsSatisfiedBy, And/Or/Not, roughly 80 lines — the day a rule shows up in two places. The day your specifications need Includes, ordering, or paging, stop hand-rolling and adopt the framework. Match the tool to the size of the problem, not to the size of the blog post.`],quote:`Don't build a cathedral for one predicate — but don't live with copy-paste either; 80 lines is the honest middle.`,table:{headers:[`Stage`,`Signal`,`Move`],rows:[[`1 call site`,`Single query, no reuse`,`Inline the expression, skip the pattern`],[`2+ call sites`,`Rule in query + validation`,`Hand-rolled Specification (~80 lines)`],[`Rich specs`,`Includes, paging, ordering`,`Adopt Ardalis.Specification`],[`Policy sprawl`,`Dozens of evolving rules`,`Rules engine — carefully, and rarely`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Queries With Specification Pattern`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vKJnAojHllw`,at:`6:07`}}],diagrams:[{id:`spec-dual-use`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One specification, two consumers`,caption:`The same expression object feeds the SQL translator and the in-memory validator — drift becomes structurally impossible.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    RULE["One business rule:<br/>PromotionEligibilitySpec"]
    RULE --> EXPR["ToExpression()<br/>rule as data"]
    RULE --> SAT["IsSatisfiedBy(candidate)<br/>rule as code"]
    EXPR --> EF["EF Core query pipeline"]
    EF --> SQL["SQL WHERE clause<br/>translated from the tree"]
    SQL --> DB[("Customers table")]
    DB --> BATCH["Nightly batch:<br/>eligible candidates"]
    SAT --> CMP["Compile() once per batch"]
    CMP --> DELEG["Reusable predicate delegate"]
    DELEG --> SVC["PromotionService:<br/>validate before granting"]
    BATCH --> SVC`},{id:`promo-sweep-sequence`,title:`Nightly promo sweep, one spec both ways`,kind:`mermaid`,caption:`The batch job queries with the expression, then re-validates each candidate with the compiled predicate — one rule, zero drift.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Job as Nightly Batch Job
    participant Svc as PromotionService
    participant Spec as EligibilitySpec
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant DB as Postgres
    Job->>Svc: Run promo sweep
    Svc->>Spec: ToExpression()
    Svc->>EF: Customers.Where(expr)
    EF->>DB: SELECT with translated WHERE
    DB-->>EF: candidate rows
    EF-->>Svc: candidates materialized
    Note over Spec: Compile() once, reuse delegate
    loop each candidate
        Svc->>Spec: IsSatisfiedBy(candidate)
        Spec-->>Svc: true or false
    end
    Svc-->>Job: eligible customers + audit trail`}],takeaways:[`Trigger on duplication: the moment the same predicate appears in 2+ places — a LINQ Where plus an if-chain — extract a specification; before that, inlining is fine.`,`Keep the hand-rolled base class under ~80 lines (ToExpression, IsSatisfiedBy, And/Or/Not); the moment you need Includes, paging, or ordering, adopt Ardalis.Specification instead of growing your own.`,`Compile once per batch, never per row: Compile() costs tens of microseconds versus nanoseconds to invoke, so per-row compilation is roughly a 1000x waste at 10k rows.`,`Never let the expression path cross a ToList() or AsEnumerable() boundary — past that line EF Core can't translate, and you'll silently pull the entire table into memory.`,`Budget for the visitor: every And/Or/Not composition needs a parameter-replacing visitor (EF Core ships one), and skipping it means a parameter-binding failure on your very first combined query.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Queries With Specification Pattern`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vKJnAojHllw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-iKDITShiZy4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:24,title:`Using Separate Read/Write Models with EF Core and CQRS`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:09`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`CQRS`],story:{headline:`The navigation property that broke our domain model at 2 AM`,paragraphs:[`It's 15:10 during a database replica failover on a Saturday and the 'People You May Know' page is timing out. The on-call dashboard shows the connection pool maxed out, CPU pinned on the SQL box, and the top offender is a follower-suggestion lookup that should be a two-table join. Instead, someone three months ago — me — had 'fixed' the query the easy way: I added a public navigation collection to the User aggregate so EF Core could eager-load followers without a fight.`,`That one convenience had consequences nobody saw coming. The rich domain model — private setters, an Email value object with real validation, invariants enforced in constructors — suddenly had a side door. A batch import job materializing users through the now-public setter started writing rows with malformed emails, and the same navigation graph made every read drag half the aggregate across the wire. The model built to protect our writes was poisoning them, and it was still too heavy to make reads fast.`,`By 4 AM the diagnosis was clear: one EF Core model was being asked to be two things at once — a fortress for business rules and a flat, fast projection surface. It can't be both. The fix that followed was almost boring: split the model, not the database.`],problem:`A single EF Core DbContext forces one set of entity classes to serve both command-side invariants and query-side projections — so either your domain model leaks navigations and loses encapsulation, or every read becomes a fight against your own mapping.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`CQRS`,`Rich Domain Model`,`Flat Read Models`,`DbContext Split`,`Configuration Predicate`],note:`Two EF Core DbContexts over one database: the write side maps a rich domain model with value objects, the read side maps flat projection classes, and a type predicate on ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly keeps each context's mappings in its own lane.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why one model can't serve both masters`,body:[`Let me start where the friction actually lives. On the write side we've got a rich domain model — strongly typed properties, value objects like Email that validate themselves, private setters, and EF Core configuration mapping those value objects into columns. This is textbook DDD and it's genuinely great: the business rules live in one place and the compiler helps you keep them there.`,`But the moment you need an efficient query, that same model turns on you. Fast reads want navigation properties and navigation collections — 'just eager-load the graph' — and exposing those on a domain entity is against every domain-modeling best practice we just praised. So you're stuck: bend the model to serve the query, or write painful, slow projections that dance around your own encapsulation.`,`Here's the first thought of this webinar: the problem isn't your modeling skill, it's asking one model to do two jobs. Reads and writes have different shapes, different performance profiles, and honestly different definitions of 'correct'. Once you accept that, the solution is almost mechanical.`],quote:`A rich domain model is a fortress for writes and a maze for reads — stop asking it to be both.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Rich Domain Model`,`Flat Read Model`,`Natural Home`],rows:[[`Enforcing invariants`,`Excellent — value objects, private setters`,`None — nothing to enforce with`,`Write side`],[`Fast projections`,`Fights you at every turn`,`Trivial — shape matches the query`,`Read side`],[`Navigation collections`,`Violates DDD best practices`,`Just columns and keys`,`Read side`],[`Change tracking`,`Essential for the unit of work`,`Pure overhead`,`Write side`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Separate Read/Write Models with EF Core and CQRS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKDITShiZy4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: two contexts, one database`,body:[`Here's how I'd walk the boundary in your codebase today. Take your existing DbContext and rename it — ApplicationWriteDbContext. Don't rename references yet, just the class. It keeps referencing the rich domain entities and their configuration classes; nothing about the write side changes except its name now tells the truth.`,`Then create the read side: a second context referencing a parallel set of flat read model classes — same tables under the hood by default, so no data duplication and no sync problem. But — and this is the flexibility you're buying — you're free to point the read context at different tables, views, or projections later without ever touching the write model.`,`Organizationally, split the entity configurations into Write and Read folders. That folder structure looks cosmetic right now, but hold that thought — it becomes the load-bearing wall in the next section.`],quote:`Same tables, two contracts — the database doesn't care, your code definitely does.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Separate Read/Write Models with EF Core and CQRS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKDITShiZy4`,at:`0:23`}},{heading:`03 · The configuration collision (and the predicate that saves you)`,body:[`Now the part that bites everyone. If you're using ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly — and you should be, it's the clean way to wire configurations — introducing a second context breaks it. The scan picks up every IEntityTypeConfiguration in the assembly, which means your write context would happily apply read-model configurations and vice versa. Silent mapping chaos.`,`The obvious fix is manual registration: ApplyConfiguration(new UserConfiguration()), one line per entity. It works, it's explicit, and it's tedious — you're back to editing two places every time you add an entity, and someone will eventually forget.`,`Here's the clever bit: the second argument of ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly is a predicate over the types being scanned. Define a static filter — WriteConfigurationsFilter — that checks the type's FullName contains your write namespace segment, pass it in, and do the mirror image for the read context. The assembly scan keeps working, and your folder names from the last section just became the partition key.`],quote:`Don't register configurations by hand — teach the scanner to read your folder names.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Manual ApplyConfiguration`,`Explicit, zero magic`,`Tedious, drifts as entities grow`,`Tiny models only`],[`Predicate-filtered assembly scan`,`Automatic, partitioned by namespace`,`Relies on naming discipline`,`Most applications`],[`One shared context`,`Single source of truth`,`The exact problem we're solving`,`Don't`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Separate Read/Write Models with EF Core and CQRS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKDITShiZy4`,at:`1:05`}},{heading:`04 · Why not Dapper? Why not two databases?`,body:[`Let's do the 'why not X' round, because someone in chat is already typing it. Why not Dapper or raw SQL for reads? Honestly — fine. But you give up LINQ composition, compiled query reuse, and one mapping stack. Flat read models with EF keep the whole team on one toolchain while still being projection-shaped.`,`Why not full CQRS with separate read and write databases? That's the real deal — denormalized read store, events or an outbox to sync, eventual consistency to reason about. Powerful, and overkill until your read traffic proves otherwise. The two-contexts-one-database pattern is the pragmatic middle: strong consistency, one migration story, zero sync infrastructure, and a clean seam to upgrade later.`,`And notice the pattern scales in one direction only, and that's a feature. If reads outgrow the shared tables, the read context is already isolated — point it at replicas, views, or a different store entirely, and nothing on the write side moves.`],quote:`CQRS is a spectrum — start at the end that doesn't need a message broker.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Two contexts, one DB`,`Strong consistency, no sync, one migration story`,`Shared table scaling limits`,`Default choice`],[`Dapper / raw SQL reads`,`Maximum control, very fast`,`Two query stacks, no LINQ reuse`,`Hot spots only`],[`Two DBs + event sync`,`Independent scaling, denormalized reads`,`Eventual consistency, infra cost`,`Read-heavy scale`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Separate Read/Write Models with EF Core and CQRS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKDITShiZy4`,at:`1:55`}},{heading:`05 · Where it breaks: migrations, drift, and tracking tax`,body:[`Before we wrap, the failure modes — because this pattern has exactly three ways to rot. First: migrations. Exactly one context owns the schema, and it's the write side. Two migration histories against one database is how you end up with a read context 'helpfully' dropping a column the write model still needs.`,`Second: drift. Read models must stay projections — keys, flat columns, nothing else. The moment a read model grows a method or a business rule, it's becoming a second domain model, and now you have two sources of truth. Push back on that PR.`,`Third: tracking. The read context loads data nobody will ever save, so make AsNoTracking your default — context-level if you can. Skip that and you're paying the change-tracking tax on every dashboard query, which is exactly the tax we started this talk trying to stop paying.`],quote:`The read model is a projection, not a junior domain model — the moment it grows behavior, cut it back.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Separate Read/Write Models with EF Core and CQRS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKDITShiZy4`,at:`3:36`}}],diagrams:[{id:`cqrs-two-contexts-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two EF Core contexts, one database`,caption:`Commands flow through the rich domain model, queries flow through flat read models, and each context applies only its own configurations via a namespace predicate.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph App["Application Layer"]
        CMD["Command Handlers"]
        QRY["Query Handlers"]
    end
    subgraph WS["Write Side - Rich Domain Model"]
        WCTX["ApplicationWriteDbContext"]
        WENT["Rich Entities + Value Objects"]
        WCFG["Write Configurations Folder"]
    end
    subgraph RS["Read Side - Flat Read Models"]
        RCTX["ApplicationReadDbContext"]
        RDTO["Flat Read Model Classes"]
        RCFG["Read Configurations Folder"]
    end
    TBL[("Users / Followers Tables")]
    CMD --> WCTX
    QRY --> RCTX
    WCFG -.->|applies to| WCTX
    RCFG -.->|applies to| RCTX
    WCTX -->|maps| WENT
    RCTX -->|maps| RDTO
    WCTX --> TBL
    RCTX --> TBL`},{id:`config-predicate-scan`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Partitioning the assembly scan with a predicate`,caption:`Startup-time configuration discovery: the predicate argument filters the scan so the write context never sees read-model mappings, and vice versa.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant App as App Startup
    participant W as ApplicationWriteDbContext
    participant S as Assembly Scan
    participant R as ApplicationReadDbContext
    App->>W: OnModelCreating
    W->>S: ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly(types, WriteConfigurationsFilter)
    S->>S: keep types where FullName contains ".Write."
    S-->>W: write-side configurations only
    Note over W: rich entities mapped, invariants intact
    App->>R: OnModelCreating
    R->>S: ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly(types, ReadConfigurationsFilter)
    S->>S: keep types where FullName contains ".Read."
    S-->>R: read-side configurations only
    Note over R: flat read models mapped, no domain leakage`}],takeaways:[`Split into two DbContexts the moment a query tempts you to expose a navigation or bypass a value object — that temptation is the trigger, not slow SQL.`,`Point both contexts at the same tables first; only split tables, views, or stores when read traffic measurably demands it.`,`Use the ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly predicate (e.g. FullName.Contains(".Write.")) to partition configurations — never hand-register IEntityTypeConfiguration classes one by one.`,`Let exactly one context own migrations (the write side); two migration histories against one schema is how drift starts.`,`Keep read models flat: one class per query shape, keys and columns only, no value objects, no behavior, and AsNoTracking as the read-side default.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Separate Read/Write Models with EF Core and CQRS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKDITShiZy4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`event-sourcing-cqrs`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-IGVRVO7KTss`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:25,title:`Why You Don't Need To Abstract Away EF Core With Clean Architecture`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 10:56`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Repository That Ate Two Sprints (And Never Swapped a Database)`,paragraphs:[`It's 11:40 on a Thursday, three weeks before launch, and we need one query: a filtered bulk status update on stale orders that the original author never imagined. I open our 'clean' data layer and find IOrderRepository with exactly four methods — GetById, Add, Update, Delete — each one a one-line pass-through to a DbSet. The new requirement doesn't fit through those four doors, so we add a fifth. Then a sixth. By Friday the repository is just a worse DbContext with extra steps and a 60-comment code review thread.`,`The kicker lands at 2 AM. The one handler that genuinely needs raw EF power — a concurrency token, a bulk update, a split query — can't get any of it through the interface, so somebody casts the repository back to the concrete context: (context as ApplicationDbContext). The abstraction built to 'swap databases someday' had never swapped anything, but it successfully hid every feature we needed on the database we actually had.`,`When I rebuilt the service, I went the opposite way. EF Core's DbSet already is a repository and the DbContext already is a unit of work — the classic patterns are in there, free of charge. So I wrote one thin IApplicationDbContext interface in the application layer, implemented it in persistence over Npgsql, and let handlers touch DbSets directly. Two sprints of ceremony deleted, zero regressions, and the boundary I actually care about — domain in, providers out — still holds.`],problem:`How do you honor Clean Architecture's dependency rule without paying the repository tax — and where, exactly, does the EF Core leak into your application layer start and stop?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`MediatR`,`Postgres`,`Npgsql`,`IApplicationDbContext`,`Strongly-Typed IDs`],note:`MediatR fronts the use cases, EF Core (core package only) is referenced straight from the application layer, and a thin IApplicationDbContext interface — implemented by the real DbContext over Npgsql in persistence — keeps the dependency arrow pointing inward.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: is a repository over a DbSet just a toll booth?`,body:[`Let's start with the uncomfortable question: what is a repository, actually? In EF Core, a DbSet already gives you Add, Find, and a LINQ endpoint that translates to SQL. The DbContext tracks changes and commits them as one unit. The repository and unit-of-work patterns are already implemented in there — wrapping them means wrapping a wrapper.`,`And here's what that wrapper costs you. Either you leak IQueryable through the interface — at which point callers write queries your 'abstraction' can't control — or you enumerate inside and eat client-side evaluation, the classic N+1 generator. Meanwhile every EF feature you actually want, from bulk updates to split queries to concurrency tokens, has to be re-exposed method by method. I've watched teams spend two sprints building this and then punch a hole in it anyway.`,`So my first thought, out loud: don't abstract away the thing that is already the abstraction. The real question isn't 'repository or not' — it's 'what seam do I actually need for the dependency rule and for testing?' And that seam can be one interface, not a whole layer.`],quote:`A repository with exactly one implementation isn't an abstraction — it's a toll booth on the way to your data.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Raw DbContext in handlers`,`Fastest to ship, full EF power`,`Concrete class couples app layer to persistence`,`Spikes, tiny services`],[`IApplicationDbContext interface`,`One file, keeps the DI seam, near-zero ceremony`,`DbSet still leaks EF types into app layer`,`Most greenfield .NET services`],[`Generic repository + unit of work`,`Hides persistence, tidy handlers`,`Pass-through methods, blocks EF features, second abstraction to maintain`,`Large teams needing strict seams`],[`CQRS: Dapper read side`,`Hand-tuned SQL for hot paths`,`Two data stacks to learn and staff`,`Read-heavy reporting paths`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why You Don't Need To Abstract Away EF Core With Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IGVRVO7KTss`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what the dependency rule actually protects`,body:[`Now let's walk the boundary the way the dependency rule actually states it: dependencies point inward. Domain references nothing. Application references Domain. Infrastructure — persistence, API, hosting — references Application. Notice what's missing from that rule: any clause saying 'and Application must not know an ORM exists.' That's a tradition, not the rule.`,`The pragmatic move is a Data folder in the Application project with one interface, IApplicationDbContext, exposing DbSet of Customer and DbSet of Order — just the sets this slice of the system needs. The Persistence project implements it with the real ApplicationDbContext, configures UseNpgsql and the connection string, and the arrow points inward. Why not just move ApplicationDbContext itself into Application? Because then provider packages, migration tooling, and connection-string plumbing all live in the wrong layer, and your swap story dies the moment Npgsql shows up in a using directive.`,`And let's be honest about the leak: DbSet is an EF type, so Application references Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore. That's the trade, and I'm fine with it — the core package is provider-agnostic. The day I care is the day the Npgsql provider itself appears, and my rule keeps that from ever happening.`],quote:`The dependency rule is an arrow, not a force field — draw the boundary where the pain actually is.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why You Don't Need To Abstract Away EF Core With Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IGVRVO7KTss`,at:`1:02`}},{heading:`03 · The handler, out loud: one command, one transaction`,body:[`Let's build the use case out loud. Feature folder Orders/Create, and the command is a record: CreateOrderCommand carrying a CustomerId. The API hands us a raw Guid, and the very first thing at the boundary maps it into a strongly-typed CustomerId — because a Guid is just a number until the type system knows what it means.`,`Decorate it with IRequest — the no-response flavor, because creating an order isn't a query — and the handler implements IRequestHandler for that command. Inside: FindAsync the customer, and if it exists, create the order and save. That's the whole handler. One use case, one transaction, and the only layer it crosses is the one that matters.`,`Why records? Because a command is immutable data — value semantics, one line of code, and the compiler stops me from mutating a request mid-pipeline. Why MediatR? Not for the hype — for the pipeline: validation, logging, and transactions become behaviors I hang around every handler instead of copy-pasting into each one.`],quote:`Parse, don't validate: once a Guid has become a CustomerId, it can never impersonate an OrderId.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why You Don't Need To Abstract Away EF Core With Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IGVRVO7KTss`,at:`2:16`}},{heading:`04 · The DI wiring that bites at 3 AM`,body:[`Now the part that actually bites. In Persistence: AddDbContext for ApplicationDbContext with options.UseNpgsql and configuration.GetConnectionString("Database"). Then register IApplicationDbContext to ApplicationDbContext as scoped and... on some .NET versions, that simple overload just doesn't resolve. You get an 'unable to resolve service' error at startup — or worse, at first request under load.`,`The reliable fix is the overload that hands you the IServiceProvider: register the interface with a factory that calls GetRequiredService for ApplicationDbContext. Why does this matter beyond making DI happy? Because it forwards the interface to the same scoped instance as the concrete class. Get this wrong and a single request carries two DbContexts — two change trackers, two transactions, and a save that silently doesn't include the entity you just added. That's a 3 AM bug wearing a Sunday-afternoon disguise.`,`One more trap while we're here: never register the interface with a different lifetime than the context. Scoped for both, always — or the 'same instance' guarantee evaporates and you're debugging phantom saves for a week.`],quote:`Two DbContexts in one request isn't redundancy — it's two change trackers quietly disagreeing.`,table:{headers:[`Registration`,`What happens`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`AddScoped interface to concrete`,`Usually resolves; can fail on some .NET versions`,`Try first`],[`Factory overload + GetRequiredService`,`Forwards interface to the same scoped DbContext instance`,`The reliable fix`],[`Different lifetime for the interface`,`Interface and context split into separate instances per scope`,`Never — split-brain risk`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why You Don't Need To Abstract Away EF Core With Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IGVRVO7KTss`,at:`9:26`}},{heading:`05 · When I'd still reach for the full abstraction`,body:[`Let me be fair to the other side, because 'it depends' is only useless when it's unprincipled. I'd reach for a fuller abstraction in three cases: a second store is genuinely on the roadmap — scheduled, not fantasy; the domain is aggregate-heavy and I want repositories to enforce aggregate boundaries so handlers can't grab arbitrary tables; or the team is large enough that a strict seam is a coordination device rather than a ceremony tax.`,`And testing? People reach for repositories to make handlers mockable, but I've mostly stopped mocking what EF already lets me test — SQLite or Testcontainers against the real thing catches more bugs than a mocked interface that returns whatever I told it to return. Mock the seam when the seam is real; don't build a seam just to have something to mock.`,`So here's the line I draw, and I'd love to hear where yours is: Application may reference Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore — the core package, provider-agnostic — but never Npgsql, never UseNpgsql, never a connection string. That single rule buys me most of what the repository layer promised, at a fraction of the cost.`],quote:`Abstract for the requirement you have, not for the database you might someday hire.`,table:{headers:[`Signal`,`Thin interface`,`Full repository layer`],rows:[[`One store, CRUD-shaped handlers`,`Yes — enough`,`No — ceremony`],[`Second store genuinely scheduled`,`Yes`,`Maybe, per aggregate`],[`Rich DDD aggregates`,`Yes`,`Yes — enforce boundaries`],[`Hot reporting queries`,`Yes + raw SQL via EF`,`Separate read stack`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`dependency-boundary`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Where the dependency arrows actually point`,caption:`The pragmatic Clean Architecture layout: Application defines IApplicationDbContext, Persistence implements it — the arrow still points inward, and the Npgsql provider never leaks past Persistence.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    API["API Layer<br/>Controllers"] -->|"MediatR.Send"| APP["Application Layer<br/>CreateOrderCommandHandler"]
    APP -->|"references entities"| DOM["Domain Layer<br/>Customer · Order"]
    APP -->|"depends on"| IFACE["IApplicationDbContext<br/>Data folder · exposes DbSets"]
    PERS["Persistence Layer<br/>ApplicationDbContext"] -->|"implements"| IFACE
    PERS -->|"references"| DOM
    PERS -->|"UseNpgsql + connection string"| PG[("Postgres")]`},{id:`create-order-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The CreateOrder request path, end to end`,caption:`One command, one handler, one transaction: the handler checks the customer through the interface, then creates the order — no repository layer in between.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as API Controller
    participant M as MediatR Pipeline
    participant H as CreateOrderCommandHandler
    participant I as IApplicationDbContext
    participant EF as EF Core + Npgsql
    participant PG as Postgres
    API->>M: Send CreateOrderCommand customerId
    M->>H: Handle command
    H->>I: Customers.FindAsync id
    I->>EF: LINQ translated to SQL
    EF->>PG: SELECT customer
    PG-->>EF: row or empty
    EF-->>H: Customer or null
    alt Customer exists
        H->>I: Orders.Add new order
        H->>I: SaveChangesAsync
        I->>EF: INSERT order in one transaction
        EF->>PG: BEGIN INSERT COMMIT
        PG-->>EF: 1 row affected
    else Customer not found
        H-->>API: throw not found
    end`}],takeaways:[`Delete any repository where 80%+ of methods are one-line DbSet pass-throughs — DbSet already is the repository, and DbContext already is the unit of work.`,`Keep IApplicationDbContext to only the DbSets your use cases actually touch; split it the moment it grows past ~10 sets.`,`Application may reference Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore (core package), never the provider — UseNpgsql, migrations, and connection strings stay in Persistence 100% of the time.`,`Register the interface via the service-provider overload (GetRequiredService) so every request gets exactly one DbContext — two instances means two change trackers and phantom save bugs.`,`Cost check: the thin-interface route is ~30 minutes up front and trivially reversible; a full repository layer costs days up front and, in my experience, grows an escape hatch within two sprints of a real requirement.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why You Don't Need To Abstract Away EF Core With Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IGVRVO7KTss`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Bz5JCbWnaHo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:26,title:`Generic Repository Pattern With EF Core - Why It Sucks`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 9:34`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Night Two Repositories Committed Half an Order`,paragraphs:[`It's the Friday before Black Friday and checkout latency is climbing. Support tickets stack up: orders stuck in 'processing' while loyalty points were already deducted. At 2 AM the on-call engineer finds the smoking gun in the logs — a checkout handler that inserts an order, runs a loyalty validation, and on failure calls SaveChangesAsync through the customers repository... which happily commits the order insert too.`,`Why? Because IRepository<Customer> and IRepository<Order> are both scoped services wrapping the same ApplicationDbContext. Change tracking lives on the context, not the repository, so 'which repository saves' was never a real question — any of them saves everything. The team had spent a week building a generic repository to make data access 'cleaner,' and it had quietly erased their transactional boundaries.`,`The second wound was self-inflicted: a reporting feature needed a projection none of the six generic methods could express, so someone added GetQueryable() to the interface 'temporarily.' Two sprints later, half the handlers were composing raw LINQ against the exposed DbSet. The abstraction existed only in code review comments — and the fix wasn't a better generic repository. It was deleting it.`],problem:`A generic IRepository<T> over EF Core charges you the full cost of an abstraction and delivers none of the control: it leaks IQueryable, makes SaveChanges ownership ambiguous across a shared DbContext, and hides nothing you actually needed hidden.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`Generic Repository`,`IQueryable`,`DbContext`,`IUnitOfWork`,`DDD Aggregates`],note:`The cast on trial: a generic IRepository<T> wrapping EF Core's DbContext, the GetQueryable escape hatch that kills the abstraction, and the DDD-style specific repository plus IUnitOfWork that replaces it.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: you're wrapping a wrapper`,body:[`Let's build the thing first so we can be fair to it. IRepository<T> goes in the application layer: GetAllAsync returning Task<List<T>>, GetByIdAsync taking a Guid, plus Insert, Update, Delete and SaveChangesAsync. Register it with AddScoped, implement it in the persistence project by injecting the ApplicationDbContext, and every method is a one-liner — context.Set<T>().ToListAsync(), FindAsync for the ID lookup, Add/Update/Remove for the writes. Add a class constraint on T so DbSet<T> compiles, and you're done in twenty minutes.`,`Now the uncomfortable question: what exactly did we abstract? EF Core's DbContext is already an implementation of the Unit of Work and Repository patterns — those patterns exist to hide persistence mechanics, and the context already does that job. Our generic repository's public surface is a mirror of the context's, method for method. When an abstraction exposes exactly what it wraps, it's not an abstraction, it's ceremony with extra steps.`,`And the pattern has no good answer to 'why not just use EF Core directly?' The usual defenses — 'we might swap ORMs someday' and 'it makes unit testing easier' — don't survive contact with reality, as we're about to see.`],quote:`DbContext already is a repository and a unit of work — wrapping it again is indirection, not abstraction.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`DbContext directly`,`Full power, zero ceremony`,`Couples handlers to EF`,`Small apps, prototypes`],[`Generic repository`,`Uniform CRUD API`,`Leaks IQueryable, ambiguous saves`,`Simple CRUD services`],[`Specific repositories`,`Intent-revealing, domain-shaped`,`More code per aggregate`,`DDD, complex domains`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Generic Repository Pattern With EF Core - Why It Sucks`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bz5JCbWnaHo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the IQueryable escape hatch`,body:[`Sooner or later you hit a query those six methods can't express — a projection, a filter across navigation properties, a paged report. The canonical move is to add a GetQueryable method to the interface and return the DbSet. The implementation is literally one line: return context.Set<T>(). And that one line is the entire abstraction dying in real time.`,`Once IQueryable<T> is public, callers compose arbitrary LINQ, which means your repository contract now includes every query anyone will ever write against it. You can't optimize centrally, can't log consistently, can't swap the ORM — callers are writing expression trees directly against EF's provider. Deferred execution then plants ambushes: queries enumerated after the context is disposed, or filters that silently fall back to client-side evaluation.`,`Be honest about what the interface guarantees at this point: nothing. The repository was supposed to be the boundary where persistence decisions live. With IQueryable exposed, the boundary is decorative — and the honest design is to inject the DbContext and skip the middleman entirely.`],quote:`An abstraction you can bypass isn't a boundary — it's a suggestion.`,table:{headers:[`Leak symptom`,`What it costs`],rows:[[`Callers compose LINQ`,`ORM swap becomes impossible`],[`Deferred execution`,`Disposal and evaluation surprises`],[`No central query home`,`Perf fixes scattered across handlers`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Generic Repository Pattern With EF Core - Why It Sucks`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bz5JCbWnaHo`,at:`2:29`}},{heading:`03 · The SaveChanges identity crisis`,body:[`Now the fun part. Register IRepository<Customer> and IRepository<Order> as scoped services; both inject the same ApplicationDbContext. In a checkout handler you call customersRepo.GetByIdAsync, ordersRepo.Insert, and then — which repository calls SaveChangesAsync? Either one, because both work. That's not flexibility, that's a bug factory.`,`Change tracking lives on the DbContext, not on the repository. So SaveChangesAsync on the orders repository commits every tracked change — including the customer mutation you made through the other repository. There's no per-repository transaction, no ownership, no boundary. Two repositories, one save, everything commits together whether you meant it or not.`,`The fix isn't cleverer generic plumbing. It's removing SaveChanges from repositories entirely and giving commit ownership to exactly one place — an IUnitOfWork abstraction, a pipeline behavior, or the handler itself. One save per unit of work, one owner, an explicit transaction boundary you can actually reason about.`],quote:`SaveChanges on a shared context isn't scoped to the caller — it commits the whole room.`,table:{headers:[`Commit ownership`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`SaveChanges on each repo`,`Feels convenient`,`Ambiguous owner, partial commits`,`Never`],[`IUnitOfWork abstraction`,`One explicit save point`,`One more interface`,`Most apps`],[`Handler-owned context`,`Zero indirection`,`Couples handlers to EF`,`Small apps`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Generic Repository Pattern With EF Core - Why It Sucks`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bz5JCbWnaHo`,at:`6:21`}},{heading:`04 · What DDD gets right: specific repositories`,body:[`Here's my actual position: the generic repository is an anti-pattern, but the specific repository — the DDD flavor — is genuinely valuable. One repository per aggregate root, interface in the domain layer, intent-revealing methods like GetOpenOrdersForCustomer instead of GetAllAsync. It returns aggregates, only what the domain needs, and never a raw IQueryable.`,`The shape changes too: no SaveChangesAsync on the repository. 'How do I fetch this aggregate' and 'when do we commit' are two different questions, so they get two different abstractions — the repository for fetching, IUnitOfWork in the application layer for committing. The generic repository conflates them, and that conflation is exactly what produced the 2 AM incident.`,`The payoff is real: the interface documents actual use cases, query tuning happens in one place, and the domain layer stays honest about what persistence offers it. You write a bit more code per aggregate and get an actual seam in return.`],quote:`A repository should speak your domain's language, not the database's.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Generic IRepository of T`,`Specific aggregate repository`],rows:[[`Methods`,`CRUD plus SaveChanges`,`Intent-revealing, use-case shaped`],[`Returns`,`List of T, IQueryable of T`,`Aggregates only`],[`Commit ownership`,`Ambiguous`,`IUnitOfWork in app layer`],[`Query tuning`,`Scattered across callers`,`Centralized in implementation`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Generic Repository Pattern With EF Core - Why It Sucks`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bz5JCbWnaHo`,at:`7:38`}},{heading:`05 · So when does a generic repo not suck?`,body:[`To be fair, there's a narrow band where the generic repository is fine: pure CRUD admin screens, internal tools, scaffolding-heavy services where every access really is get-by-id, list, save — and you've consciously decided EF Core is a permanent dependency. There it's a harmless uniformity layer, and the team probably knows it.`,`The tripwire is the first custom query. The moment someone reaches for GetQueryable, a specification interface, or a second SaveChanges caller, the abstraction has stopped paying rent. That's your signal to either go direct to the DbContext or graduate to specific repositories — decide deliberately, not by default.`],quote:`Patterns don't fail loudly — they fail the day the first custom query shows up.`}],diagrams:[{id:`generic-repo-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Generic repository topology — and where it leaks`,caption:`Two repositories, one shared DbContext, and the GetQueryable escape hatch that routes callers straight past the abstraction.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    H[Command Handler] --> RC[IRepository Customer]
    H --> RO[IRepository Order]
    RC --> C1[Repository Customer]
    RO --> C2[Repository Order]
    C1 --> Ctx[(Shared DbContext)]
    C2 --> Ctx
    Ctx --> DS1[DbSet Customer]
    Ctx --> DS2[DbSet Order]
    H -. escape hatch .-> Q[GetQueryable Order]
    Q -.-> DS2
    H -. ambiguous save .-> SC[SaveChangesAsync]
    SC -.-> Ctx`},{id:`shared-context-save-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The 2 AM half-commit, step by step`,caption:`One DbContext behind two repositories: a save through either one commits every tracked change — including the ones made through the other.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant H as Checkout Handler
    participant CR as IRepository Customer
    participant OR as IRepository Order
    participant C as Shared DbContext
    H->>CR: GetByIdAsync customerId
    CR->>C: Set Customer FindAsync
    H->>OR: Insert new order
    OR->>C: Set Order Add
    H->>OR: SaveChangesAsync
    C-->>H: Commits order AND customer mutation
    Note over C: One context - any repo save commits everything`}],takeaways:[`If your repository exposes IQueryable<T>, delete it — callers are already coupled to EF Core, so inject DbContext directly and save the indirection.`,`Never put SaveChangesAsync on a repository: with a shared DbContext, any repo's save commits every repo's changes — one IUnitOfWork, one save per request.`,`Audit your handlers: if more than ~20% bypass the repository via GetQueryable or specifications, the abstraction is dead — remove it or redesign it around real use cases.`,`Prefer specific repositories per aggregate root with intent-revealing methods — target 5 or fewer methods per repository, each mapping to a real domain use case.`,`A generic repository is only defensible for pure CRUD screens with zero custom queries; the first projection or cross-entity query is your refactor trigger.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Generic Repository Pattern With EF Core - Why It Sucks`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bz5JCbWnaHo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-us44l4WJ7Fo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:27,title:`Service Discovery in Microservices With .NET and Consul`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:25`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`Consul`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`08:45 as mobile traffic surged: the reporting API called a ghost`,paragraphs:[`It's 13:20 right after the schema migration and the nightly newsletter job is paging: every call to the reporting API fails with connection refused. The weird part? The reporting API itself is perfectly healthy — clean logs, green dashboard, just sitting there. The address we were calling wasn't.`,`The afternoon before, someone scaled the ordering service to a second replica and the Docker Compose file reshuffled container ports. The reporting API's appsettings.json still held a hardcoded URL — pointing at a container that had been recycled hours earlier. Classic config drift, discovered by pager.`,`The fix that night was a one-line config change. The fix that mattered was admitting the truth: in a system where services are born and die at runtime, a physical address in a config file is a lie waiting to happen. We spent the next sprint wiring in Consul — services register under logical names, clients resolve at request time, and health checks evict the dead.`],problem:`Hardcoded physical addresses cannot survive a system where services are created, destroyed, and scaled at runtime — the config and the cluster quietly drift apart until something pages you at 3 AM.`},tech:{stack:[`Consul`,`ASP.NET Core`,`Docker Compose`,`Typed HttpClient`,`Health Checks`],note:`Consul is the central service registry mapping logical names to physical addresses; ASP.NET Core typed clients resolve at request time, with Docker Compose hosting Consul and health checks keeping the catalog honest.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why hardcoded URLs fall over`,body:[`Let's start where the pain starts. A microservices system is dynamic — services get created, destroyed, and scaled out all day long, and they need to talk to each other. The naive answer is: hardcode the physical addresses of your downstream services in config. That works... until Tuesday, when the ordering service gets a second replica and you suddenly have two physical addresses and one config entry.`,`And it compounds. Managing a handful of addresses is annoying; managing dozens is a job in itself. Scale-out means each downstream service can have multiple physical addresses — which one do you call? Config drift means the address in appsettings and the address in the cluster quietly disagree, and you find out from a 2 AM page. So let's define the problem precisely: service URLs can change over time, and our code treats them like constants.`,`The fix isn't smarter config — it's removing physical addresses from config entirely.`],quote:`A hardcoded URL is a bet that your topology will never change — and dynamic systems change constantly.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Hardcoded URLs`,`Zero setup`,`Breaks on scale-out, config drift`,`Never (beyond a demo)`],[`Env-var config`,`Slightly flexible`,`Still static, drifts at scale`,`Tiny static systems`],[`Plain DNS`,`Built into the platform`,`No health awareness, caching hides change`,`Slow-changing workloads`],[`Service registry (Consul)`,`Dynamic, health-aware, logical names`,`One more moving part to run`,`Dynamic microservice fleets`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Service Discovery in Microservices With .NET and Consul`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=us44l4WJ7Fo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: logical names vs physical addresses`,body:[`Here's the pattern. We introduce a central service registry — in our case a Consul instance — that manages the addresses of every microservice in the system. Each downstream service registers its physical address under a logical name. 'ordering-service' isn't a real address; it's a name the registry can resolve.`,`Now flip the flow around. When the client — which could just as easily be another microservice inside our system — wants to call the ordering service, it doesn't need to know where it lives. It performs a lookup against the registry using the logical name, gets back a physical URL, and sends the network request there. Register on the way in, resolve on the way out.`,`What I love about this is the vocabulary shift. The client speaks in logical addresses — product service, search service, ordering service — and the registry owns the ugly reality of IPs and ports. You can move, scale, and redeploy services all day long, and the logical contract never changes.`],quote:`Logical names are the contract; physical addresses are an implementation detail.`,table:{headers:[`Registration model`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Self-registration`,`Simple, no extra agent`,`Lies if the process hangs`,`Default starting point`],[`Health-check verified`,`Evicts dead instances fast`,`Needs a check endpoint`,`Production services`],[`Third-party / platform`,`Orchestrator is source of truth`,`Coupled to the platform`,`Kubernetes or VM fleets`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Service Discovery in Microservices With .NET and Consul`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=us44l4WJ7Fo`,at:`2:48`}},{heading:`03 · Why a registry without health checks is a lie`,body:[`Here's the trap everyone hits in week two: the registry says the reporting service exists, but the reporting service is dead. Registration is a claim, not a fact. If a container crashed five minutes ago and never deregistered, every lookup hands you a ghost — exactly what happened to us at 17:00 as cross-region traffic shifted.`,`That's why health checks are non-negotiable. In the Consul UI you can click into a service instance and see its health checks and metadata — our reporting API shows green because its check is actually passing. When a check fails, Consul stops handing out that address, and with deregistration configured, the instance leaves the catalog entirely. The registry stays truthful.`,`Rule of thumb: any instance whose health check fails for roughly a minute should not be reachable through discovery. If it still is, you don't have service discovery — you have a slowly rotting phone book.`],quote:`A registry without health checks is a phone book of ghosts.`,table:{headers:[`Check type`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`HTTP endpoint`,`Verifies app logic, not just the port`,`Needs an endpoint`,`ASP.NET Core services`],[`TCP check`,`Dead simple`,`Port open ≠ app healthy`,`Legacy or opaque services`],[`TTL check`,`App controls its own heartbeat`,`A hung app can forget to tick`,`Background workers`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Service Discovery in Microservices With .NET and Consul`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=us44l4WJ7Fo`,at:`9:17`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring it into .NET: typed clients that resolve, not hardcode`,body:[`Now the .NET side. We run Consul in a Docker Compose container — hashicorp/consul, exposing the default port 8500 — and refactor the reporting API's typed HttpClient. Before, its base address was a hardcoded URL that had already drifted. After, it holds the logical name 'reporting-api', and resolution happens at request time.`,`You can watch it work in the Swagger UI: hit the endpoint, break inside the typed client, inspect the HttpClient instance — the base address is the logical name, and by the time the request hits the wire it's a real physical URL that Consul resolved for us. The code never learned an IP address. That's the entire refactor.`,`The honest question: why not just DNS, or Kubernetes services? If you're fully on Kubernetes, use what the platform gives you. Consul earns its keep on VMs, mixed infrastructure, or when you want health-aware resolution with rich metadata across environments. Pick the boring option your platform already provides — but never go back to hardcoded URLs.`],quote:`Make resolution a runtime concern, not a line in appsettings.`,table:{headers:[`Mechanism`,`Health-aware`,`Runs outside K8s`,`Best fit`],rows:[[`Consul`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`Mixed VM + container fleets`],[`K8s Services`,`Yes`,`No`,`Kubernetes-native teams`],[`Plain DNS`,`No`,`Yes`,`Static, slow-changing systems`],[`API Gateway`,`Partial`,`Yes`,`North-south traffic only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Service Discovery in Microservices With .NET and Consul`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=us44l4WJ7Fo`,at:`15:22`}}],diagrams:[{id:`discovery-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Discovery topology: register, look up, call`,caption:`Three downstream services register physical addresses under logical names; the client resolves 'ordering-service' through Consul before sending the real request.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Fleet["Downstream services"]
        P["Product Service"]
        S["Search Service"]
        O["Ordering Service"]
    end
    R[("Consul :8500")]
    C["Client / Reporting API"]
    P -- "register physical address" --> R
    S -- "register physical address" --> R
    O -- "register physical address" --> R
    R -- "health checks" --> P
    R -- "health checks" --> S
    R -- "health checks" --> O
    C -- "1. lookup 'ordering-service'" --> R
    R -- "2. physical URL" --> C
    C -- "3. HTTP request" --> O`},{id:`lookup-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path with a Consul lookup`,caption:`The service registers with a health check; the typed client resolves the logical name at request time and only then dials the physical address.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant App as .NET Typed Client
    participant Reg as Consul Registry
    participant Svc as Ordering Service
    Svc->>Reg: Register "ordering-service" + health check
    loop every 10s
        Reg->>Svc: HTTP health check
        Svc-->>Reg: 200 OK
    end
    App->>Reg: Resolve "ordering-service"
    Reg-->>App: Physical URL of a healthy instance
    App->>Svc: GET /api/orders
    Svc-->>App: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`If any service runs more than one replica, hardcoded URLs are already wrong — treat every physical address in config as a latent bug.`,`One logical name per service, registered with a health check interval of 15s or less, so a dead instance disappears from lookups within about a minute.`,`Cache resolved addresses for seconds, not minutes — failover should land in the next refresh cycle, not the next deploy.`,`Typed clients should hold logical names only; if an IP or port still appears in appsettings.json, the refactor isn't done.`,`Run Consul as a 3-node cluster in production (a single container on :8500 is fine for dev) — the registry sits on the critical path of every request.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Service Discovery in Microservices With .NET and Consul`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=us44l4WJ7Fo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`service-discovery`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-SR-G_U9H2tg`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:28,title:`From Modular Monolith to Microservices (Incremental Architecture Evolution)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:11`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`Green builds, silent queues: the night the in-memory bus stopped being real`,paragraphs:[`It was 1:47 AM and the extraction was 'done.' We had lifted the Shipping module out of the eShop monolith into its own ASP.NET Core service on .NET 9, dropped it into docker-compose, fixed every NuGet reference, and the build was green. Both containers booted. The dashboard showed two healthy services. We went to bed feeling like architects.`,`By 3 AM the support channel was on fire: orders were being placed, but nothing was shipping. I pulled the logs and found the ugliest kind of bug — the kind where everything works, just not together. Each service was still wired to its own MassTransit in-memory bus. Orders published OrderCreated into a bus that lived and died inside the Orders process. Shipping sat there, listening to a bus that had never heard that message. Two processes, two private buses, zero messages crossed.`,`That's the moment this talk is really about: the day your logical boundaries become physical, and every 'it's just a method call' assumption gets audited by the network. The fix was a real broker — RabbitMQ — plus a named HttpClient for the one synchronous seam we kept. But the lesson came first: the modularity we practiced in-process is the only reason this migration was possible at all.`],problem:`An in-memory MassTransit bus cannot cross a process boundary — after extraction, OrderCreated integration events published in the Orders API never reached the Shipping API, forcing a real broker (RabbitMQ) and an explicit HTTP contract to reconnect the seam.`},tech:{stack:[`Modular Monolith`,`ASP.NET Core`,`.NET 9`,`MassTransit`,`RabbitMQ`,`Named HttpClient`,`Docker Compose`,`OpenTelemetry`,`Aspire Dashboard`,`NuGet Contracts`],note:`The migration runs on .NET 9 / ASP.NET Core, with MassTransit's in-memory bus exposed as the trap and RabbitMQ as the broker that replaces it. Docker Compose hosts the two extracted services, while OpenTelemetry exports distributed traces to the Aspire dashboard.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: you earn microservices, you don't start with them`,body:[`Let me show you the system before anyone said the word 'distributed.' One deployable, two modules — Orders and Shipping — and one golden rule: modules never reach into each other's internals. If Orders needs something from Shipping, it calls Shipping's public API, a class library that delegates into the internal implementation and hands the response back. That's it. No shortcuts, no 'just this once' queries against someone else's tables.`,`Why not jump straight to microservices? Because the public API pattern is doing invisible work right now. It's a compiler-enforced rehearsal for the network. Every call that already flows through a contract instead of a private method is a call that won't need a redesign on extraction day. The modular monolith isn't a consolation prize — it's the training ground where boundaries get strong enough to survive physical separation.`],quote:`A boundary you respect in-process is the only one you can trust across the network.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Big ball of mud`,`Zero upfront design, fast start`,`Every change risks everything, no extraction path`,`Never`],[`Modular monolith`,`One deployable, enforced public APIs, cheap refactors`,`Requires discipline, shared failure domain`,`Most teams, most products`],[`Microservices day one`,`Independent deploys, isolated scaling`,`Network tax paid before boundaries are understood`,`Only with known, stable boundaries`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — From Modular Monolith to Microservices (Incremental Architecture Evolution)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SR-G_U9H2tg`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the brute-force extraction`,body:[`Here's the part nobody glamorizes: the extraction itself is almost boring, and that's the point. Create a new ASP.NET Core Web API project — eShop.Shipping.API on .NET 9 — then literally drop the Shipping module's folder out of the monolith and into it. Rename the old host to eShop.Orders.API so the names tell the truth. Reference the public API class library from the new service, fix the NuGet packages you carried over, and watch the build. When it goes green, the logical boundary just became physical.`,`One decision worth pausing on: how do you distribute those public API contracts? If all your services live in one solution, a plain project reference is fine. The moment you split into multiple solutions, contracts ship as NuGet packages — versioned, consumable, and now something you have to think about releasing. That's the moment your 'internal convention' becomes a real API with a version number, so treat it like one.`],quote:`Extraction is just promotion: the public API goes from convention to contract.`,table:{headers:[`Contract distribution`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Project reference`,`Zero setup, always in sync`,`Couples build times, single solution only`,`One-solution repos`],[`NuGet packages`,`Versioned, cross-solution, explicit releases`,`Publish overhead, stale-version drift`,`Multi-solution / multi-team`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — From Modular Monolith to Microservices (Incremental Architecture Evolution)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SR-G_U9H2tg`,at:`6:08`}},{heading:`03 · The in-memory bus betrayal`,body:[`Now the interesting problem. Inside the monolith, MassTransit's in-memory message bus was perfect — fast, zero infrastructure, and integration events like OrderCreated flowed between modules without a broker in sight. But the in-memory bus is, by definition, in one process. After the split we had two processes, each hosting its own private bus. Orders published OrderCreated into its own memory; Shipping listened on a bus that never received anything. No error, no exception — just silence.`,`So the rule is brutal and simple: the moment you have two processes, the in-memory bus is dead to you. You need a real message broker, and RabbitMQ is the natural next rung — MassTransit swaps its transport from in-memory to RabbitMQ largely through configuration, which is exactly why we picked MassTransit in the first place. The abstraction you chose 'prematurely' in the monolith is the one that saves you at 3 AM.`],quote:`An in-memory bus in a distributed system is a diary — nobody else ever reads it.`,table:{headers:[`Transport`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-memory bus`,`Zero ops, instant, ideal in one process`,`Cannot cross process boundaries, lost on restart`,`Modular monolith only`],[`RabbitMQ broker`,`Durable, cross-process, config-level swap with MassTransit`,`Real infrastructure to run and monitor`,`First step to distributed`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — From Modular Monolith to Microservices (Incremental Architecture Evolution)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SR-G_U9H2tg`,at:`10:01`}},{heading:`04 · The sync seam: named HttpClients and the shipping-info endpoint`,body:[`Not everything can be an event. Shipping sometimes needs an answer right now — so we kept one synchronous seam and made it explicit. On the Orders side we exposed an endpoint at /orders/{orderId}/shipping-info that injects the same IOrderService public API we had in the monolith, reads from the local database context, and returns either the shipping info or a 404. Same implementation, new address. The boundary didn't change shape; it changed medium.`,`On the Shipping side, we register a named HttpClient pointed at the Orders service. The base address comes from configuration — the docker-compose service name and the internal port 8080 — so 'where is Orders?' is an environment answer, not a hardcoded one. That little bit of indirection is what lets the same image run on your laptop and in the cluster without a rebuild.`],quote:`Every HTTP call you keep is a promise you now make across a network — put the address in config, not in code.`,table:{headers:[`Communication style`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Sync HTTP (named client)`,`Immediate answer, simple request/response`,`Runtime coupling, latency and failure propagate`,`Reads that need an answer now`],[`Async integration event`,`Loose coupling, survives downtime, natural audit trail`,`Eventual consistency, more moving parts`,`State changes like OrderCreated`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — From Modular Monolith to Microservices (Incremental Architecture Evolution)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SR-G_U9H2tg`,at:`13:52`}},{heading:`05 · Observability before the first cross-service call`,body:[`Last thing, and I beg you not to skip it: the moment you have two services, a stack trace stops being enough. We added container orchestrator support in docker-compose for the new Shipping API, exposed its ports, pointed both services at the same connection strings — and critically, wired the OpenTelemetry environment variables so both services export distributed traces to the Aspire dashboard. One request now touches two processes; the trace is the only artifact that shows the whole journey.`,`Think of it as insurance you buy before the crash, not after. When an OrderCreated event doesn't arrive, the difference between a five-minute diagnosis and a five-hour one is whether you can see the publish, the broker hop, and the consume as one connected trace.`],quote:`In a distributed system, the trace is your only honest narrator.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — From Modular Monolith to Microservices (Incremental Architecture Evolution)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SR-G_U9H2tg`,at:`17:34`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology-before-after`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Elevating logical boundaries into physical ones`,caption:`Same two modules, same public API contracts — but the in-process calls and in-memory bus become HTTP and RabbitMQ the moment the process splits.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Before["Before · Modular Monolith — one process"]
    direction LR
    OM["Orders Module"] -->|"public API call"| SM["Shipping Module"]
    SM -->|"public API call"| OM
    OM -.->|"in-memory bus"| SM
    OM --> DB[("Shared Database")]
    SM --> DB
  end
  subgraph After["After · Distributed System — two processes"]
    direction LR
    OA["Orders API"] -->|"GET /orders/:id/shipping-info"| SA["Shipping API"]
    OA -->|"publish OrderCreated"| MQ[["RabbitMQ"]]
    MQ -->|"consume OrderCreated"| SA
    OA --> ODB[("Orders DB")]
    SA --> SDB[("Shipping DB")]
  end
  Before ==>|"extract + promote boundary"| After`},{id:`runtime-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One shipping-info read, one OrderCreated event`,caption:`The synchronous seam rides a named HttpClient configured from docker-compose service discovery; the asynchronous seam rides the broker that replaced the in-memory bus.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant SA as Shipping API
  participant OA as Orders API
  participant MQ as RabbitMQ
  participant ODB as Orders DB

  C->>SA: GET /orders/:id/shipping-info
  SA->>OA: GET /orders/:id/shipping-info (named HttpClient)
  OA->>ODB: query shipping info
  ODB-->>OA: OrderShippingInfo row
  OA-->>SA: 200 OK / 404 Not Found
  SA-->>C: 200 OK / 404 Not Found

  Note over OA,MQ: order placed
  OA->>MQ: publish OrderCreated integration event
  MQ-->>SA: deliver OrderCreated
  SA->>SA: prepare shipment`}],takeaways:[`Run modules behind public APIs for at least 3–6 months in-process before extracting — a boundary that survived the compiler is the only one that survives the network.`,`Budget roughly 1 day per module for the mechanical split (new project, package fixes, docker-compose) and 2–3 days for the messaging seam — the broker swap is where the real work hides.`,`Replace the in-memory bus the moment you have 2 processes: 0 integration events will cross a process boundary without a real broker like RabbitMQ.`,`Distribute contracts as NuGet packages the day you split solutions — project references are a single-solution luxury, and an unversioned contract is a bug waiting to ship.`,`Wire OpenTelemetry and a trace dashboard before your first cross-service call: with 2 services, one missing trace turns a 5-minute diagnosis into a 5-hour one.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — From Modular Monolith to Microservices (Incremental Architecture Evolution)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SR-G_U9H2tg`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-z3piPJ7x4WU`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:29,title:`How to Structure a Modular Monolith Project in .NET`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 15:58`,minutes:16,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`DDD`],story:{headline:`The Foreign Key That Fused Three Modules at 3 AM`,paragraphs:[`It's 3 AM and a routine migration — renaming a column on the Users table — has just taken down Training and Notifications with it. Nobody touched those modules. But eight months earlier, someone had 'just this once' joined Training's invitations table straight onto Users to skip writing one more integration event. The join compiled. The tests passed. And the boundary everyone believed in existed only in a slide deck.`,`This is the classic modular monolith trap: the separation is logical, not physical. You ship one executable — a single ASP.NET Core Web API — against one database. Nothing stops module A from reaching into module B's tables except the discipline you bake into the solution structure itself. We spent four hours that night unwinding a dependency that should never have existed, and I spent the next morning rewriting the layout so the compiler and the CI pipeline enforce the walls instead of code review heroics.`,`What came out of that rewrite is the structure I'll walk you through today: modules that each own their endpoints, domain, and infrastructure; integration events as the only things allowed to cross a boundary; outbox and inbox tables instead of shared joins; and a self-installing module system where the host just wires everything up and gets out of the way.`],problem:`A modular monolith gives you one executable and one database — so nothing physically prevents modules from coupling, and without deliberate structure your 'modules' quietly melt into one big ball of mud.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET`,`ASP.NET Core`,`MediatR`,`FastEndpoints`,`PostgreSQL`,`Outbox Pattern`,`Inbox Pattern`,`Domain Events`,`Integration Events`,`Result Pattern`,`Module Installers`],note:`One executable ASP.NET Core host over a single PostgreSQL database; each module owns its endpoints, application, and domain, with an infrastructure project acting as the module's composition root, and outbox/inbox tables carrying integration events as the only cross-module traffic.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just one deploy' isn't the point`,body:[`Let's get the definition out of the way fast: a modular monolith is a monolith designed around interchangeable modules. One executable, one web API, one database. The keyword is modules — but here's the part nobody says out loud: because the separation is only logical, this architecture is maybe 20% project structure and 80% self-restraint.`,`So why not microservices from day one? Because you'd be paying for network latency, distributed transactions, and serious operational overhead to solve a problem you don't have yet. And why not a classic layered monolith? Because layers cut vertically through every feature — a change to user registration ripples across the data, domain, and service layers of everything. Modules cut horizontally: each one owns its slice end to end.`,`The trade I'm making is simple: accept that everything deploys together, in exchange for boundaries I can actually enforce today and extract tomorrow. That's the whole bet.`],quote:`A modular monolith is a microservices migration you haven't paid for yet.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Layered monolith`,`Simple, familiar, fast to start`,`Layers couple every feature together`,`Small CRUD apps`],[`Modular monolith`,`One deploy, real boundaries, extraction path`,`Needs enforced discipline`,`Most product teams`],[`Microservices`,`Independent deploys and scaling`,`Distributed everything, high ops cost`,`Large orgs with proven seams`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Structure a Modular Monolith Project in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z3piPJ7x4WU`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: domain events vs integration events`,body:[`Here's the distinction that makes or breaks the entire design. Inside the Users module, registering a user raises a UserRegistered domain event after the use case completes. That event lives and dies inside the module — it's handled by the module's own handlers, and no other module has ever heard of it.`,`The moment another module needs to know, the domain event handler translates it into a UserRegistered integration event and pushes it through the outbox. Integration events are the module's public API — they are the only things allowed to leave. If Training needs to react to a new user, it subscribes to the integration event, never to the domain event, and never to the Users tables.`,`Why so strict? Because the day you extract Users into its own service, everything that touched its internals breaks, and everything that consumed its integration events keeps working. The boundary you draw in code today is the boundary you'll draw in the network later.`],quote:`Domain events are your module's diary; integration events are its passport.`,table:{headers:[`Aspect`,`Domain Event`,`Integration Event`],rows:[[`Scope`,`Inside one module`,`Crosses module boundaries`],[`Payload`,`Rich domain objects`,`Stable, versioned contract`],[`Transport`,`In-process mediator`,`Outbox, relay, inbox`],[`Coupling`,`Publisher owns its handlers`,`Publisher knows only the contract`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Structure a Modular Monolith Project in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z3piPJ7x4WU`,at:`5:23`}},{heading:`03 · The outbox and inbox: one database, shared nothing`,body:[`We have exactly one PostgreSQL database, but each module owns its own tables. Users has its user tables plus an outbox. Training has trainers, invitations, and clients, plus its own outbox and inbox. Notifications is the simplest module in the system — it has no internal tables of its own; it just consumes integration events through its inbox and sends the appropriate notification.`,`Why all the ceremony? Because 'we're in the same database, let's just join' is exactly how you get the 3 AM story I opened with. The outbox means an event is committed in the same transaction as the state change — no dual-write bugs. The inbox means consumers deduplicate and process events reliably. And the relay is the only traffic cop between modules.`,`Could we skip it and call MediatR directly across modules? Technically yes, and it works great until module B's handler throws and takes down module A's request. The outbox turns a fragile synchronous coupling into a durable, retryable, observable one.`],quote:`Same database, shared nothing — not even a table.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Cross-module join`,`Fastest to write`,`Invisible coupling, migration landmines`,`Never`],[`Direct in-process call`,`Simple and synchronous`,`Runtime coupling, no retry story`,`Read-only queries at most`],[`Outbox + inbox`,`Transactional, retryable, decoupled`,`Eventual consistency, more tables`,`Default for cross-module`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Structure a Modular Monolith Project in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z3piPJ7x4WU`,at:`10:35`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring it up: every module installs itself`,body:[`So where do all these services actually get registered? Each module's infrastructure project acts as its composition root. I define an IModuleInstaller interface with one implementation per module, and at startup the host discovers every installer via assembly scanning and runs it. Inside, it's mostly ordinary dependency injection organized into a service installers folder.`,`The beauty of this is that the host project knows almost nothing. Add a new module, drop in its installer, and it lights up. Delete a module and nothing else references it. That's the 'interchangeable' part of interchangeable modules actually being true instead of just a slide in a deck.`],quote:`The host doesn't assemble the modules — it just gives them the stage.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Structure a Modular Monolith Project in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z3piPJ7x4WU`,at:`12:07`}},{heading:`05 · Endpoints, mediator, and the result chain`,body:[`At the edge, each module defines its own endpoints — separate from the host — using a class-per-endpoint library. The endpoint's job is deliberately tiny: accept a request, map it to a command or query, send it through the mediator, and convert the Result object into an ActionResult. Route, Swagger metadata, response types, and any permission requirements live right on the endpoint class.`,`Inside the handler I lean on a railway-style result chain: each step flows visibly into the next, and any step can short-circuit by mapping to an explicit failure — nothing downstream executes. Side effects like database writes happen along the chain, the domain event fires at the end, and the endpoint never needs to know whether step two or step four failed.`,`Authorization is declared on the endpoint too, so each module's security surface is visible at its edge — you can audit a module's permissions without reading a single handler.`],quote:`The endpoint translates, the handler decides, and the result chain keeps it honest.`,table:{headers:[`Failure Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Exceptions for flow`,`Familiar to everyone`,`Invisible failure paths, stack-trace tax`,`Truly exceptional cases`],[`Result chain`,`Explicit, composable, testable`,`More ceremony per step`,`Use-case logic`],[`Centralized validation`,`One place to look`,`Blind to use-case context`,`Cross-cutting input checks`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`modular-monolith-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Modular Monolith Solution Topology`,caption:`One executable host, one PostgreSQL database — but each module owns its layers, its tables, and talks only through integration events.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph HOST["Single Executable - ASP.NET Core Web API"]
        subgraph USERS["Users Module"]
            UE["Endpoints<br/>FastEndpoints + MediatR"]
            UD["Application + Domain<br/>User Entity - Domain Events"]
            UI["Infrastructure<br/>IModuleInstaller + Outbox"]
            UE --> UD
            UD --> UI
        end
        subgraph TRAINING["Training Module"]
            TE["Endpoints"]
            TD["Application + Domain<br/>Trainers - Invitations - Clients"]
            TI["Infrastructure<br/>Outbox + Inbox"]
            TE --> TD
            TD --> TI
        end
        subgraph NOTIF["Notifications Module"]
            NE["Event Consumers"]
            NI["Inbox<br/>Sends Notifications"]
            NE --> NI
        end
    end
    PG[("Single PostgreSQL Database")]
    UI --> PG
    TI --> PG
    NI --> PG
    UI -.->|integration events| TI
    UI -.->|integration events| NI`},{id:`register-user-outbox-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Register User: Command to Cross-Module Notification`,caption:`The full journey: endpoint translates to a command, the handler commits state and outbox event in one transaction, and the relay delivers the integration event to the Notifications inbox.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant E as RegisterUser Endpoint
    participant M as MediatR Pipeline
    participant H as RegisterUser Handler
    participant DB as Users Tables
    participant OB as Users Outbox
    participant RL as Outbox Relay
    participant IB as Notifications Inbox
    participant NS as Notification Sender
    participant MB as User Mailbox
    C->>E: POST /users/register
    E->>M: Send RegisterUser command
    M->>H: Handle command
    H->>DB: Insert user
    H->>OB: Append UserRegistered event
    Note over H,OB: Same transaction
    H-->>M: Result success
    M-->>E: Result
    E-->>C: 201 Created
    RL->>OB: Poll pending events
    OB-->>RL: UserRegistered integration event
    RL->>IB: Publish to inbox
    IB->>NS: Dispatch event
    NS->>MB: Send welcome email`}],takeaways:[`One module per 2–3 engineers: if a module needs eight people touching it in a sprint, it's two modules wearing a trench coat.`,`Keep each module's public API under ~10 integration events — more than that and the boundary is leaking internals.`,`Zero cross-module joins: enforce it with an architecture test that fails the PR in under 30 seconds, not a wiki page nobody reads.`,`Every publishing module gets an outbox and every consuming module gets an inbox, written in the same transaction as the state change — no dual writes, ever.`,`Run the extraction test: you should be able to pull any module into its own service in under two weeks; if you can't, the modularity is cosmetic.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Structure a Modular Monolith Project in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z3piPJ7x4WU`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-exPjdlMof1k`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:30,title:`Why Shopify Rejected Microservices (And What They Did Instead)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 15:57`,minutes:16,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The Eleven-Line Fix That Spent Black Friday in the Test Queue`,paragraphs:[`It's 9:47 PM on the Friday we rehearse Black Friday, and a pricing bug — a rounding error that compounds on multi-line carts — shows up in staging. The fix is eleven lines. My teammate has it written, reviewed, and ready in twenty minutes. Then we hit 'Run tests.'`,`Our suite had grown past 100,000 tests, and because billing, checkout, shipping, and orders all reached into each other's tables, a change anywhere meant running everything. Ninety-four minutes later the build went green — and in that window, rehearsal traffic surfaced the same rounding bug in two other modules that had quietly copied the broken logic, because nothing stopped them from reaching into pricing internals directly.`,`We spent the night untangling it, and the postmortem question wasn't 'why did the bug happen.' It was 'why could any module touch any other module's data without asking?' That's the exact question Shopify answered with a developer survey, three options on the table, and a very deliberate rejection of microservices.`],problem:`When every module can reach into every table, how do you enforce boundaries without paying the microservices tax — and when module B owns the data that module A needs, what's the right way to ask?`},tech:{stack:[`.NET`,`MVC`,`Rails Monolith`,`Modular Monolith`,`Module Boundaries`,`Domain Events`],note:`Shopify's starting point was a layered Rails monolith; the .NET equivalent is MVC controllers and views with modules carved out behind enforced boundaries. Public module APIs and in-process domain events are the mechanics that keep one database, one deployable, and zero network hops between modules.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just go microservices?`,body:[`So you've got a tangled monolith, and the internet's default answer since about 2015 has been 'microservices.' Let's steelman it for a second: independent deployments, per-service scaling, teams owning their slice end to end. Sounds like freedom. But count the tax before you sign. Every service needs its own deployment pipeline — that's N pipelines to build, monitor, and secure. Every cross-service call now pays a network hop: serialization, latency, retries, timeouts. Things that were function calls become distributed-systems problems.`,`Then there's the data. Microservices usually come with a database per service, and most of the time that's genuinely good — it enforces the boundary at the strictest possible level. But it also means you can't easily query across domains. 'Show me every order with its billing status' goes from a JOIN to a saga of API calls and eventual consistency. Shopify looked at exactly this trade-off and said no.`,`And here's the kicker: if anyone on Earth had a 'we need microservices to scale' excuse, it's the company peaking around 500 million requests per minute on Black Friday. They rejected them anyway. That should make all of us pause before splitting a 40-thousand-line CRM into fourteen services.`],quote:`Microservices trade a code problem for an operations problem — only pay that bill when your org chart, not your architecture diagram, demands it.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Layered monolith`,`Simple to start, one deploy`,`Boundaries erode into a big ball of mud`,`Prototypes, small teams`],[`Modular monolith`,`One deploy, enforced boundaries, in-process speed`,`Requires discipline and tooling`,`Most product teams`],[`Microservices`,`Independent deploys, per-service scaling`,`N pipelines, network latency, siloed data`,`Large orgs with team-scale boundaries`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Shopify Rejected Microservices (And What They Did Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=exPjdlMof1k`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Coupling isn't the enemy — unowned coupling is`,body:[`Now let's clear up a misconception, because people get this wrong in both directions. Coupling itself is not the enemy. A system with zero coupling isn't a system — it's a folder of disconnected scripts. If checkout doesn't affect orders and orders don't affect billing, what is your software even doing? The goal was never zero coupling. The goal is coupling with ownership.`,`What Shopify had was the other thing: coupling without boundaries. Billing, shipping, checkout, orders — three million lines of code where any module could reach into any other module's tables and internals, because at some point somebody was in a hurry. That's the big ball of mud. And here's my working theory: every monolith drifts toward one of two attractors — the majestic monolith or the mud ball — and the only thing that decides which one you get is whether boundaries are enforced by the compiler or by good intentions.`,`Good intentions don't survive your next reorg. The compiler does.`],quote:`A boundary that a code review can talk you out of was never a boundary.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Shopify Rejected Microservices (And What They Did Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=exPjdlMof1k`,at:`1:28`}},{heading:`03 · The survey, the 100,000 tests, and three doors`,body:[`Here's what I love about how Shopify handled this: they didn't argue about it on a whiteboard — they asked the people living in the code. They surveyed their developers, and the consensus was blunt: the architecture itself was causing friction. Not a feature, not a bug, not a bad quarter. The architecture.`,`The symptoms were textbook. Onboarding a new engineer meant learning a sprawl of moving parts, because with no enforced boundaries, 'how does checkout work?' has no short answer. And they were carrying more than 100,000 tests — which, to be clear, is a treasure if they're high-quality tests — but because everything touched everything, any change meant running the world, and the suite was slow enough to be a daily tax on every change.`,`So they had three doors: live with the mud, break it into microservices, or keep the monolith but carve it into real modules with enforced boundaries. That third door — the modular monolith — is the one almost nobody talks about, even though it's usually the right one.`],quote:`When your engineers tell you the architecture hurts, that's not complaining — that's telemetry.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`What it fixes`,`What it costs`,`Shopify's call`],rows:[[`Do nothing`,`Nothing`,`Debt compounds, slow tests, slow onboarding`,`Rejected`],[`Microservices`,`Deploy independence`,`N pipelines, latency on every call, siloed data`,`Rejected`],[`Modular monolith`,`Enforced boundaries without the network tax`,`Demands discipline and tooling`,`Chosen`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Shopify Rejected Microservices (And What They Did Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=exPjdlMof1k`,at:`3:35`}},{heading:`04 · 'But what if I need data another module owns?'`,body:[`Alright, the question you actually came here for: module A needs data, module B owns it. In a distributed system this is a nightmare — network calls, contracts, versioning, eventual consistency. In a modular monolith it's almost embarrassingly simple, and that's the whole point. You're in one process, against one database. So when Checkout needs order data, it does not SELECT from the orders tables. It calls the Orders module's public API — an in-process method call with a contract. No HTTP, no serialization, no retry policy. It's a function call that happens to respect an ownership line.`,`For the reactive side — 'tell Shipping when an order is placed' — you publish a domain event in-process and let Shipping subscribe and react on its own schedule. You get the decoupling people chase with message brokers, without standing up a broker. And the rule that keeps this from rotting: read through the owner's contract, write through the owner's API, integrate through events. The moment someone writes a cross-module JOIN 'just this once,' you've rebuilt the big ball of mud with a module system bolted on top.`,`Yes, the shared database means the temptation is always one tab away. That's exactly why the boundary has to live in the code — internal by default, one public surface per module — so the compiler catches the reach-in, not the 3 AM incident.`],quote:`Shared database, never shared tables — ask the owner, don't reach into their schema.`,table:{headers:[`Need`,`Anti-pattern`,`Modular monolith move`,`Cost`],rows:[[`Read another module's data`,`Cross-schema JOIN or direct SELECT`,`In-process call to owner's public API`,`One method call, no network`],[`React to another module's change`,`Polling the owner's tables`,`Subscribe to domain events`,`Eventual consistency`],[`Report across domains`,`Ad-hoc cross-module queries in app code`,`Owned read models / reporting surface`,`Some duplication, but isolated`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Shopify Rejected Microservices (And What They Did Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=exPjdlMof1k`,at:`5:03`}},{heading:`05 · The ceiling was never the monolith`,body:[`Let's put numbers on the ceiling, because 'monoliths don't scale' is a claim, and claims deserve receipts. Shopify's core: roughly three million lines of code, running as one modular monolith. Last Black Friday: more than six billion dollars in sales. Requests per minute peaked around five hundred million. And their databases absorbed more than fifty million requests per second at peak.`,`Read those numbers again. Every excuse we give for splitting a system — throughput, team velocity, deploy independence — Shopify hit those walls and answered them inside a monolith with enforced modules. And for us in .NET, nothing here is exotic: controllers, views, and layered structure are still first-class in .NET 10. What Shopify built isn't a technology you can install — it's a discipline you enforce. The monolith was never the ceiling. The mud was.`],quote:`Shopify's Black Friday is the receipt: monoliths don't cap your scale — mud does.`,table:{headers:[`Metric`,`Shopify at peak`],rows:[[`Codebase size`,`~3M lines of code`],[`Black Friday sales`,`$6B+`],[`Peak requests per minute`,`~500M`],[`Peak database requests per second`,`50M+`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Shopify Rejected Microservices (And What They Did Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=exPjdlMof1k`,at:`5:44`}}],diagrams:[{id:`modular-monolith-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Modular Monolith Topology`,caption:`One deployable, one process, one database — but every schema and every interaction is owned by exactly one module.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    Client["Storefront / API clients"] --> LB["Load balancer"]
    LB --> Host["Single deployable host"]
    subgraph Monolith["One process - enforced module boundaries"]
        direction TB
        Checkout["Checkout module"]
        Orders["Orders module"]
        Billing["Billing module"]
        Shipping["Shipping module"]
    end
    Host --> Checkout
    Checkout -- "in-process public API" --> Orders
    Checkout -- "in-process public API" --> Billing
    Orders -- "domain events" --> Shipping
    subgraph DB["Shared database - owned schemas"]
        direction TB
        S1[("orders schema")]
        S2[("billing schema")]
        S3[("shipping schema")]
    end
    Orders --> S1
    Billing --> S2
    Shipping --> S3`},{id:`cross-module-data-request`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Cross-Module Data: Ask, Don't Reach`,caption:`Checkout needs order data: a synchronous in-process call through the owner's public API for reads, an in-process domain event for propagation — never a cross-schema JOIN.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Checkout module
    participant O as Orders module
    participant DB as Shared database
    participant E as In-process event bus
    participant S as Shipping module
    C->>O: GetOrderSummary(orderId) - in-process call, no network
    O->>DB: SELECT from orders schema (owner only)
    DB-->>O: Order row
    O-->>C: OrderSummary contract DTO
    C->>E: publish OrderPlaced
    E->>S: deliver async
    S->>DB: INSERT into shipping schema
    Note over C,S: Reads via the owner's contract - propagation via events - never a cross-schema JOIN`}],takeaways:[`Budget the microservices tax before you buy it: N services means N deployment pipelines plus a network hop on every cross-service call — Shopify kept one deployable and still peaked around 500M requests per minute.`,`Split your test suite by module boundary: if an eleven-line pricing fix triggers 100,000+ tests, your suite is telling you your modules are fake.`,`Enforce boundaries with the compiler, not conventions: exactly one public API surface per module, everything else internal — a boundary a reviewer can wave through is not a boundary.`,`Never JOIN across module schemas: read via the owner's in-process public API, propagate via domain events — ask, don't reach.`,`Use Shopify as your ceiling: ~3M lines of code, $6B+ in one Black Friday, and 50M+ database requests per second on a modular monolith — if you're struggling well below that, fix the mud, not the monolith.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Shopify Rejected Microservices (And What They Did Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=exPjdlMof1k`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-0ZrjLEIBRWw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:31,title:`Modular Monolith Architecture: The Missing Guide to Public APIs`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:46`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`3 AM: a one-line rename in Orders dead-letters every shipment in the pipeline`,paragraphs:[`It's 3:07 AM and a 'trivial' migration just renamed one column in the Orders schema. By 3:15 the shipping pipeline is a graveyard — every OrderCreated event dead-lettered, because the Shipping module's consumer had been quietly reading Orders' tables through the OrdersDbContext. Nobody on the shipping team knew that tunnel existed. It was never in a diagram, never caught in review, never covered by a test.`,`The postmortem was humiliating in the useful way. Two modules in one process, dedicated DbContexts on paper, 'separation of concerns' in every README — and yet a rename in Orders took Shipping down like a domino. The fix wasn't microservices and it wasn't a broker upgrade. It was drawing an actual boundary: one public contract per module, everything else internal, and the compiler as the only enforcer that never sleeps.`],problem:`In a modular monolith, the boundary isn't the folder structure — it's the public API. If Shipping can reach Orders' DbContext, you don't have modules; you have a database with extra steps.`},tech:{stack:[`Modular Monolith`,`Minimal APIs`,`EF Core`,`DbContext per Module`,`Integration Events`,`OrderService Facade`,`Internal by Default`],note:`Two modules — Orders and Shipping — each with a dedicated EF Core DbContext and Minimal API endpoints, communicating via an OrderCreated integration event and, after the fix, an internal-backed OrderService facade as the module's only public door.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: two modules, one database, zero modularity`,body:[`Here's the setup we're walking into: two modules, Orders and Shipping, in the same process. Orders has a POST endpoint that creates an order and publishes an OrderCreated integration event, plus a GET to fetch it. Shipping has a consumer for that event. Both have their own DbContext — OrdersDbContext, ShippingDbContext. On paper, this looks modular.`,`But watch what the Shipping consumer actually does: it grabs the OrdersDbContext and reads the order straight out of the other module's database to figure out the shipping address. That's not a boundary — that's a tunnel. And the moment you ask 'why do we even care about modularity?', the answer is brutal: we want Orders and Shipping to evolve independently, and this coupling means every change in one cascades into the other.`,`Think about what's coming down the road: splitting an order into multiple shipments, special handling for certain items, tax and discount calculations on the ordering side. Every one of those is a change in one module that must not ripple into the other. With a direct DbContext dependency, it always will.`],quote:`A folder named 'Shipping' is not a boundary — what Shipping is allowed to touch is.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Direct DbContext access`,`Simplest, zero indirection`,`Schema coupling; changes cascade across modules`,`Reject`],[`Raw SQL into other module's tables`,`No ORM dependency`,`Still table-level coupling, fragile`,`Reject`],[`Module service contract`,`One controlled door; owner keeps freedom`,`Slightly more code`,`Prefer`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith Architecture: The Missing Guide to Public APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ZrjLEIBRWw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: who owns what`,body:[`Before we fix anything, let's do the exercise that actually pays: draw the line between the concerns. Order placement, tax handling, discounts — that's Orders territory. Splitting an order into multiple shipments, dedicated shipping for certain items, special handling during shipping — that's Shipping territory, and the Orders module shouldn't know or care that any of it exists.`,`The shipping address is the interesting one — Orders stores it, Shipping consumes it, but notice Shipping shouldn't go digging for it. It should be handed what it needs. That's the whole game: not less coupling, but deliberate coupling, through a door the owning module controls.`,`And here's the presenter confession: this exercise is uncomfortable, because every 'who owns this?' question ends with someone giving something up. That discomfort is the signal you're doing it right.`],quote:`Boundaries don't reduce coupling — they decide which changes are allowed to cascade.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Owner`,`Crosses boundary?`],rows:[[`Order placement, tax, discounts`,`Orders`,`No`],[`Split order into multiple shipments`,`Shipping`,`No`],[`Special item handling`,`Shipping`,`No`],[`Shipping address`,`Orders stores, Shipping consumes`,`Yes — via contract/event`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith Architecture: The Missing Guide to Public APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ZrjLEIBRWw`,at:`0:37`}},{heading:`03 · The first fix: put a service in front of the data`,body:[`Here's the move: define an OrderService in the Orders module with a GetOrderAsync(Guid orderId) method. The implementation is boring on purpose — inject the OrdersDbContext, fetch the order, return it. Now point the Shipping consumer at OrderService.GetOrderAsync instead of the DbContext, and watch the Shipping module's code shrink to a single call.`,`But here's the wrinkle that teaches the real lesson: the OrdersDbContext is internal, so OrderService has to be internal too. And that's not a bug — that's the design working. Internal to the module, but a much higher-level contract than raw table access. If tomorrow Orders wants to swap the fetch for a direct SQL query against its own tables, it can — Shipping never knows.`,`Notice what we didn't do: we didn't reach for microservices, a service mesh, or a second broker. Less indirection, same process, and the architectural principle — dependency direction — is now enforced by visibility modifiers instead of goodwill.`],quote:`One door per module. It can be a method or an event — but it's the only door.`,table:{headers:[`Access Path`,`Coupling Level`,`Owner Freedom`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Shared DbContext`,`Schema-level`,`None — migrations break consumers`,`Reject`],[`Raw SQL to other module's tables`,`Table-level`,`Minimal`,`Reject`],[`Service facade (OrderService)`,`Contract-level`,`Full — internals free to change`,`Prefer`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith Architecture: The Missing Guide to Public APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ZrjLEIBRWw`,at:`2:33`}},{heading:`04 · The leak we haven't plugged: returning the domain entity`,body:[`Now the honest part: GetOrderAsync still returns Order — the domain entity. That means Orders' internal model is now its public API. The day we introduce split shipments or special-handling flags on that entity, Shipping recompiles, refactors, and possibly breaks, even though its behavior never changed.`,`The fix is the one you'd expect: a dedicated contract type at the boundary. The service maps the entity to a DTO that says exactly what Shipping needs — an ID and a shipping address is all this consumer uses. The internal model evolves freely; the contract changes only when we deliberately say so.`,`Rule of thumb I keep repeating on stage: if a consumer can't tell the difference between your entity and your contract, you don't have a contract yet.`],quote:`Hand a neighbor your entity and you've handed them your schema.`,table:{headers:[`Boundary Type`,`Blast Radius of Internal Change`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Domain entity exposed`,`Every consumer recompiles and refactors`,`Reject`],[`Dedicated contract DTO`,`Only deliberate contract changes`,`Prefer`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith Architecture: The Missing Guide to Public APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ZrjLEIBRWw`,at:`3:46`}},{heading:`05 · Why internal-by-default wins`,body:[`Zoom out and ask why this beats the alternatives. A big ball of mud has zero indirection and zero enforcement — every module can touch everything, until a rename kills shipments at 3 AM. Microservices enforce the boundary with the network, but you pay in latency, versioning, and operational weight you may not need for a complex business domain in one deployable.`,`The modular monolith with internal-by-default is the sweet spot: in-process calls, minimal indirection, and the compiler itself refusing to let Shipping reference OrdersDbContext. The rule isn't in a wiki page nobody reads — it's a build error. That's what 'designing a stable public API' really means: a small, deliberate surface that the language defends on your behalf.`,`And the test is dead simple: try to reference the other module's DbContext. If it compiles, your boundary is decorative.`],quote:`The best architecture document is one the compiler refuses to let you violate.`,table:{headers:[`Style`,`Indirection`,`Boundary Enforcement`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Big ball of mud`,`None`,`None — goodwill only`,`Avoid`],[`Modular monolith, internal-by-default`,`Low — in-process calls`,`Compiler + one public contract`,`Sweet spot`],[`Microservices`,`High — network, versioning`,`Network boundary`,`When team scale demands it`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith Architecture: The Missing Guide to Public APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ZrjLEIBRWw`,at:`4:49`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Modular Monolith: the bad tunnel and the public-API door`,caption:`The Shipping consumer's direct OrdersDbContext read (the 3 AM killer) versus the controlled path through OrderService.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Orders["Orders Module"]
        API["Minimal APIs<br/>POST /orders · GET /orders/:id"]
        ODB[("OrdersDbContext")]
        PUB["Publishes<br/>OrderCreated"]
        SVC["OrderService<br/>GetOrderAsync(id)"]
    end

    subgraph Shipping["Shipping Module"]
        CON["OrderCreated<br/>Consumer"]
        SDB[("ShippingDbContext")]
        SHIP["Create Shipment"]
    end

    BUS(("Integration<br/>Events"))

    API --> ODB
    API --> PUB
    PUB --> BUS
    BUS --> CON
    CON --> SHIP
    SHIP --> SDB
    CON -. "BAD: direct read" .-> ODB
    CON -. "GOOD: public API" .-> SVC
    SVC --> ODB`},{id:`sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`OrderCreated happy path with the contract in the middle`,caption:`Sequence from POST /orders to a shipment row, with Shipping reading only through the Orders module's public service.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant O as Orders Module
    participant B as Event Bus
    participant S as Shipping Consumer
    participant OS as OrderService
    participant OD as OrdersDbContext
    participant SD as ShippingDbContext

    C->>O: POST /orders
    O->>OD: Insert order
    O->>B: Publish OrderCreated(orderId)
    B->>S: Deliver OrderCreated
    S->>OS: GetOrderAsync(orderId)
    OS->>OD: Query order (owned path)
    OD-->>OS: Order
    OS-->>S: Order contract
    S->>SD: Insert shipment`}],takeaways:[`One DbContext per module, enforced by the build: if a second module's namespace shows up in a DbContext's usings, fail CI — that's a boundary violation, not a style nit.`,`Internal by default: expose one public contract surface per module and keep it small — a handful of service methods plus your integration events; everything else stays internal.`,`Zero entity leakage: never return a domain entity across a module boundary — map to a contract DTO so internal refactors have a blast radius of exactly one module.`,`Route every cross-module interaction through one of two doors: a service facade for reads, an integration event for writes — target 100% of cross-module calls through these doors.`,`Let the compiler be the architect: internal visibility enforces dependency direction at build time (seconds) instead of code review (days) or a 3 AM incident (priceless).`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith Architecture: The Missing Guide to Public APIs`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ZrjLEIBRWw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-UidT7YYu97s`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:32,title:`How To Build an API Gateway for Microservices with YARP`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:43`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`YARP`,`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`Three Ports, Three Tokens, and a Checkout That Died at 11 PM`,paragraphs:[`It was 11 PM the night before the customer demo. The mobile team had wired checkout straight into our three microservices: POST to port 5100 to create the user, POST to 5300 to create the products, then POST to 5200 with the user id and product ids to close the sale. Three base URLs, three auth handshakes — all hardcoded in the app.`,`Then a routine Docker Compose cleanup shifted one published port, and checkout started 404ing in the demo environment. Nothing was wrong with the services. The client was simply coupled to our topology — and every new service, port change, or merge was going to be a breaking client release.`,`The fix wasn't a new framework. It was one more .NET web API — a reverse proxy built on YARP — sitting in front of everything, with a single routing table as the only contract the outside world could see.`],problem:`Clients were hard-coupled to three service addresses and three separate auth flows, so any topology change — a port, a merge, a new service — became a breaking change shipped to every client.`},tech:{stack:[`YARP`,`.NET Web API`,`Docker Compose`,`Reverse Proxy`],note:`A plain .NET web API hosting YARP is the single front door, routing public paths to the Users, Sales, and Product APIs running side-by-side in Docker Compose, each with its own database on a shared Docker network.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why three base URLs fall over`,body:[`Let me walk you through the before picture, because it's worth feeling the pain. I POST to port 5100 and get a user back. I POST to 5300 and create products. Then — only then — I can POST to 5200 with the user id and the product ids to make a sale. Three addresses, three ports, three separate auth handshakes, and the client is the poor soul orchestrating all of it.`,`Now ask yourself: why not just ship a service-discovery library to the client? Because that couples every mobile app and every third-party integrator to your internal topology. The day you rename a service or change a port, you're shipping client releases. Introducing a new service — or merging two old ones — becomes a breaking change for people who never asked to care.`,`The client shouldn't know how many services exist. It should know one URL and one contract. That's the whole itch the gateway scratches.`],quote:`Your clients should depend on your contract, not your topology.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Direct client-to-service`,`No extra hop, trivial to demo`,`N URLs, N auth flows, topology leaks into clients`,`Toys and internal tools`],[`API Gateway (YARP)`,`One URL, central auth and caching, topology hidden`,`Extra hop, becomes a bottleneck`,`Any multi-service system`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build an API Gateway for Microservices with YARP`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UidT7YYu97s`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what the gateway owns`,body:[`So we put a component in front of the services — a reverse proxy. It takes the incoming request, figures out which service is supposed to handle it, forwards it, gets the response back, and hands it to the client. As far as the client is concerned, there is exactly one backend.`,`That single door is also where the cross-cutting concerns live: authentication, caching, rate limiting, request routing. You write them once at the edge instead of three times in three services. But be honest about the trade: the gateway is now a bottleneck in your system. The mitigation isn't cleverness, it's availability — run it in a highly available setup, keep it stateless, scale it horizontally.`,`And here's the line I draw every time: if a piece of logic needs a database or business rules, it does not belong in the gateway. The gateway routes and guards; it doesn't think.`],quote:`The gateway is a bottleneck by design — so make it the cheapest thing to scale, not the smartest thing to break.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Lives in the gateway?`,`Why`],rows:[[`Request routing`,`Yes`,`Single map from public path to service`],[`AuthN / AuthZ`,`Yes`,`Authenticate once at the edge, not N times`],[`Caching / rate limits`,`Yes`,`Cross-cutting, one place to tune`],[`Business logic`,`No`,`Needs data and rules — that's a service`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build an API Gateway for Microservices with YARP`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UidT7YYu97s`,at:`0:57`}},{heading:`03 · Why YARP and not something else`,body:[`Here's the part that surprises people: the gateway is just another .NET project. I add a solution folder, create a plain ASP.NET Core web API, and add YARP — Yet Another Reverse Proxy, Microsoft's reverse-proxy library built on ASP.NET Core itself. No special template, no magic. At that point the only thing missing is the reverse proxy configuration.`,`Could you use NGINX or Envoy? Absolutely, and for pure static routing they're excellent. But YARP lives in your process: it's C#, it's config-driven from appsettings, and it's extensible with the same middleware pipeline you already know — exactly what you want when auth and caching need custom logic.`,`Ocelot is the other .NET name that comes up. It's a fine gateway, but YARP is the one Microsoft is actively building on, and it stays out of your way: your gateway is a web API that happens to proxy.`],quote:`With YARP, your gateway isn't a product you deploy — it's code you own.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`YARP`,`In-process .NET, config-driven, extensible middleware`,`You build and host it yourself`,`.NET teams wanting code-level control`],[`Ocelot`,`Mature .NET gateway, rich built-in features`,`Separate config model to learn`,`.NET teams wanting batteries-included`],[`NGINX / Envoy`,`Battle-tested, very high performance`,`Outside your codebase, custom logic is painful`,`Static routing, polyglot fleets`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build an API Gateway for Microservices with YARP`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UidT7YYu97s`,at:`1:58`}},{heading:`04 · The config is the architecture`,body:[`Once the project exists, the whole architecture lives in a config section: routes and clusters. A route says 'requests matching this path pattern go here'; a cluster lists the destinations — the actual hosts and ports of my Users, Sales, and Product APIs. Path transformations let me expose /users, /sales, and /products publicly while the services keep whatever internal shape they like.`,`This is the quiet superpower: your public API contract is now a routing table. Want to merge Sales and Orders into one service? Edit the config. Want to split Products in two? Edit the config. The clients never notice, because they never talked to the services — they talked to the gateway.`,`And because the gateway is stateless, 'highly available' just means more copies of the same config behind a load balancer. The bottleneck is real, but it's the most boring, most scalable bottleneck in your system — which is exactly where you want your bottleneck to live.`],quote:`When the route table is the contract, refactoring the backend stops being a client problem.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build an API Gateway for Microservices with YARP`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UidT7YYu97s`,at:`2:29`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology-before-after`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Before and after: one front door`,caption:`The client stops owning the topology. YARP owns the route table; each service keeps its own database on the shared Docker network.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Before["Before - client owns the topology"]
        C1[Client] -->|port 5100| U1[Users API]
        C1 -->|port 5200| S1[Sales API]
        C1 -->|port 5300| P1[Product API]
    end
    subgraph After["After - one front door"]
        C2[Client] --> G["API Gateway - YARP"]
        G -->|"/users/*"| U2["Users API :5100"]
        G -->|"/sales/*"| S2["Sales API :5200"]
        G -->|"/products/*"| P2["Product API :5300"]
        U2 --> DU[(Users DB)]
        S2 --> DS[(Sales DB)]
        P2 --> DP[(Products DB)]
    end`},{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of one sale request`,caption:`One sale, one door: the gateway matches the route, forwards to the Sales cluster, and the client only ever sees a single backend.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant G as YARP Gateway
    participant S as Sales API
    participant DB as Sales DB
    C->>G: POST /sales (userId, productIds)
    G->>G: Match route, apply auth and caching policy
    G->>S: Forward request to sales cluster
    S->>DB: Persist sale, load products
    DB-->>S: Sale saved
    S-->>G: 201 Created
    G-->>C: 201 Created`}],takeaways:[`Collapse N client-facing endpoints into 1: three base URLs means three auth flows and three breaking-change surfaces — one gateway route table cuts that to one.`,`Keep the gateway stateless and dumb — routing, auth, caching only — so you can run 2+ replicas behind a load balancer and eliminate the single point of failure.`,`Budget the extra hop at roughly 1-5 ms per request; it's cheap, but the gateway becomes your hottest path, so load-test it like a first-class service.`,`Treat the routes-and-clusters config as your public API contract: merging or splitting services becomes a config edit, not a client release.`,`If a feature needs its own database or business rules, it's a microservice behind the gateway — never gateway code.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build an API Gateway for Microservices with YARP`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UidT7YYu97s`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`api-gateway`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-5dilYMii9T4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:33,title:`Getting Started with Modular Monoliths in .NET`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:38`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`gRPC`,`REST`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Column Rename That Took Down Finance at 3 AM`,paragraphs:["It's 12:05 during a sudden traffic spike and the finance rollup job has failed for the third night in a row. The on-call engineer — let's call him Darius — is staring at a stack trace that makes no sense: `column users.contact_email does not exist`. Nobody on the finance team touched the users tables that week. Nobody on the users team even knew finance was reading them.",`A four-minute grep confirms the worst: for two years, the Finance module had been quietly joining straight onto the Users module's tables — three different queries, zero coordination, zero tests covering the seam. A 'simple' rename shipped in one module had silently severed a dependency nobody had ever written down. That's the monolith bargain: one deployable, one database, and eventually one giant ball of invisible couplings.`,`The fix wasn't microservices — nobody has the appetite for distributed transactions at 3 AM. The fix was drawing real boundaries inside the thing they already had: a public API per module, a schema per module, and a broker for every conversation that crossed a line. Same single deployable. Same database server. But the next rename shipped on a Tuesday afternoon, and finance never noticed.`],problem:`In a classic monolith, logical boundaries erode until every module can reach into every other module's tables and code. Microservices fix that by pushing boundaries to the physical level — independently deployed services — but you pay the distributed-systems tax from day one. A modular monolith enforces the boundaries logically instead: public APIs per module and isolated data, inside one deployable.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET`,`C# Interfaces`,`Message Broker`,`In-Memory Bus`,`Relational DB`,`Schema-per-Module`,`gRPC`,`REST`],note:`One deployable .NET app whose modules talk through C# interface public APIs and a message broker, with a relational database carved into per-module schemas — the minimum kit for boundaries that actually hold.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why do monoliths rot?`,body:[`Let's start where the pain starts. A monolith is a single executable — every component is developed and deployed together. That's genuinely great for the first year: one deploy, one debugger, one transaction. The problem is that complexity compounds as features pile on, and nothing in a classic monolith stops module A from reaching into module B's tables at 2 AM.`,`The industry's default answer has been microservices: take those logical boundaries and elevate them to the physical level, deploy each service independently. And yes, that kills the coupling — but now you own network failures, partial writes, versioned contracts, and a dozen pipelines whether or not you needed them. Why pay the distributed tax on day zero?`,`A modular monolith is the honest middle ground: keep the single deployable, but enforce boundaries inside it as if they were real services. Two rules do most of the work — every module exposes a public API, and every module owns its data.`],quote:`A monolith doesn't rot because it's big — it rots because nothing stops one module from reaching into another.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Monolith`,`One deploy, simple ops, easy transactions`,`Coupling grows unchecked, big-bang releases`,`Small teams, early stage`],[`Modular monolith`,`Enforced boundaries, cheap deploys, clear extraction path`,`Needs discipline and enforcement`,`Most product teams, growing domains`],[`Microservices`,`Independent deploys and scaling, fault isolation`,`Distributed tax: network, consistency, ops cost`,`Large orgs, divergent scale needs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Getting Started with Modular Monoliths in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5dilYMii9T4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: public APIs as C# interfaces`,body:[`Rule one: a public API per module. And here's the part people overthink — in .NET, that public API can literally be an interface. Module B exposes an interface; module A gets a reference to it and calls methods. Everything not on that interface is internal, full stop.`,`Why not just let modules share project references and call classes directly? Because that's exactly how the 3 AM incident happens. The interface is the contract, and the compiler becomes your boundary enforcement. If someone needs something that isn't on the interface, that's a design conversation — not a private shortcut.`,`The same rule applies to data: module A never queries module B's tables — it asks the API. The moment you allow a 'just this once' cross-module join, you're back to invisible couplings, and the next rename is someone's 3 AM.`],quote:`If the boundary isn't enforced by the compiler, it's just a comment.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Getting Started with Modular Monoliths in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5dilYMii9T4`,at:`5:04`}},{heading:`03 · Choosing the wire: in-process calls vs messaging`,body:[`Rule two is how modules talk. Two high-level options. Option one: synchronous, in-process calls through those interfaces — fast, transactional, dead simple. Option two: messaging — commands that trigger something in the other module, or events that announce something happened — flowing through a message broker.`,`Now, inside a monolith, the tempting shortcut is an in-memory bus. Don't. It isn't reliable — your process crashes mid-publish and you've lost messages, and lost messages in anything touching money is genuinely costly. If you go async, use a real broker and accept that it's infrastructure you now have to run and monitor. That's the trade: messaging buys loose coupling and charges you operational complexity.`,`Here's why I pay that cost anyway: the day you extract a module into its own service, a messaging-based integration keeps working unchanged — same broker, same contracts. An in-process call has to be redesigned; a published event just keeps flowing.`],quote:`An in-memory bus is a promise the process makes to itself — and processes break that promise the moment they crash.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-process interface call`,`Fast, ACID-friendly, zero extra infra`,`Tight runtime coupling, breaks on extraction`,`Queries, low-latency reads`],[`In-memory bus`,`Async, no infrastructure to run`,`Unreliable, silently loses messages on crash`,`Almost nothing production-critical`],[`Real message broker`,`Durable, loosely coupled, extraction-ready`,`Extra infrastructure to operate`,`Cross-module commands and events`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Getting Started with Modular Monoliths in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5dilYMii9T4`,at:`7:12`}},{heading:`04 · The four rungs of data isolation`,body:[`Data isolation is where modular monoliths live or die, and I think of it as a ladder with four rungs. Rung one: no isolation — separate tables, same schema, same physical database, same relational database type. It works, but nothing prevents the cross-module join, so the coupling creeps right back.`,`Rung two: a separate schema per module — orders, users, finance — still one physical database, still one database type. This is my default recommendation: cheap, visible boundaries your tooling can enforce. Rung three: separate databases per module. Rung four: separate database servers or even database types — at which point you've essentially arrived at microservices-style data ownership.`,`Why climb at all? Same reason as the public APIs: preventing tight coupling. Every rung makes an accidental cross-module join harder and a future extraction easier — but each rung also costs you convenient cross-module transactions and simpler operations. Climb on pain, not on fashion.`],quote:`Shared tables are shared fate.`,table:{headers:[`Isolation level`,`What it looks like`,`Coupling risk`,`Extraction effort`],rows:[[`1 · None`,`Separate tables, one schema, one physical DB`,`High — joins creep in`,`High`],[`2 · Schema per module`,`orders / users / finance schemas, one physical DB`,`Low — cross-schema joins are visible`,`Low`],[`3 · Separate databases`,`One database per module, same or separate server`,`Very low`,`Very low`],[`4 · Separate servers/types`,`Independent database engines per module`,`None at the data layer`,`None — already there`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Getting Started with Modular Monoliths in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5dilYMii9T4`,at:`7:43`}},{heading:`05 · The exit ramp: extracting a module without a rewrite`,body:[`So here's the punchline I want you to leave with. A modular monolith isn't a consolation prize for teams that 'aren't ready' for microservices — it's the on-ramp. If your modules already talk through explicit APIs and a broker, and already own their schemas, then extracting one module is a deployment decision, not a rewrite.`,`That's exactly what microservices do differently: they elevate the logical boundaries to the physical level, with independently deployed services. Genuinely valuable — when you have the org scale to pay for it. Until then, the modular monolith gives you the boundaries, the testability, and the extraction path, while keeping one deploy pipeline and one database server.`,`My rule of thumb: start modular, stay modular until a specific module's scaling or team topology demands independence — and let the architecture you already built make that extraction boring.`],quote:`A modular monolith is a microservices migration you're allowed to pause halfway.`}],diagrams:[{id:`modular-monolith-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Modular Monolith Topology`,caption:`One deployable, three modules with explicit public APIs, per-module schemas in a single relational database, and a broker for cross-module events.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph APP["Single .NET Deployable"]
        subgraph ORD["Orders Module"]
            OAPI["Public API: IOrdersApi"]
            OLOGIC["Internal logic"]
        end
        subgraph USR["Users Module"]
            UAPI["Public API: IUsersApi"]
            ULOGIC["Internal logic"]
        end
        subgraph FIN["Finance Module"]
            FAPI["Public API: IFinanceApi"]
            FLOGIC["Internal logic"]
        end
        BUS[["Message Broker"]]
    end
    subgraph DB["One Relational Database Server"]
        S1[("orders schema")]
        S2[("users schema")]
        S3[("finance schema")]
    end
    OAPI --> OLOGIC --> S1
    UAPI --> ULOGIC --> S2
    FAPI --> FLOGIC --> S3
    OAPI -. "sync call via interface" .-> UAPI
    OLOGIC -- "OrderPlaced event" --> BUS
    BUS -- "deliver event" --> FLOGIC`},{id:`order-request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request Path: Sync API + Async Event`,caption:`The hybrid pattern in action: a synchronous call through a public API for the read-side check, then a durable event through the broker for the cross-module reaction.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant O as Orders Module
    participant U as Users Module
    participant B as Message Broker
    participant F as Finance Module
    C->>O: POST /orders
    O->>U: IUsersApi.ValidateCustomer(id)
    U-->>O: Customer valid
    O->>O: INSERT into orders schema
    O->>B: OrderPlaced event
    B->>F: Deliver OrderPlaced
    F->>F: UPDATE finance schema
    Note over B,F: Same broker contract keeps working after Orders is extracted to its own service`}],takeaways:[`Ban cross-schema joins on day one: schema-per-module in one physical database removes the majority of accidental coupling before it ever exists.`,`Cap each module's public API at a single C# interface — if it grows past roughly 10 methods, the boundary is drawn in the wrong place.`,`Never route business-critical flows through an in-memory bus: one crashed process silently drops messages that cost real money — use a real broker with durable delivery.`,`Go messaging-first for cross-module commands and events: every broker-based interaction survives extraction to a separate service with zero contract changes.`,`Climb the isolation ladder one rung at a time — separate tables, then schemas, then databases, then servers — and only when coupling pain, not hype, demands it.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Getting Started with Modular Monoliths in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5dilYMii9T4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-xYtM5sJ3Nrc`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:34,title:`Modular Monolith - How To Create Your Modules`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:16`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`DDD`],story:{headline:`Three aggregates, one giant Domain folder, and a demo at 9 AM`,paragraphs:[`It's 11:47 PM the night before the Run Tracker stakeholder demo, and I'm staring at a single Domain project holding every entity the app owns — Users, Followers, Workouts, Activities, likes, comments — all one namespace, all one folder. Someone asks a 'quick' question in Slack: 'Can followers see private workouts?' To answer it I have to read User, Follower, Workout, and Activity in the same breath, because nothing in the code says where one concept ends and the next begins.`,`So I do the thing every architecture talk tells you to do: carve out modules. First instinct — one module per aggregate, three modules, done. Except each one is a ghost town: a module owning a single aggregate and a handful of handlers, while every real use case quietly reaches across two of them. That's not a boundary, that's a tax.`,`The actual cut came from one question — which entities change together? Users and followers: one story. Workouts and activities: another. Two modules, physical class library projects, a SharedKernel for the base types, and by 2 AM the compiler — not a diagram — was enforcing the boundary. The demo went fine. The codebase finally did too.`],problem:`How do you decide where module boundaries go in a modular monolith — and make them physically real in the solution — without spawning too many thin modules or drowning in cross-references?`},tech:{stack:[`Modular Monolith`,`DDD`,`Clean Architecture`,`MediatR`,`FluentValidation`,`Shared Kernel`],note:`Per-module class library projects (Domain + Application) enforce boundaries at compile time, a SharedKernel holds the only legal shared types, and MediatR wires each module's use cases into one shared pipeline.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: one module per aggregate?`,body:[`So the obvious first move: we have three aggregates on the Run Tracker diagram — Users with Followers, Activity with its likes and comments, Workout with its exercises — so three modules, one per aggregate. It maps one-to-one to the picture, it feels rigorous. But look at what you actually get: three modules each carrying a thin slice of functionality, and a pile of use cases that have to reach across two of them to do anything useful. Granularity is a trade, and 'one aggregate equals one module' is a default, not a rule.`,`The tell is the use cases. If every handler in the Activity module needs to know about the User module, you haven't created boundaries — you've built a distributed monolith inside a single process, with all the coupling and none of the payoff. A module should be able to answer a meaningful business question on its own, and none of these three can.`],quote:`Boundaries that map to your diagram are not boundaries; boundaries that map to your use cases are.`,table:{headers:[`Split strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`One module per aggregate`,`Perfect 1:1 traceability to the domain model`,`Too many thin modules, constant cross-module chatter`,`Small apps: poor`],[`Two modules: Users + Training`,`High cohesion, few seams, each owns a real capability`,`Bigger modules you must keep honest`,`This app: strong`],[`No modules at all`,`Zero upfront cost`,`Coupling compounds until the split is unaffordable`,`Prototype only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith - How To Create Your Modules`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xYtM5sJ3Nrc`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: cohesion is the compass`,body:[`Here's the question I keep asking out loud: which entities change together? Users and followers are one story — you can't reason about a follower relationship without a user, and the use cases around them are the same shape. Activities and workouts are another story — both live in the training domain, both serve the same actor doing the same job. That's high cohesion, and cohesion draws the line — not the aggregate diagram.`,`So we land on two modules: Users and Training. Notice what we did NOT do — we didn't split by technical layer across the whole app (one giant Domain, one giant Application), and we didn't split by org chart. We split along the domain's own fault lines. When you can name the business capability a module owns in one sentence, you've probably got it right.`],quote:`Split along the faults where change actually happens — not along the lines that look tidy on a whiteboard.`,table:{headers:[`Candidate group`,`Entities`,`Shared use cases`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Users module`,`User, Follower`,`High — one social-graph capability`,`Keep together`],[`Training module`,`Workout, Activity (likes, comments)`,`High — one training capability`,`Keep together`],[`User ↔ Workout`,`Cross-domain`,`Low — different rates of change`,`Separate modules`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith - How To Create Your Modules`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xYtM5sJ3Nrc`,at:`5:25`}},{heading:`03 · Making it physical: folders, projects, naming`,body:[`A boundary that lives only in your head is not a boundary. So we make it real: a Modules folder, then Modules.Users and Modules.Training, and inside each a class library per layer — Modules.Users.Domain, Modules.Users.Application, same for Training. The naming convention does double duty: it tells you the module and the layer, and it lets the compiler enforce what the architecture diagram merely claims.`,`One practical gotcha: Visual Studio solution folders are logical only, so create the physical folders on disk too, or the next person opening the repo in a different tool sees a flat mess. Then it's just moving files — Users and Followers into the Users domain project, Workouts and Activities into Training — and fixing compile errors one by one. Those errors are a feature: every single one is a hidden dependency surfacing. A ReSharper namespace pass finishes the job.`],quote:`If the compiler can't see your module boundary, neither can the next developer at 2 AM.`,table:{headers:[`Project`,`Owns`,`References`,`Never references`],rows:[[`Modules.Users.Domain`,`User, Follower entities`,`SharedKernel`,`Other modules`],[`Modules.Users.Application`,`Use cases, validators, DI extension`,`Users.Domain, SharedKernel`,`Training.*`],[`Modules.Training.Domain`,`Workout, Activity entities`,`SharedKernel`,`Other modules`],[`Modules.Training.Application`,`Use cases, validators, DI extension`,`Training.Domain, SharedKernel`,`Users.*`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith - How To Create Your Modules`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xYtM5sJ3Nrc`,at:`6:42`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring it up: SharedKernel and one pipeline`,body:[`Now the wiring. The first compile errors are the shared base types, and those move into a SharedKernel project that both domain projects reference. That's the only shared project. The moment you're tempted to add a second shared reference between modules, stop — that's the boundary dissolving in real time.`,`The application layer is where people panic about double registration, but MediatR is forgiving here: calling AddMediatR multiple times is safe, because under the hood it registers the required services only once — and we call it once per module's application assembly so each module's use cases get discovered. Pipeline behaviors, though, get registered exactly once, from the original application project; you don't want validation or logging running twice per request. Validators get scanned per assembly with AddValidatorsFromAssembly, each module exposes its own AddXxx DI extension, and the follower service registration that no longer belongs gets deleted.`],quote:`Register handlers per module, behaviors once — the pipeline belongs to the app, the use cases belong to the modules.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Where registered`,`How many times`],rows:[[`AddMediatR (use case discovery)`,`Each module's Application assembly`,`Once per module — safe to repeat`],[`Pipeline behaviors`,`Composition root (host app)`,`Exactly once`],[`Validators`,`Per module assembly`,`Once per module`],[`Module DI extensions`,`Host Program.cs`,`One call per module`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith - How To Create Your Modules`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xYtM5sJ3Nrc`,at:`9:27`}},{heading:`05 · The questions I ask before every cut`,body:[`Before you cut, answer these out loud. What business capability does this module own — can you say it in one sentence? Which aggregates change together, and which change on different schedules? How many use cases would have to cross the boundary, and could they live on one side? If a module can't answer 'what do I own,' it's a folder, not a module.`,`And the meta-rule: boundaries are cheap to merge and expensive to draw wrong. Start coarser than your instincts tell you — two modules beat five thin ones — and let the pain of a specific dependency justify the next split. The refactor we just did was mostly moving files and fixing namespaces; if your split requires rewriting core logic, you drew the line through something that wanted to stay together.`],quote:`Draw boundaries coarser than feels right, then let real coupling earn the next cut.`,table:{headers:[`Question`,`Green flag`,`Red flag`],rows:[[`One-sentence capability?`,`'Owns user identity and social graph'`,`'It handles stuff'`],[`Change together?`,`Aggregates evolve on the same cadence`,`One aggregate ships weekly, the other yearly`],[`Cross-boundary use cases?`,`Near zero`,`Most handlers need the other module`],[`Cost of the split?`,`Move files + rename namespaces`,`Rewrite core logic`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith - How To Create Your Modules`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xYtM5sJ3Nrc`,at:`10:32`}}],diagrams:[{id:`module-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Run Tracker modular monolith: project topology and references`,caption:`Two modules, each with its own Domain and Application class library, sharing only a SharedKernel — the host composes everything at the composition root.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    Host["RunTracker Host - composition root"]
    SK["SharedKernel"]
    subgraph UM["Users Module"]
        UA["Modules.Users.Application"] --> UD["Modules.Users.Domain"]
    end
    subgraph TM["Training Module"]
        TA["Modules.Training.Application"] --> TD["Modules.Training.Domain"]
    end
    Host --> UA
    Host --> TA
    UD --> SK
    TD --> SK
    Host -. "AddMediatR per module, behaviors once" .- Host`},{id:`boundary-decision`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Decision flow: from aggregates to real modules`,caption:`The repeatable path from a domain model to physically enforced module boundaries.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["List your DDD aggregates"] --> B{"Which aggregates change together?"}
    B -->|"High cohesion"| C["Group as one candidate module"]
    B -->|"Low cohesion"| D["Keep as separate candidates"]
    C --> E{"Candidate too thin - little functionality?"}
    D --> E
    E -->|"Yes"| F["Merge with closest cohesive neighbor"]
    E -->|"No"| G{"Only shared base types cross the seam?"}
    F --> G
    G -->|"Yes"| H["Promote to module: Domain + Application class libraries"]
    G -->|"No"| I["Move shared types to SharedKernel"]
    I --> H
    H --> J["Wire DI: AddMediatR per module, pipeline behaviors once at the host"]`}],takeaways:[`Start with 2–4 modules; if an aggregate-per-module split yields more than ~5 thin modules, merge along cohesion lines (User+Follower, Workout+Activity).`,`One module = exactly one Domain class library + one Application class library; add more projects per module only when a third layer earns its keep.`,`Zero cross-module project references — a module may reference only itself and SharedKernel; the first cross-module reference is the boundary failing.`,`Call AddMediatR once per module's Application assembly (safe to repeat — services register once), but register pipeline behaviors exactly once at the composition root.`,`A healthy split moves files and renames namespaces in under a day; if the refactor rewrites core logic, you cut through a cohesive aggregate — redraw the line.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Modular Monolith - How To Create Your Modules`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xYtM5sJ3Nrc`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-S2zqdX411RQ`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-architecture`,order:35,title:`I Built the Same App as a Monolith and a Modular Monolith`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:24`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`One Entity, 412 References, Zero Boundaries`,paragraphs:[`It started as a innocent Tuesday ticket: extract the notifications module into its own service so we could scale email sends independently. Two weeks later I was staring at grep output at 1 AM — 412 references to the User entity scattered across workouts, followers, activities, and disputes. Nobody had ever drawn a line in that codebase; the application project just had feature folders, and every feature quietly reached into every other feature's domain.`,`The worst part wasn't the count — it was that the compiler never complained. Everything lived in one shared domain project, so any class could reference any other class, and for three years, it did. When we finally tried to declare a boundary, we discovered the boundary had to be excavated, not declared.`,`So we rebuilt the exact same app as a modular monolith: a top-level modules folder, one domain project per module, and a public API project exposing nothing but interfaces. Same features, still a single deployment unit. But now, when someone says 'extract notifications,' the answer is a two-week mechanical project instead of a three-week archaeology dig.`],problem:`In a classic monolith, hidden dependencies between features are invisible until the day you try to increase modularity — and then they're all you can see.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET`,`Modular Monolith`,`Bounded Contexts`,`Assembly Boundaries`,`Public API Contracts`,`Single Deployment`],note:`The same application built twice: once as one shared domain assembly where anything can reference anything, once as self-contained modules whose seams the compiler itself enforces.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: isn't every monolith already modular?`,body:[`So let's start with the question I get most often: doesn't 'modular monolith' just describe... any monolith? Fair challenge. A module is a self-contained component of a larger system — think payments, reviews, users, bookings, disputes, notifications — that you combine to produce the whole. Both of our versions ship as a single deployment unit, so from the outside they look identical.`,`The difference is whether the seams exist before you need them. In the classic version, the seams are aspirational — folders, good intentions, comments. In the modular version, the seams are structural — separate assemblies, explicit contracts. Same functionality, radically different cost when you ask the question 'can I take one piece out and run it alone?'`,`And that's the real product being sold here: optionality. The modular monolith buys you the ability to extract a module, scale it independently, version it separately, or give it its own database — without rewriting it. The price is more upfront work. There's no free lunch, only a choice about when you pay.`],quote:`A monolith and a modular monolith ship the same way — they only differ on the day you try to take one apart.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Classic monolith`,`Least upfront work, fastest start`,`Hidden coupling, extraction is archaeology`,`Prototypes, tiny teams, throwaway`],[`Modular monolith`,`Clear seams, extractable modules, one deployment`,`More upfront design, requires discipline`,`Most product teams, most products`],[`Microservices`,`Independent scale, deploy, and data per service`,`Distributed complexity from day one`,`Proven boundaries at high scale`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built the Same App as a Monolith and a Modular Monolith`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S2zqdX411RQ`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: identifiers cross, entities never`,body:[`Now let's dig into the domain project of the classic version. You find small bounded contexts hiding in folders: users and followers implement their own related behavior, while training holds workouts and activities. Here's the interesting bit — the workout only knows the user's identifier. It doesn't know anything else about the user. And honestly? That's correct. Identifiers are exactly what we allow across bounded contexts.`,`But then you notice the same physical user appears as three different types in the codebase. The better implementation is deliberate: define a User type per bounded context — physically the same person in our system, but logically a different abstraction inside each context. Same for activities tracking workouts: it needs workout details, but it doesn't need to know much about the user at all.`,`This is the rule I want burned into your brain: identifiers and DTOs cross boundaries; entities never do. The moment a workout class holds a full User entity, the users module's schema, invariants, and refactor freedom are hostage to the training module.`],quote:`IDs are the currency of module boundaries — entities are contraband.`,table:{headers:[`Crossing Type`,`Allowed?`,`Why`],rows:[[`Identifier (userId)`,`Yes`,`Stable, carries zero behavior`],[`DTO from public API`,`Yes`,`Explicit, versionable contract`],[`Entity reference`,`No`,`Couples schemas and invariants forever`],[`Shared helper class`,`Duplicate instead`,`A copy beats a hidden dependency`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built the Same App as a Monolith and a Modular Monolith`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S2zqdX411RQ`,at:`0:25`}},{heading:`03 · Where boundaries go to die: the application project`,body:[`Here's the plot twist from our rebuild. The domain project alone actually looked decent — two bounded contexts, some real separation. Then we opened the application project, and the lines blurred immediately: feature folders for workouts, users, followers, activities, all containing use cases that freely scope across contexts. Folders suggest boundaries. They do not enforce them.`,`In the modular version, I introduced a top-level modules folder, and each module contains all the projects it needs — its own domain project, its own application logic, and a public API project. That separate domain assembly is the whole trick: other bounded contexts physically cannot reference your entities. The compiler says no. They have to find another way, which means they have to go through the door you designed.`,`And what's behind the door? The users module's API project exposes simple interface definitions — that's it. That's the entire surface area. When the boundary lives in the type system instead of in a conventions document, it survives contact with deadlines.`],quote:`Folders are suggestions; assemblies are laws.`,table:{headers:[`Enforcement Mechanism`,`Strength`,`Failure Mode`],rows:[[`Folder conventions`,`Weak`,`One rushed PR away from tangle`],[`Separate assemblies`,`Strong`,`Compiler blocks illegal references outright`],[`Public API project`,`Strong`,`Only interfaces are visible from outside`],[`Architecture tests in CI`,`Medium`,`Catches drift after compile, before merge`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built the Same App as a Monolith and a Modular Monolith`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S2zqdX411RQ`,at:`3:22`}},{heading:`04 · The exit ramp: what extraction actually costs`,body:[`So why pay the upfront tax at all? Because at any point you can take one module and extract it into its own service — its own scaling curve, its own versioning rules, its own deployment mechanism, even its own database engine if its requirements demand it. That's the flexibility dividend, and it only pays out if the boundaries were real.`,`In the classic monolith, that same move surfaces every hidden dependency at once. You only find them when you try to increase modularity — that's not a saying, that's a law of nature I verified at 1 AM with 412 grep hits. The modular monolith front-loads the pain: more design work on day one, but extraction becomes a mechanical project instead of a rewrite.`,`One caution before you run off and modularize everything: extraction should be driven by a concrete force — scale, release cadence, data isolation, team ownership. A modular monolith is the option, not the obligation. Keep the single deployment until reality demands otherwise.`],quote:`You pay for boundaries either way — upfront by design, or later by excavation.`,table:{headers:[`Driver`,`Extract when...`,`Stay merged when...`],rows:[[`Scale`,`Module needs its own scaling curve`,`Load profile is uniform`],[`Versioning`,`Release cadence diverges from the rest`,`One team, one cadence`],[`Data`,`Needs a different database engine`,`Cross-module transactions matter`],[`Ownership`,`A separate team takes the domain`,`Single team owns the code`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built the Same App as a Monolith and a Modular Monolith`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S2zqdX411RQ`,at:`6:49`}}],diagrams:[{id:`two-geographies`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Same app, two internal geographies`,caption:`Both deploy as one unit — only the modular version has seams the compiler can see and enforce.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Classic["Classic Monolith"]
        direction TB
        SA["Application Project - feature folders"]
        SD["Shared Domain Project"]
        SA --> SD
        SA -. "any feature reaches any entity" .-> SD
    end
    subgraph Modular["Modular Monolith"]
        direction TB
        UM["Users Module: Domain + App + Public API"]
        BM["Bookings Module: Domain + App + Public API"]
        NM["Notifications Module: Domain + App + Public API"]
        BM -- "calls contract" --> UM
        NM -- "calls contract" --> UM
    end
    DEP["Single Deployment Unit"]
    Classic --> DEP
    Modular --> DEP`},{id:`cross-module-call`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`A legal crossing: Bookings asks Users`,caption:`The call travels through the public API contract and returns a DTO — the User entity never leaves its module.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant B as Bookings Module
    participant C as Users Public API Contract
    participant U as Users Module Internals
    participant DB as Users Data
    B->>C: GetUserProfile userId
    C->>U: invoke implementation
    U->>DB: query by id
    DB-->>U: user row
    U-->>C: UserProfileDto
    C-->>B: UserProfileDto
    Note over B,U: Bookings never sees the User entity — only the DTO`}],takeaways:[`One domain assembly per module — if two modules reference the same entity assembly, you have one module with extra folders.`,`Cross-module calls may carry identifiers and DTOs only; every entity that leaks across a seam adds roughly a sprint to a future extraction.`,`Budget 20-30% more upfront design time for a modular monolith — that's the extraction cost you're pre-paying at a discount.`,`Audit the boundary quarterly: pick a module and estimate its extraction cost; if it has grown past two sprints, the seam is rotting.`,`Don't extract until a concrete driver — scale, versioning, data isolation, or team ownership — forces it; a modular monolith is the option, not the obligation.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built the Same App as a Monolith and a Modular Monolith`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S2zqdX411RQ`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`monolith-vs-microservices`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`}];export{e as lessons};